<header><h1>RulesPal TI4 FAQ (community consensus and designer rulings): Notes</h1></header>
<article>
<p>Every note on this site that <a href="https://www.rulespal.com/twilight-imperium-4/faq" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RulesPal TI4 FAQ (community consensus and designer rulings)</a> supports, with the text it is quoted for, grouped by page. <b>Explicit</b> sources state the note directly. <b>Implicit</b> sources support it without stating it.</p>
<h2><a href="/R_abilities">Abilities</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>An ability may be triggered during the timing window it was gained, if applicable. For example, if a player receives the <a href="/F_creuss">Creuss</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Faction_Promissory_Note:~:text=Creuss%20Iff"><i>Creuss Iff</i></a> promissory note at the start of their turn, they may use it immediately.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I receive Creuss IFF in a transaction at the start of my turn and play it in that same window?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it is still the start of your turn until you actually take an action.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_ul">Ul</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Titans_of_Ul#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Awaken"><sc>Awaken</sc></a> ability is an example of an ability that checks its condition when it triggers; activating a system without a sleeper token will not trigger <sc>Awaken</sc>, even if a sleeper token is later added during the activation step of that tactical action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When can the Titans use Awaken &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Right after activating a system that already held your sleeper token; a sleeper placed later in that activation, say by Scanlink exploration, can&rsquo;t be awakened then.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Assault Cannon</i> technology is an example of an ability that checks its condition when it would resolve; a player without three or more non&ndash;fighter ships in the system cannot use <i>Assault Cannon</i>, even if they previously had more ships.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If both players have start-of-combat abilities like Assault Cannon &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;with each trigger re-checked when its turn comes. So the attacker&rsquo;s Assault Cannon can push the defender below 3 non-fighter ships and deny the defender&rsquo;s own Assault Cannon.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Once an ability has been resolved, it cannot be undone or withdrawn, even if another player then resolves an ability in response. The player may instead respond by resolving another ability when they next have the opportunity during that timing window.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Once I resolve an ability in a timing window, can I take it back after my opponent responds?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; anything already resolved stays resolved &hellip; you can answer it with another ability on your next turn in that window.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each paragraph on a card is a separate ability, and is resolved independently of the others. For example, a promissory note&rsquo;s final paragraph instructing the player to return the card is still resolved, even if an earlier paragraph had no effect.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is each paragraph on a card a separate ability &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; each paragraph is its own ability and resolves on its own. So a note&rsquo;s final return line still happens even if an earlier paragraph had no effect.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>&ldquo;At the start of your turn&rdquo; abilities are resolved before the player chooses their action or passes, and so before a system is activated. Such abilities can only be resolved during the action phase, as players do not take turns during other phases.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When do &lsquo;at the start of your turn&rsquo; abilities happen, and do they work in other phases?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Before you choose your action or pass, so they come before activating a system &hellip; They only exist in the action phase.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An ability that refers to &ldquo;a player&rdquo; may be used on the player resolving it. Only an ability that refers to &ldquo;another player&rdquo; excludes that player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do &lsquo;during the action phase&rsquo; agent abilities use my action, and does &lsquo;a player&rsquo; include me?&rdquo;: &ldquo;&rsquo;A player&rsquo; includes you; only &lsquo;another player&rsquo; excludes you.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each unit on the board is its own separate physical object. For example, two or more of the <a href="/F_norr">N&rsquo;orr</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Sardakk_N&#x27;orr#:~:text=Exotrireme"><i>Exotrireme II</i></a> in a system may use their ability to destroy themselves and other ships, as the ability is linked to the units on the game board, and not the unit upgrade technology card.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly can Exotrireme II use its ability?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Each Exotrireme II has its own copy, resolved one at a time.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During the action phase, abilities are resolved in the current relative turn order, starting with the active player. For example, if it is currently the <i>Trade</i> (5) player&rsquo;s turn, they will have the option to resolve first, then the <i>Warfare</i> (6) player, then the <i>Technology</i> (7) player, and so on.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When several players want to resolve abilities at the same time, who goes first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;In the action phase, the active player goes first, then the other players in initiative order counting on from the active player&rsquo;s number and wrapping around.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player has multiple abilities they wish to resolve, they must be resolved one at a time. Between each ability being resolved, each other player will have the opportunity to resolve one ability.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If both players have the same &lsquo;after a round of space combat&rsquo; ability, such as two Exotrireme II dreadnoughts, who resolves first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;alternating one ability at a time until nobody wants to resolve more&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Once every player has consecutively declined to resolve an ability during a timing window, no more abilities may be resolved during that window.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If I pass on playing an action card or ability in a timing window, can I still play one later in that window?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; passing is not final. Players keep taking turns in the window, and if anyone plays something after you passed, you get another chance. The window only closes once every player passes in a row.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may decline to resolve an ability during a timing window, but later choose to resolve an ability during that same timing window, but only if another player chooses to resolve an ability after the player declined.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If I pass on playing an action card or ability in a timing window, can I still play one later in that window?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; passing is not final. Players keep taking turns in the window, and if anyone plays something after you passed, you get another chance. The window only closes once every player passes in a row.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Following on from the above example, had Alice declined to resolve &ldquo;Alice &num;2&rdquo;, she could still resolve &ldquo;Alice &num;3&rdquo;, as Cheng resolved &ldquo;Cheng &num;2&rdquo;, breaking the series of consecutive declinations. However, if Alice had instead declined to resolve &ldquo;Alice &num;3&rdquo;, she would be unable to resolve &ldquo;Alice &num;4&rdquo;, as then each player would have consecutively declined to resolve an ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If I pass on playing an action card or ability in a timing window, can I still play one later in that window?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; passing is not final. Players keep taking turns in the window, and if anyone plays something after you passed, you get another chance. The window only closes once every player passes in a row.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If there are multiple abilities that players wish to resolve at the same time during the status phase, such as two players each using the Creuss player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Wormhole%20Generator"><i>Wormhole Generator</i></a> faction technology, they are resolved in initiative order.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If two players have abilities at the same point of the status phase, such as both using Wormhole Generator, who goes first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Initiative order: the player with the lower initiative number resolves first.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The secondary abilities of strategy cards are resolved in clockwise order, after the primary ability.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When a strategy card is played, do other players resolve the secondary right away &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Right away, clockwise from the active player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_action_cards">Action Cards</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Players start the game with zero action cards.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do players start the game with commodities, trade goods or action cards?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; nobody starts with commodities, trade goods or action cards.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may choose to reveal any or all of the action cards in their hand.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I show my action cards or secret objectives to other players?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; you may reveal your own hidden cards to anyone at any time, and they still count as hidden afterwards.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During the status phase, action cards are drawn in initiative order, though this rarely matters in practice.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;In what order are action cards drawn in the status phase?&rdquo;: &ldquo;In initiative order, though it rarely matters in practice.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player&rsquo;s action card is canceled, usually via a <i>Sabotage</i> action card, then if that player has a second copy of that action card, they may play it.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my action card is cancelled by Sabotage, can I play another copy &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; a cancelled card is treated as never having been played.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot use action cards that destroy units, such as <i>Lucky Shot</i>, against their own units.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;abilities that destroy units can only be used against other players&rsquo; units and planets.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an action card targets &ldquo;each&rdquo; of a particular type of object, that action card may target zero of those objects. As such, these cards may be played even if they would cause no effect.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I play an action card whose effect would amount to nothing &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; doing the card&rsquo;s text with a result of zero still counts as resolving it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player has more than seven action cards, they must discard the excess.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If I have 7 action cards and draw 2 &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Draw both, then discard down to seven.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Action cards are discarded faceup. Players may look through the action card discard pile.<sup>2,4,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is the action card discard pile face up, and can players look through it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes to both &mdash; discards are face up and public.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_active_player">Active Player</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The active player will have the first opportunity to resolve abilities during each timing window.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When must Xxcha use Nullification Field &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The active player gets first turn in that window.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>All transactions during the action phase must involve the active player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who can make transactions, and when?&rdquo;: &ldquo;During the action phase, only the active player can transact.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a non&ndash;active player produces hits using the <sc>Space Cannon Offense</sc> ability of one of their units, those hits must be assigned to the active player&rsquo;s units.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;During Space Cannon Offense, who can shoot at whom?&rdquo;: &ldquo;each other player with Space Cannon in range fires, but only at the active player&rsquo;s ships.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_active_system">Active System</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A system is only activated by placing a command token during the Activation step of a tactical action or through the primary ability of the Warfare strategy card. Command tokens placed by other effects, such as the primary ability of the <i>Diplomacy</i> strategy card or a retreat, and action cards that target a system, do not activate that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Diplomacy tokens, retreats or action cards like Ghost Ship or Plague count as activating a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a system is activated only by placing a command token as the first step of a tactical action. Tokens placed by Diplomacy or a retreat and cards aimed at a system don&rsquo;t activate it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_adjacency">Adjacency</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A unit is not adjacent to the system it is in.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do ships in Mecatol Rex&rsquo;s system, or in an anomaly, count as adjacent to it for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a system isn&rsquo;t adjacent to itself.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_agenda_card">Agenda Card</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When resolving the primary ability of the <i>Politics</i> card, the active player cannot show the agenda cards they drew to any other player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After Politics, is it public whether the agenda cards went to the top or bottom of the deck?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You also can&rsquo;t show the cards themselves to other players, since you may only reveal hidden components you own.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During the agenda phase, if the outcome of an agenda affects multiple players, it does so in speaker order, starting with the speaker and proceeding clockwise. If an agenda is resolved during the action phase, such as with the <a href="/F_keleres">Keleres</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Council_Keleres#Thunder's_Edge_2:~:text=Executive%20Order"><i>Executive Order</i></a> technology, it does so in initiative order.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;In what order are agenda-phase secret objectives scored?&rdquo;: &ldquo;In speaker order, clockwise; it only matters if several players would win from the points, a tie that also goes clockwise from the speaker.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_agenda_phase">Agenda Phase</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When a player casts votes during the agenda phase, it is not that player&rsquo;s turn for the purpose of game effects.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When do &lsquo;at the start of your turn&rsquo; abilities happen, and do they work in other phases?&rdquo;: &ldquo;They only exist in the action phase: you don&rsquo;t have turns in the strategy, status or agenda phases.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Players may exhaust planets during the agenda phase for purposes other than casting votes, such as spending resources or using technology specialties. Planets exhausted this way cannot then be used to cast votes.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I exhaust planets for resources or technology specialties during the agenda phase?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; nothing limits planets to voting then. You can spend them on things like Inheritance Systems or research from a Technology Rider, but those planets then can&rsquo;t vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Casting zero votes for an outcome is the same as abstaining. A player that casts zero votes gains no positive or negative effects of voting for that outcome.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I abstain, and does casting zero votes let me use Distinguished Councilor or Bribery?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Casting zero votes is the same as abstaining.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The speaker decides tied outcomes even if they cannot vote on that agenda, such as when they are the <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player or have played a rider, as deciding a tie is not a vote.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a speaker who can&rsquo;t vote, such as the Nekro Virus or a speaker who played a rider, still break ties?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; breaking a tie isn&rsquo;t voting, so the speaker always does it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An agenda&rsquo;s outcome affects every player, regardless of how they voted or whether they abstained, unless the agenda states otherwise, such as the <i>Mutiny</i> agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does an agenda&rsquo;s outcome affect everyone, or only the players who voted for it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Everyone, unless the card itself limits the effect to players who voted a certain way, as Mutiny does. Abstaining doesn&rsquo;t exempt you.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During the agenda phase, if the outcome of an agenda affects multiple players, it does so in speaker order, starting with the speaker and proceeding clockwise. If an agenda is resolved during the action phase, such as with the <a href="/F_keleres">Keleres</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Council_Keleres#Thunder's_Edge_2:~:text=Executive%20Order"><i>Executive Order</i></a> technology, it does so in initiative order.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;In what order are agenda-phase secret objectives scored?&rdquo;: &ldquo;In speaker order, clockwise; it only matters if several players would win from the points, a tie that also goes clockwise from the speaker.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Riders are resolved after the agenda is fully resolved, in speaker order.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an agenda outcome and a rider both award points, which resolves first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The agenda&rsquo;s outcome resolves first, then the riders that predicted it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each pair of players may resolve one transaction during each agenda.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can players make transactions with non-neighbors during the agenda phase?&rdquo;: &ldquo;while each agenda is being resolved &hellip; every pair of players may make one transaction, neighbors or not.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Planets are readied at the end of the agenda phase; other exhausted cards, such as technologies and agents, stay exhausted.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I exhaust planets for resources or technology specialties during the agenda phase?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Exhausted cards other than planets don&rsquo;t ready at the end of the agenda phase.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_anomalies">Anomalies</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>An empty system is not an anomaly.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which systems count as anomalies?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only tiles with a red border: asteroid fields, nebulae, gravity rifts and supernovas. Empty systems and wormhole systems are not anomalies unless bordered.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system containing a wormhole is not an anomaly.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which systems count as anomalies?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Empty systems and wormhole systems are not anomalies unless bordered.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system containing a <a href="/F_vuilraith">Vuil&rsquo;raith</a> <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Vuil&#x27;Raith_Cabal#:~:text=Dimensional%20Tear">Dimensional Tear</a> is an anomaly.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Vuil&rsquo;raith ships roll for Cormund&rsquo;s gravity rift &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it is simply a gravity rift, and counts as an anomaly for objectives.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_anti_fighter_barrage">Anti&ndash;Fighter Barrage (Unit Ability)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A non&ndash;fighter ship cannot use its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability to cancel a hit produced by an anti&ndash;fighter barrage roll, unless an effect such as the <i>Waylay</i> action card makes it eligible to be hit.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Raid Formation and Waylay interact?&rdquo;: &ldquo;With Waylay, those hits still hit ships and the opponent assigns them normally, using Sustain Damage on undamaged ships if they like.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Effects such as the <i>Shields Holding</i> action card, which trigger on hits produced during space combat, may be used to cancel a hit produced by an anti&ndash;fighter barrage roll.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which hits can Shields Holding cancel?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Hits during space combat, including Anti-Fighter Barrage in the first round.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The &ldquo;0&rdquo; side of the d10 represents a result of 10.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the 0 on the ten-sided die count as 0 or 10?&rdquo;: &ldquo;10 &mdash; the Living Rules Reference FAQ confirms the 0 face is a 10.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_asteroid_field">Asteroid Field</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player with the <i>Antimass Deflectors</i> technology may move their ships through asteroid fields.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which systems count as anomalies?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Antimass Deflectors is the technology that lets ships move in&rdquo; asteroid fields.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_attach">Attach</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A single planet may have multiple attachments.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does exploring a new planet happen before Integrated Economy?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Attachments can stack, so a planet may hold several.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_blockaded">Blockaded</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>While a player is involved in a space combat in a system, their units in that system are not blockaded, as both players will have ships in the system.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With Prophecy of Kings capture rules, when exactly is a space dock blockaded?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a system with another player&rsquo;s ships and none of yours &hellip; during combat both sides have ships, so a dock isn&rsquo;t blockaded until combat ends.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_bombardment">Bombardment (Unit Ability)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The <b>Bombardment</b> step happens outside of combat. No combat effects may affect a bombardment roll.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Morale Boost, faction modifiers and rerolls affect &hellip; Bombardment &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; only rolls made against a unit&rsquo;s combat value are combat rolls.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot score a secret objective that requires them to win a combat or similar by destroying units during the <b>Bombardment</b> step.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Space Cannon, Bombardment or landing on an empty planet count as winning a combat for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; &hellip; bombarding away all defenders &hellip; involves no combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A single planet must be chosen for each unit with the <sc>Bombardment</sc> ability; any hits generated by that unit will be assigned to units on that planet.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can one ship bombard several planets, or split its dice between them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a unit bombards a single planet with all its dice.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Mechs may use their <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability to cancel a hit produced by a bombardment roll.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a ship use Sustain Damage against Space Cannon hits?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; Sustain Damage can cancel any hit produced against the unit.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The &ldquo;0&rdquo; side of the d10 represents a result of 10.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the 0 on the ten-sided die count as 0 or 10?&rdquo;: &ldquo;10 &mdash; the Living Rules Reference FAQ confirms the 0 face is a 10.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_breakthroughs">Breakthroughs</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When a player gains their faction&rsquo;s breakthrough, they perform a breakthrough roll, which may cause The Fracture to enter play.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Crimson Rebellion&rsquo;s starting breakthrough trigger the Fracture roll?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the roll triggers whenever a player gains a breakthrough from any source.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a breakthrough has a passive ability and an exhaust ability, the passive ability always remains in effect, even when the breakthrough is exhausted.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are breakthroughs technologies, and do their passive abilities work while exhausted?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Their passive abilities keep working while exhausted.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A breakthrough itself is not a technology; it alone cannot be used to meet a prerequisite when researching a technology, nor can it alone be used to meet the technology requirement of an objective.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;They aren&rsquo;t technologies, so a breakthrough like Mentak&rsquo;s Corsair doesn&rsquo;t count as a unit upgrade or for anything that cares about technologies.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_rebellion">Rebellion</a> player gains their breakthrough as a starting component, which triggers a breakthrough roll during setup; The Fracture may be in play from the start of the game.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Crimson Rebellion&rsquo;s starting breakthrough trigger the Fracture roll?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; you gain starting components at setup &hellip; a player reports the developer saying the game can start with the Fracture in play.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_capacity">Capacity (Attribute)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Capacity limits are checked immediately whenever a combat is not in progress.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is capacity checked if a carrier is lost?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Immediately whenever it isn&rsquo;t combat, for example after each player&rsquo;s Space Cannon Offense volley, before the next player fires.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If, after movement, a player has more fighters and ground forces in the active system than their ships there can carry, they must remove the excess before the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is capacity checked if a carrier is lost?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Immediately whenever it isn&rsquo;t combat, for example after each player&rsquo;s Space Cannon Offense volley, before the next player fires.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a ship with capacity is destroyed during the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step, its player removes the excess units immediately, after that player&rsquo;s hits are assigned and before the next player rolls. The excess units do not remain until space combat.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is capacity checked if a carrier is lost?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Immediately whenever it isn&rsquo;t combat, for example after each player&rsquo;s Space Cannon Offense volley, before the next player fires.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A ship cannot pick up more units from a planet than it has capacity. However, a ship may pick up any units (including zero) in a system as it transports, even if doing so would cause units to be removed due to capacity limits.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a carrier pick up more units than its capacity so the excess are destroyed &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t pick up more than the moving ships&rsquo; capacity &hellip; But you can load ground forces and leave fighters or infantry already in space behind; capacity is a system total, so those left over are removed if what remains can&rsquo;t hold them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The carrier may transport the four fighters, leaving behind all eight of the infantry in the system.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a carrier pick up more units than its capacity so the excess are destroyed &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t pick up more than the moving ships&rsquo; capacity &hellip; But you can load ground forces and leave fighters or infantry already in space behind; capacity is a system total, so those left over are removed if what remains can&rsquo;t hold them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The carrier may transport four of the eight infantry, leaving behind the four remaining infantry in the system. Now that the system has zero capacity, all four of the fighters will be removed.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a carrier pick up more units than its capacity so the excess are destroyed &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t pick up more than the moving ships&rsquo; capacity &hellip; But you can load ground forces and leave fighters or infantry already in space behind; capacity is a system total, so those left over are removed if what remains can&rsquo;t hold them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The carrier may transport two of the eight infantry and two of the four fighters, leaving behind the six remaining infantry in the system. Now that the system has zero capacity, the two remaining fighters will be removed.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a carrier pick up more units than its capacity so the excess are destroyed &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t pick up more than the moving ships&rsquo; capacity &hellip; But you can load ground forces and leave fighters or infantry already in space behind; capacity is a system total, so those left over are removed if what remains can&rsquo;t hold them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The carrier may transport nothing, leaving behind all eight of the infantry in the system. Now that the system has zero capacity, all four of the fighters will be removed.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a carrier pick up more units than its capacity so the excess are destroyed &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t pick up more than the moving ships&rsquo; capacity &hellip; But you can load ground forces and leave fighters or infantry already in space behind; capacity is a system total, so those left over are removed if what remains can&rsquo;t hold them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The carrier cannot attempt to pick up all eight infantry, even if it immediately removes four of them (and the fighters).<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a carrier pick up more units than its capacity so the excess are destroyed, or leave fighters behind to die?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t pick up more than the moving ships&rsquo; capacity, so you can&rsquo;t overload to destroy your own units.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Units that are removed due to capacity limits are not destroyed. For example, removed Infantry II units do not roll for the Infantry II unit upgrade&rsquo;s ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After ships stop moving, are fighters and ground forces still tied to the ship that carried them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Units removed for lack of capacity are removed, not destroyed, so Infantry II doesn&rsquo;t trigger.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_capture">Capture</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Units are returned as soon as a space dock is blockaded.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If I win the space combat and then invade the planet with an enemy dock in the same action &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the dock is blockaded from the end of space combat until it is destroyed, so the units return.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_coexistence">Coexistence</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If a game effect instructs a player to coexist on a planet they do control, then another player will gain control of that planet (which will exhaust that planet).<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does coexistence work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;if they held it, they lose control&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>All coexisting units are blockaded, regardless of what ships, if any, are in the system. This includes units that are not structures, such as mechs with the <sc>Production</sc> ability.<sup>2,6,7,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does coexistence work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Coexisting structures still work but count as blockaded&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player who is coexisting on a planet may start a ground combat against the player that controls that planet during the invasion step of a tactical action.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a coexisting player end coexistence and attack?&rdquo;: &ldquo;activate the system and commit the coexisting units against the planet; win the ground combat and you take control as normal&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When scoring an objective, a coexisting player considers both themselves and the controlling player to control that planet. The player that controls the planet does not count a coexisting player as also controlling it.<sup>2,6,7,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does coexistence work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;they count as controlling it for objectives and neighbors&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_combat">Combat (Attribute)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player only wins a combat if a space combat or ground combat occurred. Destroying all opposing ships with the Space Cannon ability, destroying all opposing ground forces with the Bombardment ability, landing ground forces on a planet without opposing ground forces, or the opposing player avoiding the combat with the <a href="/F_naalu">Naalu</a> player&rsquo;s Foresight faction ability does not count as winning a combat. A planet containing only another player&rsquo;s structures does not cause a ground combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Space Cannon, Bombardment or landing on an empty planet count as winning a combat for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a combat is a space combat or a ground combat &hellip; Destroying ships with Space Cannon during movement, bombarding away all defenders, or landing unopposed involves no combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The &ldquo;0&rdquo; side of the d10 represents a result of 10.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the 0 on the ten-sided die count as 0 or 10?&rdquo;: &ldquo;10 &mdash; the Living Rules Reference FAQ confirms the 0 face is a 10.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_command_sheet">Command Sheet</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When a command token is gained, it may be placed in any of the three pools if no specific pool is specified by the gaining effect.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;In the status phase, do I get back all the command tokens I used on the board?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Newly gained tokens may go in any pool.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_command_tokens">Command Tokens</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Command tokens on a player&rsquo;s command sheet are not in that player&rsquo;s reinforcements.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens if I would gain a command token but have none left in my reinforcements?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Tokens you spend &hellip; go back to your reinforcements, not to your command sheet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an effect instructs a player to spend a command token from their reinforcements, that player must have a command token in their reinforcements to do so. The token remains in their reinforcements, unless the effect places it on the game board.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does spending a command token &lsquo;from your reinforcements&rsquo; mean?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You need a token in reinforcements to pay; it isn&rsquo;t purged or moved to your sheet, so it normally just stays in reinforcements, or goes on the board if the effect says so.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system is only activated by placing a command token during the Activation step of a tactical action or through the primary ability of the Warfare strategy card. Command tokens placed by other effects, such as the primary ability of the <i>Diplomacy</i> strategy card or a retreat, and action cards that target a system, do not activate that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Diplomacy tokens, retreats or action cards like Ghost Ship or Plague count as activating a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a system is activated only by placing a command token as the first step of a tactical action. Tokens placed by Diplomacy or a retreat and cards aimed at a system don&rsquo;t activate it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_commodities">Commodities</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player starts the game with zero commodities.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do players start the game with commodities, trade goods or action cards?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; nobody starts with commodities, trade goods or action cards.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player has their maximum number of commodities, and is instructed to replenish their commodities, they will take no commodities. However, they will still trigger any &ldquo;when [this] player replenishes commodities&rdquo; effects, most notably the <i>Trade Agreement</i> promissory note.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Minister of Commerce, Industry, Peace and War interact &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Minister of Commerce still pays 1 trade good per neighbor when your commodities were already full, since you still replenished.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_component_action">Component Action</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>There is no limit to how many component actions a player may perform during a round, other than any limit stated on the component itself. Each component action uses that player&rsquo;s action for the turn.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are component actions limited to once per round?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; each one simply uses your action for the turn. The only limits are those printed on the component itself.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_component_limitations">Component Limitations</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>As each player only has ten plastic fighter figures and twelve plastic infantry figures, a player cannot have fighters in more than ten systems, and cannot have infantry on more than twelve planets or being carried in more than twelve space areas, or any combination thereof.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are fighter and infantry tokens limited, and what are they for?&rdquo;: &ldquo;each stack of tokens must sit with at least one plastic piece of the same type. So your plastic fighters and infantry limit how many places you can have them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Players may also swap infantry and fighter plastic pieces for tokens at any time.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;You may swap a plastic piece on the board for a token at any time to free the plastic for use elsewhere.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_construction">Construction (Strategy Card)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>After the active player resolves the primary ability of the <i>Construction</i> strategy card, each other player, beginning with the player to the left of the active player and proceeding clockwise, may spend one command token from their strategy pool to place one structure on a planet they control. The command token is not placed in any system.<sup>2,13</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Construction card differ?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Its secondary spends a strategy token to place a structure on a planet you control, but the token is no longer placed in that system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may have a maximum of one space dock on each planet.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I build space docks or PDS with Production?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A planet holds at most one space dock and two PDS, Mecatol Rex included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may have a maximum of two PDS units on each planet.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I build space docks or PDS with Production?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A planet holds at most one space dock and two PDS, Mecatol Rex included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Placing structures with the Construction strategy card is not producing units; the structures have no cost, and production rules do not apply.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do I pay resources for the structures placed with Construction?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; space docks and PDS have no cost. Construction places them; it isn&rsquo;t production, so production rules don&rsquo;t apply.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Resolving the secondary ability of the <i>Construction</i> strategy card does not activate a system, and it does not matter whether the system has already been activated.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Construction card differ?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It doesn&rsquo;t activate the system or care whether it was activated.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_control">Control</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Different players may control different planets in the same system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can different players control different planets in the same system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; each planet is controlled separately and nobody controls the space area.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player is given another player&rsquo;s planet card, such as with the <a href="/F_hacan">Hacan</a> player&rsquo;s mech, <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Emirates_of_Hacan#Mech:~:text=Pride%20of%20Kenara">Pride of Kenara</a>, they gain control of that planet.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When a planet card is given to another player, by an exploration effect or the Hacan mech, does control go with it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; control and holding the planet card go together, so the receiver controls the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each player controls every planet in their home system from the start of the game, even if that planet contains none of their units. As this control is not gained, it does not trigger abilities that resolve when a player gains control of a planet, such as the <a href="/F_saar">Saar</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Clan_of_Saar#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Scavenge"><sc>Scavenge</sc></a> faction ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do I control both planets in my home system at the start, and does that trigger &lsquo;gain control&rsquo; effects?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You control every home planet from the start, empty or not; a control token on an empty one is just a reminder. Because you begin with control rather than gaining it, abilities like Saar&rsquo;s Scavenge don&rsquo;t trigger at setup.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_cost">Cost (Attribute)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Whenever a unit is produced, its cost must be paid, unless the effect that is producing the unit explicitly says otherwise.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do units &lsquo;placed&rsquo; by an effect cost resources, and do they count as producing?&rdquo;: &ldquo;An effect that says &lsquo;produce&rsquo; means you pay the cost unless it says otherwise.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Units that are placed or gained by an effect, rather than produced, are free and do not count as being produced; for example, ships placed with the <a href="/F_muaat">Muaat</a> player&rsquo;s <sc>Star Forge</sc> ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do units &lsquo;placed&rsquo; by an effect cost resources, and do they count as producing?&rdquo;: &ldquo;&rsquo;Place&rsquo; or &lsquo;gain&rsquo; is free and isn&rsquo;t producing, so Muaat&rsquo;s Star Forge doesn&rsquo;t count as producing and doesn&rsquo;t trigger Magmus Reactor&rsquo;s trade good.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_deals">Deals</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>In general, only the terms of the deal that a player can immediately perform, even without the deal taking place, are binding.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is a deal binding?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only when its terms resolve immediately &hellip; Promises about future actions, and if-then conditions, are non-binding and can be broken.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Making a deal to not make use of a unit&rsquo;s <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability during a tactical action, before ships have been moved.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is a deal binding?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only when its terms resolve immediately &hellip; Promises about future actions, and if-then conditions, are non-binding and can be broken.&rdquo; &ldquo;taking a trade good not to fire Space Cannon at the moment you decide whether to fire binds you.&rdquo; A deal made earlier does not.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Making a deal for a player to vote on an agenda in a certain way, when it is not yet that player&rsquo;s opportunity to vote.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is a deal to buy someone&rsquo;s votes binding?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only for a player about to cast votes right now &hellip; a promise to vote a certain way later can still be broken.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any deal that will resolve after dice are rolled.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can two players agree not to fight &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;rolling and assigning hits are mandatory and a deal can&rsquo;t override the rules.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The following are examples of binding deals:<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is a deal binding?&rdquo;: &ldquo;taking a trade good not to fire Space Cannon at the moment you decide whether to fire binds you not to fire.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Making a deal to not make use of a unit&rsquo;s <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability, at the moment that player decides whether to use it.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is a deal binding?&rdquo;: &ldquo;taking a trade good not to fire Space Cannon at the moment you decide whether to fire binds you not to fire.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Making a deal for a player to vote on an agenda in a certain way, when that player is about to cast their votes.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is a deal to buy someone&rsquo;s votes binding?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only for a player about to cast votes right now. Paying the next voter in exchange for their vote binds it, since nothing can happen in between&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_destroyed">Destroyed</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A ship removed by a gravity rift is not destroyed.<sup>2,10</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When a carrier leaves a gravity rift &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Units lost this way are removed rather than destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Units that are removed due to capacity limits are not destroyed. For example, removed Infantry II units do not roll for the Infantry II unit upgrade&rsquo;s ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After ships stop moving, are fighters and ground forces still tied to the ship that carried them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Units removed for lack of capacity are removed, not destroyed, so Infantry II doesn&rsquo;t trigger.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Units destroyed by space cannon or bombardment rolls are not destroyed during combat. Units destroyed by anti&ndash;fighter barrage rolls are destroyed during combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Vuil&rsquo;raith Devour ability capture units destroyed by Space Cannon or Bombardment?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; those happen outside space and ground combat, so Devour doesn&rsquo;t see them; Anti-Fighter Barrage is part of combat and counts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_diplomacy">Diplomacy (Strategy Card)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If the player with <i>Diplomacy</i> controls fewer than two exhausted planets, they may still perform a strategic action. They will ready as many planets as possible.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the revised Diplomacy strategy card work?&rdquo;: the primary player may &ldquo;ready up to 2 exhausted planets they control.&rdquo; &ldquo;Up to&rdquo; covers fewer.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Game effects that can remove a command token from a system may still be used to remove a command token from the system containing the Diplomacy token. Should this happen, players may first wish to replace the Diplomacy token with the corresponding command tokens, then remove the required command token, leaving the rest in the system.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Warfare, Counterstroke or other effects remove a token placed by Diplomacy?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; once placed it is an ordinary command token &hellip; Warfare, Counterstroke, Unexpected Action and similar effects can remove it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_elimination">Elimination</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A space station is not a planet; controlling one does not prevent a player from being eliminated.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;A space station isn&rsquo;t a planet, so controlling one doesn&rsquo;t stop you being eliminated.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_entropic_scars">Entropic Scars</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A unit in an entropic scar cannot use unit abilities.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Arborec commander work with a Letani in an Entropic Scar?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the scar stops unit abilities being used but doesn&rsquo;t remove them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A unit in an entropic scar still has its unit abilities, even though it cannot use them. As such, a text ability that only requires a unit with a unit ability may still be used, such as the <a href="/F_arborec">Arborec</a> player&rsquo;s Dirzuga Rophal commander with a unit that has <sc>Production</sc>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Arborec commander work with a Letani in an Entropic Scar?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; the scar stops unit abilities being used but doesn&rsquo;t remove them, and the commander is a text ability that only needs a unit with Production.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player need not meet the prerequisites for the technology they gain this way.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If an effect lets me research a technology, do I pay for it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;&rsquo;Gain&rsquo; is different from &lsquo;research&rsquo;: gaining ignores prerequisites.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_exhausted">Exhausted</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Planets will also ready at the end of each agenda phase.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does the agenda phase happen, and when are planets readied?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Planets ready again at the end of the agenda phase for the next action phase.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_expedition">Expeditions</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The player who claimed the sixth and final slice flips the expedition token to the Thunder&rsquo;s Edge side, and places it in a system on the game board. They must choose a system that does not contain a planet, a supernova, or a printed wormhole, and is not in The Fracture, should it be in play.<sup>2,6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Thunder&rsquo;s Edge expedition finish, and who gets the planet?&rdquo;: &ldquo;that player must flip Thunder&rsquo;s Edge and place it in a system with no planet &hellip; supernova or printed wormhole.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Thunder&rsquo;s Edge cannot be placed in a system that contains a planet token, such as Mirage, as that system contains a planet.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Thunder&rsquo;s Edge expedition finish, and who gets the planet?&rdquo;: &ldquo;place it in a system with no planet (token planets like Mirage count), supernova or printed wormhole.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Finally, the player who had claimed the most slices of the expedition token places a number of infantry equal to the number of slices they had claimed on to Thunder&rsquo;s Edge. If multiple players are tied for the most number of slices, the player placing Thunder&rsquo;s Edge chooses one of those players to place the infantry; they may choose themself.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;on a tie, the player who claimed the last slice chooses among the tied players.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_exploration">Exploration</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When a player gains control of a planet that is not controlled by another player, they explore it before resolving any &ldquo;after you gain control of a planet&rdquo; abilities, such as the <i>Integrated Economy</i> technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does exploration work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;When you gain control of a planet nobody controlled, draw a card from the exploration deck matching its trait; this happens before &lsquo;after you gain control&rsquo; effects like Integrated Economy or Pre-Fab Arcologies.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Before resolving an exploration card, it is revealed to all players.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: transactions may occur &ldquo;after an exploration card is revealed but before it resolves.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may perform transactions after revealing an exploration card and before resolving it.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can abilities be used after an exploration card is revealed but before it resolves?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only transactions, per a FAQ entry players quote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_fighter_tokens">Fighter Tokens</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>As each player only has ten plastic fighter figures, a player cannot have fighters in more than ten systems.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are fighter and infantry tokens limited, and what are they for?&rdquo;: &ldquo;each stack of tokens must sit with at least one plastic piece of the same type. So your plastic fighters and infantry limit how many places you can have them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Fighter tokens are not component limited.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are fighter and infantry tokens limited &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Tokens are unlimited.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_fleet_pool">Fleet Pool</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Fighters and ground forces do not count toward capacity during combat. When combat ends however, units will need to be removed to meet capacity requirements.<sup>1,2,3,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my carrier is destroyed in combat &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;capacity isn&rsquo;t checked during combat, so they stay while it lasts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships may be moved into or produced in a system even if doing so would exceed the fleet pool limit. However, some of those ships must immediately be removed to satisfy the limit.<sup>2,5,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I deliberately go over my fleet pool by moving or producing, then remove ships of my choice?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; nothing stops you; you just remove the excess right away, choosing which.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If some of those ships are fighters II, they may instead count toward that player&rsquo;s capacity in that system. If capacity is exceeded, fighters or ground forces must be removed to meet the limit.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can fighters stay in a system without a carrier or space dock?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Fighter IIs that don&rsquo;t fit in capacity count against your fleet pool like other ships.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During movement, a ship may move through a system regardless of how many ships are in that system relative to the fleet pool limit.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is the fleet pool limit checked &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;At all times, except for ships that are only moving through other systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_fracture">The Fracture</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The &ldquo;0&rdquo; side of the d10 represents a result of 10.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the 0 on the ten-sided die count as 0 or 10?&rdquo;: &ldquo;10.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The placement location of The Fracture is arbitrary, and should have no mechanical effect on the game state.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is The Fracture 3 systems or 7 &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It can sit anywhere, and the galaxy tiles it touches stay edge systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>All systems in The Fracture are considered to be on the edge of the game board.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;every Fracture system also counts as on the edge.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Frontier tokens are not placed in systems in The Fracture when it is brought into play, as frontier tokens are only placed during setup. However, if The Fracture is in play when the <a href="/F_empyrean">Empyrean</a> player resolves their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Empyrean#Leaders:~:text=Multiverse%20Shift"><i>Multiverse Shift</i></a> hero ability, frontier tokens will be placed in the systems in The Fracture that do not contain a planet.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do empty Fracture systems get frontier tokens, and can wormholes go there?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Not at the start: frontier tokens are placed at setup, before the Fracture is in play, though the Empyrean hero can later put them on its empty systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When The Fracture is brought into play, several ingress tokens will be placed on the game board. Several systems in The Fracture contain a printed egress. Each ingress is adjacent to each egress, and vice versa. An ingress is not adjacent to an ingress and an egress is not adjacent to an egress.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can ships move from an egress back to an ingress to leave The Fracture?&rdquo;: &ldquo;ingress and egress adjacency works both ways, like wormholes, except ingresses aren&rsquo;t adjacent to other ingresses and egresses aren&rsquo;t adjacent to other egresses&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>With sufficient movement, a ship could move from a system containing an ingress in the regular game map, into a system containing an egress within The Fracture, and then out to a different system containing an ingress in the regular game map. A ship could also do the reverse to travel from an egress to a different egress via an ingress.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can ships pass through the Fracture in one move &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes, they can go in through one ingress and out via an egress to another ingress system if their move allows.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a different game effect places The Fracture into play, or The Fracture enters play because of the <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player&rsquo;s breakthrough (which does not have a synergy), then the player that caused The Fracture to enter play chooses one planet with a technology specialty for each of the four colors of technology, and places ingress tokens in each of those planets&rsquo; systems.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who places ingress tokens when Rapid Mobilization brings the Fracture in?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the triggering player places ingress tokens in four different systems, each with a planet of a different specialty if able&rdquo; About Rapid Mobilization; Nekro not named.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_frontier_tokens">Frontier Tokens</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Frontier tokens are not placed in systems in The Fracture when it is brought into play, as frontier tokens are only placed during setup. However, if The Fracture is in play when the <a href="/F_empyrean">Empyrean</a> player resolves their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Empyrean#Leaders:~:text=Multiverse%20Shift"><i>Multiverse Shift</i></a> hero ability, frontier tokens will be placed in the systems in The Fracture that do not contain a planet.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do empty Fracture systems get frontier tokens, and can wormholes go there?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Not at the start: frontier tokens are placed at setup, before the Fracture is in play, though the Empyrean hero can later put them on its empty systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_game_board">Game Board</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If the edge of a system tile is touching a hyperlane tile, that edge does not cause that system tile to be on the edge of the game board.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With hyperlanes, which systems are on the edge of the board?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A system is on the edge only if one of its sides touches neither another system nor a hyperlane tile.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When using the five&ndash; and seven&ndash;player hyperlane setups, the system tile in the center of the ring of hyperlanes is not on the edge of the game board.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;tiles beside hyperlanes, and a system enclosed by them, aren&rsquo;t edge systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_game_round">Game Round</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>During the strategy, status and agenda phases, players do not take turns for the purpose of game effects.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When do &lsquo;at the start of your turn&rsquo; abilities happen &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you don&rsquo;t have turns in the strategy, status or agenda phases.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A game has a maximum of nine game rounds.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does the game end if nobody reaches 10 victory points?&rdquo;: &ldquo;When the speaker has to reveal a public objective in the status phase and none remain &hellip; With five stage I and five stage II objectives, that is round 9 at the latest.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_gravity_rift">Gravity Rift</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Moving into a gravity rift, usually when it is the active system, will not provide the +1, nor will the ships have to roll for removal.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do gravity rifts work: when do I get +1 move and when do I roll?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A ship that stops in a rift gains nothing and doesn&rsquo;t roll until it later leaves.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a ship moves through or out of multiple gravity rifts, each gravity rift provides +1 to its move value and requires a separate roll for removal. If it moves out of the same gravity rift more than once, that gravity rift provides +1 only once, but each instance requires a separate roll.<sup>1,2,6,7,8</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do gravity rifts work: when do I get +1 move and when do I roll?&rdquo;: &ldquo;gets +1 move for that path, one per rift &hellip; Each ship that leaves or passes through a rift rolls.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A ship removed by a gravity rift is not destroyed.<sup>2,10</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When a carrier leaves a gravity rift &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Units lost this way are removed rather than destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>All ship movement must be declared before any of them roll for gravity rift removal. A player may not choose additional ships to move after seeing the result of this roll.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do ships move one at a time, and can I wait to see gravity rift rolls before moving others?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; you declare every moving ship and they move together; rift rolls come after.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each ship rolls for gravity rift removal as it exits the gravity rift, during movement. The fleet pool limit in the active system is checked only after all moving ships arrive, so ships removed by a gravity rift do not count against it.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do ships move one at a time, and can I wait to see gravity rift rolls before moving others?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Fleet pool isn&rsquo;t checked in systems you pass through, only at the end, and you remove any excess then.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The retreating player must declare which, if any, fighters and ground forces they are moving, and which ships will be transporting them. If they have the Fighter II technology, then for each fighter, they must choose if that fighter is moving itself or being transported.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When a carrier leaves a gravity rift, do the fighters and ground forces it carries roll too?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Declare which ship carries what before rolling.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each non&ndash;fighter ship and each Fighter II moving itself will have to roll for removal. Transported units are also removed if the ship transporting them is removed.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When a carrier leaves a gravity rift, do the fighters and ground forces it carries roll too?&rdquo;: &ldquo;only the ships moving out roll &hellip; if a carrier is lost, everything it carries goes with it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The +1 will have no effect, as retreats are restricted to adjacent systems.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where can my ships retreat to?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Movement bonuses don&rsquo;t matter, as a retreat always goes to an adjacent system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system containing a <a href="/F_vuilraith">Vuil&rsquo;raith</a> <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Vuil&#x27;Raith_Cabal#:~:text=Dimensional%20Tear">Dimensional Tear</a> is an anomaly.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Vuil&rsquo;raith ships roll for Cormund&rsquo;s gravity rift &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it is simply a gravity rift, and counts as an anomaly for objectives.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_ground_combat">Ground Combat</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The &ldquo;start of combat&rdquo; and &ldquo;start of combat round&rdquo; windows occur before the <b>Roll Dice</b> step.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are &lsquo;start of combat&rsquo; and &lsquo;start of the first combat round&rsquo; abilities in the same timing window, and how often do they happen?&rdquo;: &ldquo;they are the same window, before Anti-Fighter Barrage&rdquo; start-of-combat windows precede combat rolling.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may score up to one secret objective per ground combat.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How and when are action-phase secret objectives scored?&rdquo;: &ldquo;one in a space combat and another in the ground combat that follows is fine.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player only wins a combat if a space combat or ground combat occurred. Destroying all opposing ships with the Space Cannon ability, destroying all opposing ground forces with the Bombardment ability, landing ground forces on a planet without opposing ground forces, or the opposing player avoiding the combat with the <a href="/F_naalu">Naalu</a> player&rsquo;s Foresight faction ability does not count as winning a combat. A planet containing only another player&rsquo;s structures does not cause a ground combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Space Cannon, Bombardment or landing on an empty planet count as winning a combat for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a combat is a space combat or a ground combat &hellip; Destroying ships with Space Cannon during movement, bombarding away all defenders, or landing unopposed involves no combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The &ldquo;0&rdquo; side of the d10 represents a result of 10.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the 0 on the ten-sided die count as 0 or 10?&rdquo;: &ldquo;10 &mdash; the Living Rules Reference FAQ confirms the 0 face is a 10.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_hyperlanes">Hyperlanes</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If the edge of a system tile is touching a hyperlane tile, that edge does not cause that system tile to be on the edge of the game board.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With hyperlanes, which systems are on the edge of the board?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A system is on the edge only if one of its sides touches neither another system nor a hyperlane tile.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When using the five&ndash; and seven&ndash;player hyperlane setups, the system tile in the center of the ring of hyperlanes is not on the edge of the game board.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;tiles beside hyperlanes, and a system enclosed by them, aren&rsquo;t edge systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_imperial">Imperial (Strategy Card)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player must control every planet in their home system to score a public objective using the first primary ability of <i>Imperial</i>.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I score objectives if another player controls a planet in my home system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Not public objectives &mdash; you must control every planet in your home system to score one, and the Imperial card doesn&rsquo;t override that.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player who controls Mecatol Rex does not need to control every planet in their home system to gain a victory point from the second primary ability of <i>Imperial</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I score objectives if another player controls a planet in my home system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Everything else still works: &hellip; Imperial&rsquo;s Mecatol Rex point.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player who controls Mecatol Rex while resolving the primary ability of <i>Imperial</i> must gain a victory point; they cannot instead draw a secret objective.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What do the Imperial abilities give &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;gives 1 victory point if you control Mecatol Rex &hellip; otherwise you draw a secret objective.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Scoring a public objective with the primary ability of Imperial does not prevent a player from scoring another public objective during the status phase of the same round. A status phase public objective may be scored immediately with Imperial.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I score a public objective with Imperial and another in the same round&rsquo;s status phase?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; they are separate scoring windows. Imperial scores a public objective immediately even if it says status phase.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_infantry_tokens">Infantry Tokens</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>As each player only has twelve plastic infantry figures, a player cannot have infantry on more than twelve planets or being transported in more than twelve space areas, or any combination thereof.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are fighter and infantry tokens limited, and what are they for?&rdquo;: &ldquo;each stack of tokens must sit with at least one plastic piece of the same type. So your plastic fighters and infantry limit how many places you can have them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Infantry tokens are not component limited.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is there a limit to how many infantry can be on one planet?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; any number of infantry can be on a planet. You are only limited by your components, and tokens can stand in for extra infantry.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may remove a plastic infantry figure from a planet or space area, along with any accompanying infantry tokens, if doing so allows them to swap it into the active system and allows them to commit ground forces to and fight ground combats on multiple planets during the <b>Invasion</b> step of the tactical action in that system when they could otherwise not do so.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are fighter and infantry tokens limited, and what are they for?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You may swap a plastic piece on the board for a token at any time to free the plastic for use elsewhere.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_initiative_order">Initiative Order</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If there are multiple abilities that players wish to resolve at the same time during the action phase, initiative order will determine the order they are resolved in. See the <a href="/R_abilities">abilities page</a> for more details.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When several players want to resolve abilities at the same time, who goes first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;In the action phase, the active player goes first, then the other players in initiative order.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_invasion">Invasion</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If a player gains control of an uncontrolled planet during the <b>Establish Control</b> step, they explore it.<sup>1,2,8</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does exploration work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;When you gain control of a planet nobody controlled, draw a card from the exploration deck matching its trait&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If Mecatol Rex has the custodians token on it, a player must remove it before they may commit ground forces to it.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do I take Mecatol Rex &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;during invasion, before committing ground forces, spend 6 influence to remove the custodians token.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Unless an ability states otherwise, ground forces can only land on a planet by being committed during the Commit Ground Forces step, even if the player already controls that planet. Abilities that move ships as part of a component action do not allow ground forces to land on planets.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I land ground forces on a planet, even one I control, outside the invasion step?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; ground forces land on planets only by being committed in the invasion step of a tactical action &hellip; Movement abilities used as component actions skip the tactical action steps and can&rsquo;t land anyone.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_leaders">Leaders</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A commander&rsquo;s ability cannot be applied to the instance of the effect that unlocked it.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Nekro commander draw an action card for the very technology that unlocks it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; unlock conditions are checked only after the current effect fully resolves.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A commander may be unlocked between two abilities that occur in the same timing window.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: unlock conditions are checked &ldquo;after the current effect fully resolves,&rdquo; so a later ability in the window can use it.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Using an agent&rsquo;s ability on behalf of another player is not a transaction, and does not require the players to be neighbors.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is using my agent for another player a transaction, and must we be neighbors?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No to both &mdash; it is just an ability.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_leadership">Leadership (Strategy Card)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The secondary ability of <i>Leadership</i> does not require a player to spend a strategy token.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a strategy card&rsquo;s primary ability cost a command token, and does the Leadership secondary?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No to both.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_legendary_planets">Legendary Planets</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Legendary planet ability cards exhaust and ready independently from their respective planet card.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is a legendary planet&rsquo;s ability card exhausted when I take the planet?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It works independently of the planet: you can use it after spending the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An ability that readies a planet cannot be used to ready a legendary planet ability card.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is a legendary planet&rsquo;s ability card exhausted when I take the planet?&rdquo;: &ldquo;effects that ready the planet don&rsquo;t ready it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_mechs">Mechs</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Effects that refer to &ldquo;ground forces&rdquo; apply to mechs. Effects that refer to &ldquo;infantry&rdquo; do not.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are mechs ground forces?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes, for every purpose. They aren&rsquo;t infantry, so infantry-only rules &hellip; don&rsquo;t apply to them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_modifiers">Modifiers</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When an effect, such as retreating, moves a unit to an adjacent system, modifying that unit&rsquo;s move value will have no effect.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where can my ships retreat to?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Movement bonuses don&rsquo;t matter, as a retreat always goes to an adjacent system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_move">Move (Attribute)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When an effect, such as retreating, moves a unit to an adjacent system, modifying that unit&rsquo;s move value will have no effect.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where can my ships retreat to?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Movement bonuses don&rsquo;t matter, as a retreat always goes to an adjacent system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_movement">Movement</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When an effect, such as retreating, moves a unit to an adjacent system, modifying that unit&rsquo;s move value will have no effect.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where can my ships retreat to?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Movement bonuses don&rsquo;t matter, as a retreat always goes to an adjacent system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When an ability moves a player&rsquo;s ships outside of a tactical action, those ships may transport units out of systems that contain that player&rsquo;s command tokens; this restriction only applies during tactical actions.<sup>1,2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an ability moves units outside a tactical action, like the Argent or L1Z1X hero, do normal movement rules apply?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the command-token restriction and other tactical-action movement rules are ignored.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships only move &ldquo;into&rdquo; the destination system. A ship may move &ldquo;through&rdquo; other systems.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I move more ships into a system that already has my command token on a later turn?&rdquo;: &ldquo;ships only move into the active system&rdquo; ships move into the destination; other systems are only passed through.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Fighters alone will block other players&rsquo; ships from moving through that system, whether they are accompanied by a Space Dock or they are Fighters II.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What blocks my ships from moving through a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Any other player&rsquo;s ships in it, fighters included. The Living Rules Reference dropped the old exception for fighters, so even Fighter I next to a space dock blocks.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Another player&rsquo;s ground forces and PDS do not prevent a ship from moving through a system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What blocks my ships from moving through a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;neither do enemy ground forces or PDS.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A deal cannot allow a player&rsquo;s ships to move through a system that contains another player&rsquo;s ships.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What blocks my ships from moving through a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A deal can&rsquo;t let you through.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When an ability moves ships outside of a tactical action, the movement restrictions of anomalies still apply, unless the ability says otherwise.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an ability moves units outside a tactical action, like the Argent or L1Z1X hero, do normal movement rules apply?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Anomaly rules still apply, so no ability moves ships into a supernova unless it says so.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_nebula">Nebula</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Movement bonuses, such as those from the <i>Gravity Drive</i> technology or the <i>Flank Speed</i> action card, are added to the move value of 1.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do nebulae work for movement, and does Gravity Drive help?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A ship leaving a nebula has move 1, and bonuses like Gravity Drive or Flank Speed are then added on top.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_neighbors">Neighbors</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player may make a deal with any player, however, these deals may only include a transaction if the two players are neighbors.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who can make transactions, and when?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Deals that don&rsquo;t exchange components can be made with anyone at any time.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Some effects allow players who are not neighbors to resolve transactions, such as controlling space stations, the agenda phase, the <a href="/F_hacan">Hacan</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Emirates_of_Hacan#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Guild%20Ships"><sc>Guild Ships</sc></a> faction ability, the <a href="/F_nomad">Nomad</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Nomad#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Temporal%20Command%20Suite"><i>Temporal Command Suite</i></a> technology and the <a href="/F_yssaril">Yssaril</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Yssaril_Tribes#Breakthrough:~:text=Deepgloom%20Executable"><i>Deepgloom Executable</i></a> breakthrough.<sup>1,2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can players make transactions with non-neighbors during the agenda phase?&rdquo;: &ldquo;every pair of players may make one transaction, neighbors or not. That doesn&rsquo;t make them neighbors for anything else.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may resolve one transaction with each other player per each agenda during the agenda phase, however, the agenda phase does not make all players neighbors.<sup>1,2,3,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can players make transactions with non-neighbors during the agenda phase?&rdquo;: &ldquo;every pair of players may make one transaction, neighbors or not. That doesn&rsquo;t make them neighbors for anything else.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships that move through a system do not make their owner neighbors with players who have units in or adjacent to that system, as those ships are never in that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who counts as my neighbor?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Ships moving through systems don&rsquo;t create neighbors on the way, since they are never in those systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Players in a space combat or ground combat with each other are neighbors, as they both have units in the same system.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who counts as my neighbor?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Players with a unit or controlled planet in the same system or adjacent systems, wormholes included, so two players fighting in a system are neighbors.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_objective_cards">Objective Cards</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A status phase objective must have its condition met during the status phase.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I mark an objective as soon as I meet it and score it later &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You must qualify at the moment you score. Status-phase objectives check the condition then.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any number of players may score a given public objective during the status phase.<sup>2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do revealed public objectives stay in play, and can several players score the same one?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Any number of players can score each, but each player scores a given objective only once.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may score an action phase secret objective when they are not the active player.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How and when are action-phase secret objectives scored?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Secret objectives marked Action Phase are scored the moment you meet them, even on another player&rsquo;s turn and even after you have passed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If two players could each score an action phase objective during the same combat, the player whose condition is met first scores first; for example, a flagship or war sun is destroyed during a combat round, before the combat ends and has a winner. If both conditions are met at the same moment, the active player scores first.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If two players could each score an action-phase objective in the same combat, who scores first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Whichever condition is met first. Destroying a flagship or war sun happens during a combat round, before the combat ends and has a winner.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <b>Bombardment</b> and <b>Space Cannon</b> steps happen outside of combat.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which kills trigger Nekro&rsquo;s Technological Singularity?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Kills from Space Cannon, Bombardment &hellip; happen outside combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <b>Anti&ndash;Fighter Barrage</b> step, however, is a step during combat.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;Anti-Fighter Barrage is part of combat and counts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may choose to reveal any or all of their unscored secret objectives to other players.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I show my action cards or secret objectives to other players?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; you may reveal your own hidden cards to anyone at any time, and they still count as hidden afterwards.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_pds">PDS</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A PDS is both a structure and a unit.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Codex (Omega) Magen Defense Grid still work if the PDS &hellip; has been hit by Disable?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it is still a structure.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each PDS also has the <sc>Planetary Shield</sc> ability.<sup>2,3,8</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Codex (Omega) Magen Defense Grid still work if the PDS &hellip; has been hit by Disable?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Disable only strips the PDS&rsquo;s Planetary Shield and Space Cannon.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_planets">Planets</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Mecatol Rex and planets in home systems do not have traits.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Titans put more than one sleeper on a planet with Terragenesis, or one on Mecatol Rex?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Mecatol Rex and home planets have no trait and can&rsquo;t be explored.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Planets with traits can be explored.<sup>2,8</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: planets without a trait &ldquo;can&rsquo;t be explored.&rdquo; Planets with traits can.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A planet cannot be spent for both its influence and resources during a single exhaustion.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a planet&rsquo;s resources and influence both be spent, or spent twice?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; exhausting a planet spends either its resources or its influence, never both.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a planet is exhausted for its technology specialty, it does not provide influence or resources.<sup>2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens when an exploration card adds a technology specialty &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;An attached specialty counts as the planet&rsquo;s own and is used by exhausting the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_planetary_shield">Planetary Shield (Unit Ability)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If the <sc>Planetary Shield</sc> ability prevents the use of a <sc>Bombardment</sc> ability, then the ability of the <i>X&ndash;89 Bacterial Weapon &Omega;</i> technology cannot be used.<sup>2,10</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does X-89 Bacterial Weapon Omega work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Planetary Shield, by blocking Bombardment, blocks it too.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a war sun causes a unit to be treated as if it does not have a <sc>Planetary Shield</sc> ability, that unit may still use the ability of the <i>Magen Defense Grid &Omega;</i> technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a war sun shut off Magen Defense Grid?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only the original version, which needs a unit with Planetary Shield &hellip; The Codex Omega version only&rdquo; needs a structure.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a war sun causes a unit to be treated as if it does not have a <sc>Planetary Shield</sc> ability, all units in that system with a <sc>Bombardment</sc> ability may use it against the planet that unit is on.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can one ship bombard several planets, or split its dice between them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A war sun removes Planetary Shield from other players&rsquo; units in its system, so all your bombarding units can then hit planets with PDS.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_politics">Politics (Strategy Card)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When resolving the primary ability of the <i>Politics</i> card, the active player cannot show the agenda cards they drew to any other player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After Politics, is it public whether the agenda cards went to the top or bottom of the deck?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You also can&rsquo;t show the cards themselves to other players, since you may only reveal hidden components you own.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other players may see whether each agenda card is placed on the top or the bottom of the agenda deck. Although the active player cannot show the cards, they may tell other players what the cards are, truthfully or not.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After Politics, is it public whether the agenda cards went to the top or bottom &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; everyone can see where each card goes; only the faces stay secret &hellip; you can tell them what they are (truthfully or not).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The speaker token cannot be exchanged in a transaction. However, the active player may make a deal with another player, including receiving a transaction, in exchange for choosing them as the new speaker, following the normal rules for deals and transactions.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the speaker token be traded or sold?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Not in a transaction &mdash; it isn&rsquo;t a tradeable component. But the Politics player can take payment, or make deals, in exchange for naming someone speaker.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The active player looks at and places the top two cards of the agenda deck even if the custodians token has not yet been removed from Mecatol Rex.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Politics look at and rearrange the agenda deck before anyone takes Mecatol Rex?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; the agenda deck exists from the start and nothing restricts that part of Politics before the agenda phase begins.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_producing_units">Producing Units</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Whenever a unit is produced, its cost must be paid, unless the effect that is producing the unit explicitly says otherwise.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do units &lsquo;placed&rsquo; by an effect cost resources, and do they count as producing?&rdquo;: &ldquo;An effect that says &lsquo;produce&rsquo; means you pay the cost unless it says otherwise.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Units that are placed or gained by an effect, rather than produced, are free and do not count as being produced; for example, ships placed with the <a href="/F_muaat">Muaat</a> player&rsquo;s <sc>Star Forge</sc> ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do units &lsquo;placed&rsquo; by an effect cost resources, and do they count as producing?&rdquo;: &ldquo;&rsquo;Place&rsquo; or &lsquo;gain&rsquo; is free and isn&rsquo;t producing, so Muaat&rsquo;s Star Forge doesn&rsquo;t count as producing and doesn&rsquo;t trigger Magmus Reactor&rsquo;s trade good.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an effect allows a player to produce a unit of a specific type, then factional variations do not prevent that player from producing that type of unit.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do faction units like the Muaat Prototype War Sun count as their base unit type?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a faction unit counts as its base unit type for every purpose.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_production">Production (Unit Ability)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A unit&rsquo;s <sc>Production</sc> value is the maximum number of units that may be produced by that unit, regardless of cost. For example, a space dock with &ldquo;<sc>Production</sc> 6&rdquo; could produce six dreadnoughts or six fighters.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Production limit the number of units or their total cost?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The number of units &hellip; a war sun uses one point of Production and you pay its 12 cost separately.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Placement of produced units is not limited by a unit&rsquo;s <sc>Production</sc> value, only by the location of the producing units.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where are produced ground forces placed?&rdquo;: &ldquo;With several producing planets you may split them among those planets however you like, even all on one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>For example, say a player is performing a tactical action on tile 75. They have one Space Dock I each on Abaddon and Ashtroth, but not on Loki. They combine their production to place seven infantry. They could place all seven on Abaddon, all seven on Ashtroth, or split them. They cannot place any on Loki.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;split them among those planets however you like.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an effect allows a player to produce a ship of a specific type, then factional variations do not prevent that player from producing that type of ship.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do faction units like the Muaat Prototype War Sun count as their base unit type?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a faction unit counts as its base unit type for every purpose.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ground forces produced during the <b>Production</b> step of a tactical action cannot be committed to land on a planet during that tactical action, as the <b>Invasion</b> step has already been resolved.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can ground forces I produce take an uncontrolled planet in the same tactical action?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; production is the last step, after invasion &hellip; They can invade in a later action.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_promissory_notes">Promissory Notes</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player may choose to reveal any or all of the promissory notes in their hand.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I show my action cards or secret objectives to other players?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; you may reveal your own hidden cards to anyone at any time, and they still count as hidden afterwards.&rdquo; Promissory notes in a player&rsquo;s hand are that player&rsquo;s hidden cards.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When two players make a trade for a promissory note, the specific promissory note that was traded may be hidden from other players.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I trade on a promissory note I received from another player, and must I show it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you needn&rsquo;t show it, though the recipient may want to see it first.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player receives a card, such as a promissory note, in a transaction during that card&rsquo;s timing window, they may play it immediately. For example, a promissory note with &ldquo;At the start of your turn:&rdquo; timing may be played immediately if the transaction occurred during the &ldquo;start of turn&rdquo; timing window.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I receive Creuss IFF in a transaction at the start of my turn and play it in that same window?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you can trade for the note and then resolve its start-of-turn ability.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Returning a promissory note to its owner as part of resolving it is not a transaction. The note is returned even if the two players are not neighbors.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is returning a used promissory note to its owner a transaction?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; returning it is part of resolving the card, so it needs no agreement and happens even if you are no longer neighbors.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_readied">Readied</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Passive abilities on an exhausted card are still in effect while that card is exhausted.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are breakthroughs technologies &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Their passive abilities keep working while exhausted.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Planets will also ready at the end of each agenda phase.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does the agenda phase happen, and when are planets readied?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Planets ready again at the end of the agenda phase.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_reinforcements">Reinforcements</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If a player wishes to place a unit, but there are none of that type left in their reinforcements, they may remove a unit of that type from any system that does not contain one of their command tokens and place that instead.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I remove my own units from the board when I want to?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The one exception is taking a unit off the board when you need to place one and none are in reinforcements.&rdquo; command-token restriction stated in the structures entry.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Command tokens on a player&rsquo;s command sheet are not in that player&rsquo;s reinforcements.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens if I would gain a command token but have none left in my reinforcements?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Tokens you spend &hellip; go back to your reinforcements, not to your command sheet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_rerolls">Rerolls</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Effects that reroll &ldquo;combat rolls&rdquo; do not reroll anti&ndash;fighter barrage, bombardment or space cannon rolls.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Morale Boost, faction modifiers and rerolls affect Anti-Fighter Barrage, Space Cannon, Bombardment or Ambush?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; only rolls made against a unit&rsquo;s combat value are combat rolls.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_ships">Ships</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A Fighter II may count toward either the fleet pool limit or a player&rsquo;s capacity.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Fighter IIs move on their own into a system already at my fleet limit?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If ships in the destination have room for them, they don&rsquo;t count against your fleet pool.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_space_cannon">Space Cannon (Unit Ability)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step happens before combat. Any secret objectives that require winning a combat or similar cannot be scored if all of one player&rsquo;s ships in a system are destroyed during this step.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Space Cannon, Bombardment or landing on an empty planet count as winning a combat for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Destroying ships with Space Cannon during movement &hellip; involves no combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each unit with the Space Cannon ability may use it once during the Space Cannon Offense step, regardless of how many ships moved into the active system, and again during the Space Cannon Defense step if ground forces are committed to its planet. A PDS does not need ground forces on its planet to use its Space Cannon ability, and may do so even if its system contains its owner&rsquo;s command token.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How many times does a PDS fire in one tactical action?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Once at Space Cannon Offense, after movement, however many ships came in, plus once more at Space Cannon Defense if ground forces are committed to its planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player that chooses to use the <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability of their units may choose whether or not they use the ability of the <i>Plasma Scoring</i> technology, if they own it.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is Plasma Scoring&rsquo;s extra die optional &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It is a &lsquo;may&rsquo;, so you can decline it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If multiple players are resolving the <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability of their units against the active player, the active player must resolve capacity limits after assigning one player&rsquo;s hits and before the next player makes their space cannon roll.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is capacity checked if a carrier is lost?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Immediately whenever it isn&rsquo;t combat, for example after each player&rsquo;s Space Cannon Offense volley, before the next player fires.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot assign hits produced by a space cannon roll to their own units.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;During Space Cannon Offense, who can shoot at whom?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The active player fires first and chooses which other player&rsquo;s ships in the active system take the hits.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may use the <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability of a ship in the active system to cancel a hit produced during the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a ship use Sustain Damage against Space Cannon hits?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; Sustain Damage can cancel any hit produced against the unit, Space Cannon included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The &ldquo;0&rdquo; side of the d10 represents a result of 10.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the 0 on the ten-sided die count as 0 or 10?&rdquo;: &ldquo;10 &mdash; the Living Rules Reference FAQ confirms the 0 face is a 10.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_space_combat">Space Combat</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Abilities that resolve at the start of a space combat, such as the <i>Assault Cannon</i> technology and the <a href="/F_mentak">Mentak</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Mentak_Coalition#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Ambush"><sc>Ambush</sc></a> faction ability, are resolved before the <b>Anti&ndash;Fighter Barrage</b> step.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Mentak&rsquo;s Ambush work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;At the start of space combat, before Anti-Fighter Barrage.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player&rsquo;s fighters and ground forces do not count against capacity during combat. At the end of combat, any excess units are removed and returned to that player&rsquo;s reinforcements.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is capacity checked if a carrier is lost?&rdquo;: &ldquo;During combat fighters and ground forces may exceed capacity and keep fighting; the excess is removed when the space combat ends.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships that have announced a retreat still roll dice during the Roll Dice step of that combat round, then retreat during the Retreat step. Retreating this way avoids abilities that resolve after the combat round, such as the <a href="/F_norr">N&rsquo;orr</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Sardakk_N&#x27;orr#:~:text=Exotrireme"><i>Exotrireme II</i></a>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do ships that announced a retreat still roll that round?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; announcing a retreat doesn&rsquo;t stop you firing; you roll normally and then leave in the retreat step. Retreating in that step also escapes Exotrireme II.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a space combat reaches a state where neither player can win, the attacker must retreat. If the attacker cannot retreat, their units in that combat are destroyed.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens if neither side can ever win a space combat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Living Rules Reference FAQ says the attacker must retreat. If the attacker has nowhere legal to go, players generally treat those ships as destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Modifying the move value of a ship will have no effect when retreating. Retreats are limited to adjacent systems.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where can my ships retreat to?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Movement bonuses don&rsquo;t matter, as a retreat always goes to an adjacent system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Dark Energy Tap</i> technology allows a player to retreat into a system that does not contain their ships or a planet they control.<sup>2,8</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where can my ships retreat to?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Dark Energy Tap drops the second requirement.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships can retreat out of the active system, and transport units out of it, even if it contains the retreating player&rsquo;s command token.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I retreat or Skilled Retreat into, or carry units out of, systems that contain my command token?&rdquo;: &ldquo;retreating ships can carry units out of the combat system even if your token is there, because the transport restriction excludes the active system, whoever activated it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>While retreating, a player may transport zero or more ground forces from planets they control in the active system. The player need not decide which ground forces they transport until they execute the retreat.<sup>2,10</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When my ships retreat, can they pick up ground forces from my planets in the combat system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;retreating ships move and transport units like any other movement.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>While retreating, a player cannot transport more fighters and/or ground forces than they have capacity in the retreating ships.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Skilled Retreat differ &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the usual transport rules apply.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a player retreats, their fighters that remain in the space area of the active system are removed, even if that system contains one of their space docks.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens to units that can&rsquo;t go with a retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Fighters and ground forces in the space area that the retreating ships can&rsquo;t carry are destroyed, even if a space dock could have supported them.&rdquo; RulesPal says destroyed; LRR 78.7b says removed.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ground forces transported by retreating ships, or moved with the <i>Skilled Retreat</i> action card, cannot land on planets in the system they move to; they remain in the space area.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can ground forces that retreated with my ships, or moved with Skilled Retreat, land on a planet in the new system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; landing only happens in the invasion step of a tactical action in the active system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player only wins a combat if a space combat or ground combat occurred. Destroying all opposing ships with the Space Cannon ability, destroying all opposing ground forces with the Bombardment ability, landing ground forces on a planet without opposing ground forces, or the opposing player avoiding the combat with the <a href="/F_naalu">Naalu</a> player&rsquo;s Foresight faction ability does not count as winning a combat. A planet containing only another player&rsquo;s structures does not cause a ground combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Space Cannon, Bombardment or landing on an empty planet count as winning a combat for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a combat is a space combat or a ground combat &hellip; Destroying ships with Space Cannon during movement, bombarding away all defenders, or landing unopposed involves no combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The &ldquo;0&rdquo; side of the d10 represents a result of 10.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the 0 on the ten-sided die count as 0 or 10?&rdquo;: &ldquo;10.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_space_dock">Space Dock</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A Space Dock is both a structure and a unit.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are PDS and space docks ground forces?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; PDS and space docks are structures, a separate unit type.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Fighters transported during movement remain assigned to the transporting ship until movement is complete. They cannot be counted toward a space dock&rsquo;s allocation until after movement is complete.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After ships stop moving, are fighters and ground forces still tied to the ship that carried them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; only while moving.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_space_stations">Space Stations</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player gains control of a space station when they are the only player with ships in that space station&rsquo;s system.<sup>2,6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When do I gain and lose control of a space station?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You gain it as soon as you are the only player with ships in its system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the player who controls the space station moves their ships out of the system, they retain control of that space station.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;you keep it after leaving until another player becomes the only one with ships there.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A space station is not a planet; controlling one does not prevent a player from being eliminated.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;A space station isn&rsquo;t a planet, so controlling one doesn&rsquo;t stop you being eliminated.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_speaker">Speaker</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>During the strategy and agenda phases, the speaker will have the first opportunity to resolve abilities during each timing window.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When several players want to resolve abilities at the same time, who goes first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;In the strategy and agenda phases, go in order starting with the speaker instead.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_status_phase">Status Phase</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player cannot score public objectives if that player does not control each planet in their home system.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I score objectives if another player controls a planet in my home system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Not public objectives &mdash; you must control every planet in your home system to score one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If there are multiple abilities that players wish to resolve at the same time during the status phase, such as two players each using the <a href="/F_creuss">Creuss</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Wormhole%20Generator"><i>Wormhole Generator</i></a> faction technology, they are resolved in initiative order.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If two players have abilities at the same point of the status phase, such as both using Wormhole Generator, who goes first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Initiative order: the player with the lower initiative number resolves first.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_strategy_card">Strategy Card</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>During three&ndash; and four&ndash;player games, each player will choose two strategy cards during the strategy phase.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do strategy cards work in three- and four-player games?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Each player picks two strategy cards, in a second pass of the selection.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Players will use the lower of the two initiative numbers on their strategy cards to determine the initiative order.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With two strategy cards, which one sets my initiative, and must I use the lower card first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only your lower-numbered card sets your initiative.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player with two strategy cards may perform the strategic actions of those cards in either order. They still take only one action per turn.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With two strategy cards, which one sets my initiative, and must I use the lower card first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You still take one turn at a time like everyone else, never two in a row, and you may use either card first.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How many actions do I take per turn, and in what order must I use action types?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Exactly one action per turn &hellip; You may use them in any order over the round.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_strategy_phase">Strategy Phase</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If a player chooses a strategy card, but later loses it, they will keep any trade goods that were on the strategy card.<sup>5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Hacan&rsquo;s Quantum Datahub Node swap strategy cards?&rdquo;: &ldquo;trade goods were already collected when the cards were picked.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_supernova">Supernova</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A supernova may be activated, and ships in a supernova may move out of it; only ships moving into or through a supernova are prevented.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Mirage sit in a supernova, and can I activate a supernova, build there and leave?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A supernova can be activated; it only stops ships moving into or through it &hellip; and ships can always move out.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a ship finds itself in a supernova by some uncommon game effect, it may remain there without harm.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who can move into or through a supernova?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A supernova never destroys ships already there.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Mirage planet can be placed in a supernova, as nothing prevents a planet from being in a supernova.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Mirage sit in a supernova, and can I activate a supernova, build there and leave?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes to all. Nothing stops a planet being in a supernova, and an exploration card can give you the Mirage there.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_sustain_damage">Sustain Damage</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A ship in the active system may use its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability to cancel a hit produced by a space cannon roll during the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a ship use Sustain Damage against Space Cannon hits?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; Sustain Damage can cancel any hit produced against the unit, Space Cannon included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Mechs may use their <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability to cancel a hit produced by a bombardment roll.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a ship use Sustain Damage against Space Cannon hits?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; Sustain Damage can cancel any hit produced against the unit.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A non&ndash;fighter ship cannot use its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability to cancel a hit produced by an anti&ndash;fighter barrage roll, unless an effect such as the <i>Waylay</i> action card makes it eligible to be hit.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Raid Formation and Waylay interact?&rdquo;: &ldquo;With Waylay, those hits still hit ships and the opponent assigns them normally, using Sustain Damage on undamaged ships if they like.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A unit may only use its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability once in each timing window, even if it is repaired.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a ship repaired by the Empyrean flagship use Sustain Damage again in the same round?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a unit can sustain only once per instance of the timing window.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If multiple ships use their <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability, a <i>Direct Hit</i> action card may be played for each.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can several Direct Hits or Shields Holdings be played in the same combat round?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Several Direct Hits, yes: each Sustain Damage use is its own trigger, so you can answer each with a Direct Hit on that ship.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a player uses the <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability of one of their ships, they do not have to decide if another of their ships in the same combat will use its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability until after their opponent has used or declined to use a <i>Direct Hit</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can several Direct Hits or Shields Holdings be played in the same combat round?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Sustain Damage is used unit by unit, so you can wait to see a Direct Hit before sustaining with more.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A mech cannot be destroyed by <i>Direct Hit</i>, as it is not a ship.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Direct Hit destroy a mech that uses Sustain Damage?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; Direct Hit targets ships, and mechs are ground forces.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_synergy">Synergy</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When scoring an objective, a player may treat each technology they own that matches one color of their synergy as any color of that synergy.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are breakthroughs technologies &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Synergy lets a technology of one linked colour count as the other for colour objectives.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_system_tiles">System Tiles</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A home system remains its original owner&rsquo;s home system, even if another player gains control of its planets.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a home system stop being a home system when another player takes it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; it stays its original owner&rsquo;s home system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_tactical_action">Tactical Action</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Each of these five steps happen during every tactical action, unless explicitly skipped; the active player does not have to choose between fighting a combat or producing units, for example.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I move into a system, fight, invade and then produce there in the same tactical action?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; the steps run activation, movement, space combat, invasion, then production.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An ability that ends a player&rsquo;s turn ends that turn as soon as it is resolved. No further abilities may be resolved during that same timing window. &ldquo;End of turn&rdquo; abilities may still be resolved.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When must Xxcha use Nullification Field &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Once the turn ends, nothing more happens in it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_technology">Technology</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If a player is instructed to research a technology by any game effect, that player must meet the prerequisites for the technology they research.<sup>1,2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If an effect lets me research a technology, do I pay for it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You still meet prerequisites (specialties can help).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player is instructed to gain a technology, that player does not have to meet the prerequisites.<sup>1,2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;&rsquo;Gain&rsquo; is different from &lsquo;research&rsquo;: gaining ignores prerequisites.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player is playing a faction with a faction&ndash;specific unit upgrade, they cannot research or otherwise gain the generic upgrade for that unit type.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do faction technologies replace generic ones?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only faction unit upgrades replace the matching generic upgrade; all other faction technologies are extras.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An exhausted technology still satisfies prerequisites.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a technology I just researched, or one that is exhausted, count as a prerequisite?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes to both. Owned technologies always count, exhausted or not.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The first technology researched with the primary ability of the <i>Technology</i> strategy card may satisfy a prerequisite of the second technology researched with that ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;the first technology from the Technology primary counts for the second.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an exhausted technology is returned to a player&rsquo;s technology deck and later researched again, it is gained readied.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If an exhausted technology returns to my deck and is researched again, is it exhausted?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; cards in the deck have no state, so it comes back ready.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_trade">Trade (Strategy Card)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Choosing players to resolve the secondary ability of <i>Trade</i> without spending a command token is not a transaction. Those players do not need to be neighbors of the active player, and it does not count toward the active player&rsquo;s transaction limit.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is replenishing other players&rsquo; commodities with Trade a transaction?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; it is a strategy card effect: recipients needn&rsquo;t be neighbors, can&rsquo;t refuse, it doesn&rsquo;t use up a transaction&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A deal made during a given step is non&ndash;binding if any part of it is to resolve during a later step. As such, if Alice makes a deal to choose a player with the primary ability in exchange for commodities from that player after they perform the secondary, then such a deal will be non&ndash;binding.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is a deal binding?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only when its terms resolve immediately &hellip; Promises about future actions, and if-then conditions, are non-binding and can be broken.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player has their maximum number of commodities, and is instructed to replenish their commodities, they will take no commodities. However, they will still trigger any &ldquo;when [this] player replenishes commodities&rdquo; effects, most notably the <i>Trade Agreement</i> promissory note.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Minister of Commerce, Industry, Peace and War interact &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Minister of Commerce still pays 1 trade good per neighbor when your commodities were already full, since you still replenished.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_transactions">Transactions</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Players cannot resolve transactions during the strategy phase or the status phase. Players may still negotiate during these phases, but the terms of a deal that involves trade goods cannot be resolved immediately, so that deal is not binding.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is a deal binding?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only when its terms resolve immediately.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may perform a transaction during combat with their opponent, even if they are neighbors only in the active system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is a transaction an action, and when during a turn can it happen?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the active player can make one with each neighbor at any point in their turn, even mid-combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Commodities received in a transaction cannot be given back as part of that same transaction; doing so would require a second transaction.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When do commodities turn into trade goods, and can I give them and get them back in one transaction?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A transaction is one simultaneous exchange, so commodities you receive can&rsquo;t be sent back in it; that would be a second transaction.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player may perform multiple actions on their turn, they may still only resolve one transaction with each of their neighbors.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is a transaction an action, and when during a turn can it happen?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the active player can make one with each neighbor at any point in their turn.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_transport">Transport</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A ship cannot pick up more units from a planet than it has capacity.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a carrier pick up more units than its capacity so the excess are destroyed, or leave fighters behind to die?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t pick up more than the moving ships&rsquo; capacity &hellip; But you can load ground forces and leave fighters or infantry already in space behind; capacity is a system total, so those left over are removed if what remains can&rsquo;t hold them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The carrier may transport the four fighters, leaving behind all eight of the infantry in the system.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a carrier pick up more units than its capacity so the excess are destroyed &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t pick up more than the moving ships&rsquo; capacity &hellip; But you can load ground forces and leave fighters or infantry already in space behind; capacity is a system total, so those left over are removed if what remains can&rsquo;t hold them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The carrier may transport four of the eight infantry, leaving behind the four remaining infantry in the system. Now that the system has zero capacity, all four of the fighters will be removed.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a carrier pick up more units than its capacity so the excess are destroyed &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t pick up more than the moving ships&rsquo; capacity &hellip; But you can load ground forces and leave fighters or infantry already in space behind; capacity is a system total, so those left over are removed if what remains can&rsquo;t hold them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The carrier may transport two of the eight infantry and two of the four fighters, leaving behind the six remaining infantry in the system. Now that the system has zero capacity, the two remaining fighters will be removed.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a carrier pick up more units than its capacity so the excess are destroyed &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t pick up more than the moving ships&rsquo; capacity &hellip; But you can load ground forces and leave fighters or infantry already in space behind; capacity is a system total, so those left over are removed if what remains can&rsquo;t hold them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The carrier may transport nothing, leaving behind all eight of the infantry in the system. Now that the system has zero capacity, all four of the fighters will be removed.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a carrier pick up more units than its capacity so the excess are destroyed &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t pick up more than the moving ships&rsquo; capacity &hellip; But you can load ground forces and leave fighters or infantry already in space behind; capacity is a system total, so those left over are removed if what remains can&rsquo;t hold them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The carrier cannot attempt to pick up all eight infantry, even if it immediately removes four of them (and the fighters).<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a carrier pick up more units than its capacity so the excess are destroyed, or leave fighters behind to die?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t pick up more than the moving ships&rsquo; capacity, so you can&rsquo;t overload to destroy your own units.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A ship may pick up ground forces from a planet in the active system, usually to invade a different planet in the same system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can ground forces already on one planet invade another planet in the same system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only if a ship with capacity picked them up into the space area during the movement step.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A ship may transport units in its system as it retreats or is otherwise moved out of the active system.<sup>1,2,5,10</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I retreat or Skilled Retreat into, or carry units out of, systems that contain my command token?&rdquo;: &ldquo;retreating ships can carry units out of the combat system even if your token is there.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_units">Units</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Fighters and infantry are semi&ndash;limited, via the use of tokens.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are fighter and infantry tokens limited, and what are they for?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Tokens are unlimited, but each stack of tokens must sit with at least one plastic piece of the same type.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A faction&ndash;specific unit counts as its base unit type for all purposes. For example, the <a href="/F_muaat">Muaat</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Embers_of_Muaat#War_Sun:~:text=Prototype%20War%20Sun">Prototype War Sun</a> is a war sun. Only the researched unit upgrade card is a technology; the unit printed on a faction sheet is not.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do faction units like the Muaat Prototype War Sun count as their base unit type?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; a faction unit counts as its base unit type for every purpose, so the Prototype War Sun is a war sun. Only the researched upgrade card is a technology.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an effect allows a player to produce a ship of a specific type, then factional variations do not prevent that player from producing that type of ship.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do faction units like the Muaat Prototype War Sun count as their base unit type?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a faction unit counts as its base unit type for every purpose.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot remove their own units from the game board at will; units are only removed by game effects, such as being destroyed or captured. The exception is that a player may remove a unit from the board in order to place it when there are none of that type in their reinforcements.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I remove my own units from the board when I want to?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; units leave the board only through the game (destroyed, captured and so on). The one exception is taking a unit off the board when you need to place one and none are in reinforcements.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_unit_upgrades">Unit Upgrades</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If a player is playing a faction with a faction&ndash;specific unit upgrade, they cannot research or otherwise gain the generic upgrade for that unit type.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do faction technologies replace generic ones?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only faction unit upgrades replace the matching generic upgrade; all other faction technologies are extras.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_victory_points">Victory Points</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>During the agenda phase, if multiple players gain victory points at the same time, they gain them in speaker order, starting with the speaker and proceeding clockwise.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;In what order are agenda-phase secret objectives scored?&rdquo;: &ldquo;In speaker order, clockwise; it only matters if several players would win from the points, a tie that also goes clockwise from the speaker.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_warfare">Warfare (Strategy Card)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The active player performs a tactical action in any system without placing a command token, even if the system already has their command token in it; that system still counts as being activated.<sup>2,13</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Warfare strategy card work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The primary lets you redistribute your tokens and perform a tactical action without placing a command token, even in a system that already holds yours or a Diplomacy token &hellip; that system still counts as activated.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The active player may redistribute their command tokens before and after this tactical action.<sup>2,13</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Warfare strategy card work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The primary lets you redistribute your tokens and perform a tactical action without placing a command token.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After the active player resolves the primary ability of the <i>Warfare</i> strategy card, each other player, beginning with the player to the left of the active player and proceeding clockwise, may spend one command token from their strategy pool to resolve the <sc>Production</sc> abilities of units in their home system.<sup>2,13</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Warfare strategy card work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The secondary produces with all your units in your home system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Resolving the secondary ability of Warfare does not activate the player&rsquo;s home system. It may be resolved even if that system already contains one of their command tokens.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Warfare secondary work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It doesn&rsquo;t activate the system and works even if your home system already has your token.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During the tactical action of the <i>Warfare</i> primary ability, ships may move into a system that contains one of the player&rsquo;s command tokens, but ships still cannot move out of a system that contains one of the player&rsquo;s command tokens.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Warfare strategy card work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can move ships into an already-activated system that way, but not out of one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>No command token is placed in the system where the <i>Warfare</i> primary ability is resolved, so ships in that system, including ones produced there, may move during a later tactical action that round.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Warfare strategy card work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;perform a tactical action without placing a command token, even in a system that already holds yours or a Diplomacy token, so newly produced ships can move later that round; that system still counts as activated.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_wormhole_nexus">Wormhole Nexus</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The wormhole nexus will flip if a unit exists in the system while inactive, even if that unit did not move through a gamma wormhole.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do players reach the Wormhole Nexus &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Once a unit enters or someone gains Mallice, the Nexus flips.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_wormholes">Wormholes</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A system containing a wormhole is not an anomaly.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which systems count as anomalies?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Empty systems and wormhole systems are not anomalies unless bordered.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system cannot be adjacent to itself, even if it has multiple wormholes in it.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do ships in Mecatol Rex&rsquo;s system, or in an anomaly, count as adjacent to it for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a system isn&rsquo;t adjacent to itself.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the two systems are adjacent because of a wormhole and because of a shared edge or another reason, the player may choose whether or not the ship moves through the wormhole.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do wormholes work, and must my ships use them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A wormhole only adds connections: you don&rsquo;t have to use it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the two systems are adjacent because of two types of wormholes, the player chooses which type of wormhole the ship moves through.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does the Ion Storm flip, and does using a matching wormhole in the same system flip it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;With several wormhole routes, the moving player chooses which, if any, they use&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_arborec">The Arborec</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If the Arborec player produces units with both a space dock and Letani Warriors in the same system, any infantry they produce must be placed on a planet that contains a Letani Warrior.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Arborec infantry Production work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Arborec space docks can never produce infantry, so infantry output is capped by the Letani Warriors&rsquo; own Production.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Arborec player may produce <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Arborec#Mech:~:text=Letani%20Behemoth">Letani Behemoth</a> with their space docks.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Arborec infantry Production work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Arborec space docks can never produce infantry.&rdquo; Mechs are not infantry.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Stymie</i> cannot place a command token in the home system of an eliminated player.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Arborec Stymie note stop the Arborec from producing in their home system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Stymie only covers non-home systems containing the holder&rsquo;s units and the systems adjacent to them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>No infantry may move beyond an adjacent system. For example, consider a series of systems A&ndash;B&ndash;C. Only systems connected by a &ldquo;&ndash;&rdquo; are adjacent. In this case:<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Bioplasmosis or Transit Diodes move ground forces onto enemy planets, or chain across systems?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Bioplasmosis moves each infantry once, to a planet in its own or an adjacent system, so no hopping across several systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An infantry cannot move from A to B, then B to C.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Bioplasmosis or Transit Diodes move ground forces onto enemy planets, or chain across systems?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Bioplasmosis moves each infantry once, to a planet in its own or an adjacent system, so no hopping across several systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An infantry may move from A to B, while another infantry moves from B to C.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Bioplasmosis moves each infantry once.&rdquo; Each infantry moves separately.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An infantry may move from A to B, while another infantry moves from C to B.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Bioplasmosis moves each infantry once.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An infantry may move from B to A, while another infantry moves from B to C.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Bioplasmosis moves each infantry once.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When multiple units use their <sc>Production</sc> ability, the <sc>Production</sc> value is pooled. As such, if the Arborec player has two Letani Warrior I in a system, they may produce two infantry for one resource, rather than two.<sup>1,2,3,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Their Production pools with your other units in the system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If Letani Warriors are on different planets, any produced ground forces may be split between those planets, regardless of the production value of the Letani Warriors on that planet.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where are produced ground forces placed?&rdquo;: &ldquo;With several producing planets you may split them among those planets however you like, even all on one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The produced units must be paid for.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal on Integrated Economy (a similar non-Production effect): &ldquo;paid normally.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Duha Menaimon</i> must be in the system when it is activated for its effect to trigger. It will not trigger if it is moved into the active system later in the turn.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does the Arborec flagship produce, and can it move in first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;so it only works if it is already in the active system; it can&rsquo;t move in and then produce&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The produced unit must be paid for.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Saar&rsquo;s Chaos Mapping production work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you still pay for it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>No unit is using its <sc>Production</sc> ability. The <i>Sarween Tools</i> technology and similar effects will not apply.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Sarween Tools work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It needs a unit&rsquo;s Production ability, so it doesn&rsquo;t apply to effects that just let you produce.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A unit with <sc>Production</sc> in an entropic scar still has <sc>Production</sc>, even though it cannot use it. As such, Dirzuga Rophal may still be used in that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Arborec commander work with a Letani in an Entropic Scar?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; the scar stops unit abilities being used but doesn&rsquo;t remove them, and the commander is a text ability that only needs a unit with Production.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>No unit is using its <sc>Production</sc> ability. The <i>Sarween Tools</i> technology and similar effects will not apply.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Sarween Tools work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It needs a unit&rsquo;s Production ability, so it doesn&rsquo;t apply to effects that just let you produce.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Arborec player may produce Letani Behemoth with their space docks.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Arborec infantry Production work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Arborec space docks can never produce infantry.&rdquo; Mechs are not infantry.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_argent">The Argent Flight</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The Argent player always votes first, even if they are the speaker.<sup>2,14</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With Argent Flight&rsquo;s Zeal, what is the voting order?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Argent votes first, even as speaker.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When the Argent player is the speaker, effects timed &ldquo;after the speaker votes&rdquo;, such as the <i>Bribery</i> action card, resolve right after the Argent player votes.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With Argent Flight&rsquo;s Zeal, what is the voting order?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Effects timed &lsquo;after the speaker votes&rsquo;, like Bribery, come right after the Argent vote when Argent is speaker.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other players vote in their regular order after the Argent player votes; clockwise starting with the player to the left of the speaker (skipping over the Argent player as necessary).<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;then voting resumes as normal from the speaker&rsquo;s left.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <sc>Anti&ndash;Fighter Barrage</sc> ability can still be used even if no fighters are present in the system.<sup>1,2,8</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Argent Flight&rsquo;s Raid Formation work &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Argent fire barrage even when the opponent has no fighters.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The chosen ship becomes damaged, it does not use its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability. As such, effects that trigger when a ship uses its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability, such as the <i>Direct Hit</i> action card, cannot be played.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Argent Flight&rsquo;s Raid Formation work &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It produces no hits and those ships don&rsquo;t use Sustain Damage, so it can&rsquo;t destroy anything and Direct Hit or Non-Euclidean Shielding don&rsquo;t come into it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <a href="/F_letnev">Letnev</a> player owns their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Barony_of_Letnev#:~:text=Non-Euclidean%20Shielding"><i>Non&ndash;Euclidean Shielding</i></a> faction technology, this will not reduce the number of ships that the Argent player chooses.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Direct Hit or Non-Euclidean Shielding don&rsquo;t come into it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Argent player plays the <i>Waylay</i> action card, hits in excess of their opponent&rsquo;s fighters will first cause chosen ships to become damaged, then those same hits must still be assigned.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Raid Formation and Waylay interact?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Raid Formation works off the barrage roll without using up the hits. With Waylay, those hits still hit ships and the opponent assigns them normally.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>For example, say the opponent has three fighters and three dreadnoughts, undamaged. The Argent player plays <i>Waylay</i>, and rolls five hits. This allows them to cause two of the dreadnoughts to become damaged. The opponent then has to assign all five hits. The final dreadnought may use its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability to cancel one of those hits.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;the opponent assigns them normally, using Sustain Damage on undamaged ships if they like.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player with the <i>Light/Wave Deflector</i> technology cannot move their ships through systems containing the Argent player&rsquo;s structures, if the Argent player owns <i>Aerie Hololattice</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Argent&rsquo;s Aerie Hololattice stop ships with Light/Wave Deflector moving through?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it says other players can&rsquo;t move through systems with Argent structures, and &lsquo;cannot&rsquo; is absolute.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships in systems with one of the Argent player&rsquo;s command tokens may move.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Argent and L1Z1X heroes move ships &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;from any number of systems for the Argent hero, ignoring command tokens in the systems they leave.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships move directly to their destination system. They may only transport units from their origin system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Argent and L1Z1X heroes move ships &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;no path or move value &hellip; The ships can carry units starting with them, but can&rsquo;t pick up along a path.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Helix Protocol</i> may be used to move ships into the wormhole nexus. If the nexus is inactive, this will cause it to activate.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Argent and L1Z1X heroes move ships &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the Argent hero can move ships into the Wormhole Nexus, which flips to its active side at the end of that action.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_letnev">The Barony of Letnev</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player may play <i>War Funding</i> when the Letnev player has zero or one trade goods. The Letnev player will lose the single trade good, if applicable, and the other effects will resolve as normal.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Letnev War Funding promissory note still work if the Letnev player has fewer than 2 trade goods?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; the reroll still applies.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If there are multiple hits that can be canceled, then the Letnev player must cancel two hits whenever one of their units uses its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability; they cannot cancel just one.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Non-Euclidean Shielding work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Every time one of your units uses Sustain Damage, it cancels 2 hits instead of 1.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Say the Letnev player has exactly one hit produced against them remaining, and one of their units uses its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability to cancel it. If additional hits are produced against the Letnev player&rsquo;s units, such as with the <i>Reflective Shielding</i> action card, the Letnev player cannot retroactively use the previous use of <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> to allow <i>Non&ndash;Euclidean Shielding</i> to cancel a second hit.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Non-Euclidean Shielding work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Unused cancellation is lost; it doesn&rsquo;t carry over to a later hit such as Devotion&rsquo;s.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If multiple units use their <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability within a single step, the trade goods are gained one at a time.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Letnev commander give a trade good for each unit that uses Sustain Damage?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; each use triggers it separately.&rdquo; each use triggers separately, so goods are gained one at a time.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_saar">The Clan of Saar</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The Saar player does not gain trade goods from <sc>Scavenge</sc> for the planets in their home system at the start of the game, as they begin the game in control of those planets rather than gaining control of them.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do I control both planets in my home system at the start &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Because you begin with control rather than gaining it, abilities like Saar&rsquo;s Scavenge don&rsquo;t trigger at setup.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>All of the Saar player&rsquo;s ground forces must be placed on the same planet.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Saar&rsquo;s Ragh&rsquo;s Call work in an invasion?&rdquo;: &ldquo;moves the Saar ground forces from one planet to another single planet&rdquo; Ragh&rsquo;s Call moves all Saar ground forces to a single planet.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A unit may be produced on each of the Saar player&rsquo;s turns.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Saar&rsquo;s Chaos Mapping production work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;At the start of each of your turns, including the turn you pass, you may produce one unit.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A unit may be produced on the turn the Saar player passes.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;including the turn you pass.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The produced unit must be paid for.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Saar&rsquo;s Chaos Mapping production work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you still pay for it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>No unit is using its <sc>Production</sc> ability. The <i>Sarween Tools</i> technology and similar effects will not apply.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Saar&rsquo;s Chaos Mapping production work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;no unit&rsquo;s Production ability is used, so Sarween Tools doesn&rsquo;t apply.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_creuss">Creuss</a> player may place a wormhole token in an asteroid field that contains the Saar player&rsquo;s ships, but still cannot activate that system while <i>Chaos Mapping</i> applies.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Creuss use a wormhole token to get into an asteroid field protected by Saar&rsquo;s Chaos Mapping?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Creuss can place the token there but still can&rsquo;t activate the system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A Floating Factory does not count toward the Saar player&rsquo;s fleet pool.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are Saar Floating Factories ships, and do they count against the fleet pool?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; Floating Factories are never ships &hellip; They don&rsquo;t count against your fleet pool.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>There is no limit to the number of Floating Factories that may be in the same system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Saar Floating Factories move and produce in the same action, and how many can share a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Any number can share a system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a Floating Factory produces ground forces, they may be placed in the space area or on a planet they control in that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where are produced ground forces placed?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If the producing unit is in space, like a Saar Floating Factory &hellip;, they can go in that space area or on any planet you control in the system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A Floating Factory may move into the active system and use its <sc>Production</sc> ability during the same tactical action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Saar Floating Factories move and produce in the same action, and how many can share a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; nothing requires a dock to start the turn in the active system, so a Floating Factory can move in, carry ground forces to invade, and produce.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When resolving an ability of the <i>Construction</i> strategy card, the Saar player must control a planet in the system they place a Floating Factory in.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where do Saar Floating Factories go, and how many can share a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If an effect places a space dock on a planet, choose the planet as the effect says, then the Floating Factory&rsquo;s own rule puts it in that system&rsquo;s space area.&rdquo; Saar still needs a planet for Construction; implicit.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Game effects that affect ship movement, such as the <i>Gravity Drive</i> technology, the <i>Flank Speed</i> action card, the <i>Light/Wave Deflector</i> technology, the <i>Antimass Deflectors</i> technology, etc, may affect Floating Factories.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are Saar Floating Factories ships &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;every ability that affects movement, such as Gravity Drive, Flank Speed, Antimass Deflectors or Light/Wave Deflector, still applies to them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A Floating Factory moving through or out of a gravity rift must roll for removal. It will receive the +1 to its movement.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do movement effects like Gravity Drive and gravity rifts apply to Saar Floating Factories?&rdquo;: &ldquo;they get Gravity Drive, Flank Speed and rift bonuses, and he later confirmed they roll when leaving a gravity rift.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Hits produced by space cannon rolls cannot be assigned to a Floating Factory.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;hits cannot be assigned to it because it is not a ship.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a Floating Factory is in a system that contains none of the Saar player&rsquo;s ships, and another player moves ships into that system, the Floating Factory is immediately destroyed.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens to a Saar Floating Factory when enemy ships arrive, and can it retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If it is alone with enemy ships, it is destroyed before anything can be done.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>This will happen before the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step. The Floating Factory is destroyed, even if all of the other player&rsquo; ships are destroyed during this step.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens to a Saar Floating Factory when enemy ships arrive, and can it retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it is destroyed before anything can be done.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Saar Floating Factories move and produce in the same action, and how many can share a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A Floating Factory is blockaded, and destroyed, the moment enemy ships arrive without Saar ships.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If another player&rsquo;s ships move through a system that contains a Floating Factory and none of the Saar player&rsquo;s ships, the Floating Factory is not destroyed; it is destroyed only if those ships end their movement in that system.<sup>5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Saar Floating Factories move and produce in the same action, and how many can share a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;destroyed, the moment enemy ships arrive.&rdquo; Ships passing through do not arrive.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Saar player announces a retreat during a space combat, but all their ships are destroyed that round, any Floating Factories in that system are destroyed without retreating.<sup>5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens to a Saar Floating Factory when enemy ships arrive, and can it retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If it is alone with enemy ships, it is destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Saar player retreats from a space combat, Floating Factories in the active system may retreat with the Saar player&rsquo;s other ships, even if those ships are only fighters. Floating Factories may also be moved by the <i>Skilled Retreat</i> action card, provided they are never alone with another player&rsquo;s ships.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens to a Saar Floating Factory when enemy ships arrive, and can it retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If other Saar ships, even fighters, are with it, it can retreat or Skilled Retreat with them as long as it is never left alone with enemy ships.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A unit is not adjacent to the system it is in.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do ships in Mecatol Rex&rsquo;s system, or in an anomaly, count as adjacent to it for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a system isn&rsquo;t adjacent to itself.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Saar player gains control of multiple planets, they may deploy one Scavenger Zeta to each.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If Saar take several planets in one action, can they deploy a mech on each?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; each planet gained is its own timing window.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_keleres">Council Keleres</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The Keleres player chooses their starting technology after all other players have gained their starting technologies.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does the Council Keleres choose its starting technologies?&rdquo;: &ldquo;After every other faction has gathered its starting technologies, regardless of speaker order.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After playing <i>Keleres Rider</i>, a player may use additional abilities, including playing other rider cards.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can one player play several riders on the same agenda, on different outcomes?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; FFG confirmed by email that you can play several riders in the same vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any other riders may be played to predict the same or a different outcome.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can one player play several riders on the same agenda, on different outcomes?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; FFG confirmed by email that you can play several riders in the same vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of a successful <i>Keleres Rider</i> will be resolved after the effect of the agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an agenda outcome and a rider both award points, which resolves first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The agenda&rsquo;s outcome resolves first, then the riders that predicted it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Riders may be played when resolving <i>Executive Order</i>.<sup>2,6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does an agenda voted through Keleres&rsquo;s Executive Order count as the agenda phase?&rdquo;: &ldquo;&rsquo;when/after an agenda is revealed&rsquo; effects still work.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a third law is put into play, the <i>Dictate Policy</i> secret objective cannot be scored until the agenda phase.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does an agenda voted through Keleres&rsquo;s Executive Order count as the agenda phase?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; phase steps like readying don&rsquo;t happen and agenda-phase secrets, Drive the Debate included, can&rsquo;t be scored.&rdquo; Dictate Policy is an agenda-phase secret objective.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Drive the Debate</i> secret objective cannot be scored while resolving <i>Executive Order</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;agenda-phase secrets, Drive the Debate included, can&rsquo;t be scored.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When resolving <i>Executive Order</i>, the <a href="/F_argent">Argent</a> player does not vote first, but still casts additional votes.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Argent&rsquo;s Zeal gives its extra votes but not the vote-first part.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Keleres player is neighbors with another player, that player is neighbors with the Keleres player.<sup>*</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Unverified</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Keleres I.I.H.Q. Modernization make systems adjacent for movement?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it only makes players neighbors for transactions.&rdquo; Symmetry is not addressed.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Custodia Vigilia is not on the game board, and does not exist in a system.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;though it has no system tile.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships placed by <i>Overwing Zeta</i> count against the Keleres player&rsquo;s fleet pool immediately; any excess non&ndash;fighter ships are removed at once.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is the fleet pool limit checked, and can I bring extra ships into a fight and trim afterwards?&rdquo;: &ldquo;At all times, except for ships that are only moving through other systems. Once ships arrive in the active system, any non-fighter ships over your fleet pool are removed straight away.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a planet contains multiple mechs, the invading player must pay one influence for each, as separate payments.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;each mech is its own instance of the ability, so the attacker pays 1 influence per mech.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_rebellion">The Crimson Rebellion</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li><sc>Sundered</sc> only prevents the Rebellion player&rsquo;s ships from moving through wormholes other than epsilon wormholes. Other wormholes still make systems adjacent for the Rebellion player for all other purposes, such as <sc>Space Cannon</sc>, neighbors, objectives, action cards, and the ability of the Exile.<sup>2,6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Crimson Rebellion&rsquo;s inability to use wormholes remove wormhole adjacency?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; it only stops them moving through wormholes, as players relay from the designer. Adjacency still counts for neighbors, breach placement, Space Cannon and objectives.&rdquo; covers most listed purposes; action cards and Exile not mentioned.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player has a ship in a system containing multiple breaches at the end of the status phase, they may remove any or all of them.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Crimson Rebellion breaches work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;At the end of the status phase a player with ships there may remove any or all active breaches.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The destroyer must be on the game board when the combat ends in order to place the breach. If the destroyer was in the active system, and was destroyed in the space combat, it cannot be used to place a breach.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Crimson Rebellion breaches work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A breach goes in the combat&rsquo;s system at the end of combat &hellip; if one of your destroyers is in range then.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A breach may be placed after a space combat and/or a ground combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;at the end of combat.&rdquo; Not limited to space combat.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Rebellion player transports ground forces during the swap, those ground forces will be placed in the space area of their destination system. They cannot be committed to planets in that system until and unless a tactical action is resolved in that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After a Crimson Rebellion swap, are the ships locked, and can moved units invade?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A swap is a component action, not a tactical action, so there&rsquo;s no invasion unless the ability says so.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Fragment Reality</i> cannot be used during a combat between two other players.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Crimson Rebellion hero join a combat between two other players?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; it is meant for combats you are part of; there are no rules for three-way combat&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships placed by <i>Fragment Reality</i> count against the Rebellion player&rsquo;s fleet pool immediately, but capacity is not checked until the end of the combat.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is the fleet pool limit checked, and can I bring extra ships into a fight and trim afterwards?&rdquo;: &ldquo;At all times, except for ships that are only moving through other systems. Once ships arrive in the active system, any non-fighter ships over your fleet pool are removed straight away.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The passive, movement ability of <i>Resonance Generator</i> is still in effect when the card is exhausted.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, on the similar Lazax Gate Folding: &ldquo;The passive part &hellip; doesn&rsquo;t need the card ready, so it works while exhausted.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_deepwrought">The Deepwrought Scholarate</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If a player gains the <i>Antimass Deflectors</i> technology through <i>Share Knowledge</i> and later loses it, their units may remain in and move out of any asteroid fields they are currently in, but cannot move additional units into or through.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If I lose Antimass Deflectors while my ships sit in an asteroid field, or place units in an anomaly, what happens?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Nothing &mdash; anomaly rules restrict moving into or through them, not being in them or moving out. Ships already there stay and may leave normally&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Deepwrought player replaces the <i>Antimass Deflectors</i> technology, their units may remain in and move out of any asteroid fields they are currently in, but cannot move additional units into or through.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If I lose Antimass Deflectors while my ships sit in an asteroid field, or place units in an anomaly, what happens?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Nothing &mdash; anomaly rules restrict moving into or through them, not being in them or moving out. Ships already there stay and may leave normally&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Deepwrought player does not have the capability to prevent other players from using the ability of Aello.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a researching player use both the Deepwrought commander and alliance for -2?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; each is a separate optional trigger, and the Deepwrought and their ally can&rsquo;t refuse.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player has the Deepwrought player&rsquo;s <i>Alliance</i> promissory note, then other players may use the ability of either or both (or neither):<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a researching player use both the Deepwrought commander and alliance for -2?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; each is a separate optional trigger, and the Deepwrought and their ally can&rsquo;t refuse.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Another player may use the ability from both for a two resource discount, triggering the commodity ability for both the Deepwrought player and the player with their <i>Alliance</i> promissory note.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a researching player use both the Deepwrought commander and alliance for -2?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; each is a separate optional trigger, and the Deepwrought and their ally can&rsquo;t refuse.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Another player may use the ability from only the Deepwrought player for a one resource discount, triggering the commodity ability for just the Deepwrought player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;each is a separate optional trigger.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Another player may use the ability from only the player with the <i>Alliance</i> promissory note for a one resource discount, triggering the commodity ability for just the player with the <i>Alliance</i> promissory note.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;each is a separate optional trigger.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Another player may use the ability from neither player, and receive no discount; the commodity ability will trigger for neither the Deepwrought player nor the player with their <i>Alliance</i> promissory note.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;each is a separate optional trigger.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If they also owned the purged technology, the Deepwrought player too will research another technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If Wave Function Collapse purges a technology another player gained through Share Knowledge &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the holder owns the gained technology, so it is purged and they research another.&rdquo; Covers a different holder.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If another player has gained the purged technology through <i>Share Knowledge</i>, they own it, so they purge it and research another technology, which they keep after <i>Share Knowledge</i> is returned.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If Wave Function Collapse purges a technology another player gained through Share Knowledge, does that player research a replacement?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Most players say yes: the holder owns the gained technology, so it is purged and they research another, keeping it after the note returns.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player purges a technology, they will gain three command tokens instead of researching another technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Nekro Virus play Reveal Prototype?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes, gaining 3 command tokens instead of the technology.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_muaat">The Embers of Muaat</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ships placed with <sc>Star Forge</sc> are not produced, and do not trigger effects that occur when units use <sc>Production</sc>, such as the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Embers_of_Muaat#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Magmus%20Reactor"><i>Magmus Reactor</i></a> technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do units &lsquo;placed&rsquo; by an effect cost resources, and do they count as producing?&rdquo;: &ldquo;&rsquo;Place&rsquo; or &lsquo;gain&rsquo; is free and isn&rsquo;t producing, so Muaat&rsquo;s Star Forge doesn&rsquo;t count as producing and doesn&rsquo;t trigger Magmus Reactor&rsquo;s trade good.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The produced units must be paid for.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can Integrated Economy produce?&rdquo;: &ldquo;up to the new planet&rsquo;s resource value in cost, paid normally.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each trade good gained may trigger the <a href="/F_mentak">Mentak</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Mentak_Coalition#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Pillage"><sc>Pillage</sc></a> faction ability.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly can Mentak use Pillage?&rdquo;: &ldquo;After each instance of a neighbor gaining trade goods.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Muaat player&rsquo;s units may exist in and move out of the supernova system, even if the Muaat player does not own <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Embers_of_Muaat#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Magmus%20Reactor"><i>Magmus Reactor</i></a>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Muaat hero&rsquo;s Nova Seed treat Muaat&rsquo;s own units &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Muaat&rsquo;s own stay, the war sun included, and can leave later, since nothing stops ships moving out of a supernova.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Muaat player moves a war sun into a system containing the <a href="/F_naalu">Naalu</a> player&rsquo;s ships, the Muaat player, as the active player, may resolve <i>Nova Seed</i> before the Naalu player resolves their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Naalu_Collective#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Foresight"><sc>Foresight</sc></a> faction ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who goes first when the Muaat hero and Naalu Foresight both trigger as a war sun moves in?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Muaat, as the active player, so the hero can destroy the Naalu ships before Foresight.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If multiple Ember Colossi are in or adjacent to the system, each places one infantry.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do several Muaat mechs each place infantry when Star Forge is used?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; each Muaat mech in or adjacent to the Star Forge system places its own infantry.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_hacan">The Emirates of Hacan</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>This ability does not make the Hacan player neighbors with all other players.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who counts as my neighbor?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Hacan can transact with non-neighbors, and anyone can with them, but that doesn&rsquo;t make them neighbors for Pillage.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Hacan player is still limited to one transaction with every other player per turn.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Hacan trade action cards?&rdquo;: &ldquo;still within one transaction per player per turn.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any number of action cards may be traded.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Any number of action cards can go either way in a transaction the Hacan are part of.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Hacan player has their <i>Political Secret</i> played during an agenda, they may still trade action cards, as <sc>Arbiters</sc> is a passive ability.<sup>1,2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What exactly does Political Secret stop the affected player from doing?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Mandatory and passive abilities still apply, such as &hellip; Hacan&rsquo;s Arbiters.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If either strategy card had trade goods on it at the start of the strategy phase, those trade goods remain with the player that originally chose the strategy card.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Hacan&rsquo;s Quantum Datahub Node swap strategy cards?&rdquo;: &ldquo;trade goods were already collected when the cards were picked.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>In a three&ndash; or four&ndash;player game, the Hacan player chooses which strategy cards are swapped.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Hacan&rsquo;s Quantum Datahub Node swap strategy cards?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Hacan chooses both which of their cards to give and which face-up card to take.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Public Disgrace</i> action card cannot be played against a strategy card the Hacan player takes with <i>Quantum Datahub Node</i>, as <i>Quantum Datahub Node</i> is resolved at the end of the strategy phase and is not a player &ldquo;choosing a strategy card.&rdquo;<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Public Disgrace be played against a strategy card swap from Quantum Datahub Node or Imperial Arbiter?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; Public Disgrace triggers when a player chooses a strategy card, and those swaps at the end of the strategy phase aren&rsquo;t choices.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>This ability may be triggered only once for each die rolled. The Hacan player cannot spend two (or more) trade goods to apply +2 (or more) to a single die, but they may spend two (or more) trade goods to apply +1 to each of two (or more) dice.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Hacan flagship&rsquo;s trade-good bonus work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;spend 1 trade good for +1 on any of your units&rsquo; dice in its system, at most once per die.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>This ability only affects combat rolls. It cannot modify rolls for <sc>Space Cannon</sc>, <sc>Anti&ndash;Fighter Barrage</sc>, or other unit abilities.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Hacan flagship&rsquo;s trade-good bonus work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It only modifies combat rolls, not Space Cannon or other ability rolls.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_empyrean">The Empyrean</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The Empyrean player does not decide to use this ability until after the other player has activated a system. Any deal made to use this ability before the system has been activated is non&ndash;binding.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is a deal binding?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Promises about future actions, and if-then conditions, are non-binding and can be broken.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Empyrean player does not decide to use this ability until after the other player has activated a system. Any deal made to use this ability before the system has been activated is non&ndash;binding.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is a deal binding?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Promises about future actions, and if-then conditions, are non-binding and can be broken.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A unit may only use its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability once in each timing window, even if it is repaired.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a ship repaired by the Empyrean flagship use Sustain Damage again in the same round?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a unit can sustain only once per instance of the timing window.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may play a <i>Direct Hit</i> action card during the same timing window as this ability. If they do so, the ship is destroyed.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Direct Hit destroy a mech that uses Sustain Damage?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A ship repaired by the Empyrean flagship after sustaining can still be Direct Hit, since it did use Sustain Damage.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Influence must be spent separately for each use of this ability. As such, any excess influence used to repair a ship is wasted, and cannot be used to repair a different ship.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Each repair is paid separately.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>While the agenda phase allows a player to resolve transactions with all other players, it does not make that player neighbors with all other players.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can players make transactions with non-neighbors during the agenda phase?&rdquo;: &ldquo;That doesn&rsquo;t make them neighbors for anything else, as the developer confirmed when asked about Mentak&rsquo;s Pillage.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Empyrean player becomes neighbors with all players when the final player moves ships into a system containing one of the Empyrean player&rsquo;s command tokens, Xuange will unlock, but the Empyrean player will not be able to resolve its ability from that movement.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does a commander unlock?&rdquo;: &ldquo;As soon as its condition is met, mid-turn if need be &hellip; It is checked only after the current effect fully resolves.&rdquo; The current effect is the movement, so Xuange unlocks only after the movement resolves, after the window to resolve its ability from that movement.</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I unlock the Naalu commander partway through a production and use it for the rest?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; production is one step, so the unlock condition is only met after it finishes; use the commander from your next production.&rdquo; The same applies to movement.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Empyrean player moves a ship into a system containing one of the <a href="/F_saar">Saar</a> player&rsquo;s Floating Factories, and no other ships, that <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Clan_of_Saar#Space_Dock:~:text=Floating%20Factory">Floating Factory</a> will be blockaded and destroyed before the Empyrean player can unlock Xuange. If the Empyrean and Saar players are not neighbors in any other way, then Xuange will not unlock.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Saar Floating Factories move and produce in the same action, and how many can share a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A Floating Factory is blockaded, and destroyed, the moment enemy ships arrive without Saar ships.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does a commander unlock?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It is checked only after the current effect fully resolves.&rdquo; By then the Floating Factory has already been destroyed.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Only the system in which a ship ends its movement is &ldquo;moved into&rdquo;; systems a ship moves through are not. As such, Xuange returns at most one command token for each movement.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do abilities that trigger on ships moving into a system &hellip; count systems merely passed through?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; only the system where movement ends is &lsquo;moved into&rsquo;, so the Empyrean commander returns at most one token per move.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If The Fracture is in play when the Empyrean player resolves <i>Multiverse Shift</i>, frontier tokens will be placed in the systems in The Fracture that do not contain a planet.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;the Empyrean hero can later put them on its empty systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>This effect may cancel a <i>Sabotage</i> action card.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Sabotage cancel another Sabotage or a promissory note?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Other cancel effects that lack that restriction can still cancel a Sabotage.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any targeted planets, system, units, players, cards, outcomes, and/or other such objects.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly is Sabotage played&rdquo;: &ldquo;After the player announces the card&rsquo;s choices (target planet, ship or player, rider prediction, retreat destination and so on).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the action card that was canceled by a Watcher was to be used to perform a component action, the active player must perform a different action, or pass.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my component action card is cancelled by Sabotage &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a cancelled card counts as not played, so you still take an action (or pass, if allowed).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the component action was to be the second action the active player was to perform on their turn, through an effect such as the <i>Fleet Logistics</i> technology, the active player may end their turn without performing a second action and without passing.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my component action card is cancelled by Sabotage, have I used my action for the turn?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If it was the optional extra action from Fleet Logistics, you can just end your turn.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_sol">The Federation of Sol</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li><sc>Orbital Drop</sc> may be used multiple times each round, once for each command token spent from the Sol player&rsquo;s strategy pool. Each use is a separate action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Sol&rsquo;s Orbital Drop be used more than once a round &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It can be used as many times as you have tokens in your strategy pool, each use as a separate action.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Sol player owns the <i>Hyper Metabolism</i> technology, they gain four command tokens during the status phase.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is Hyper Metabolism misprinted?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It stacks with Sol&rsquo;s extra token, so Sol with it gains 4.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player received <i>Military Support</i> on a previous turn, they may play it at the start of the Sol player&rsquo;s turn, and immediately perform a transaction with the Sol player to regain it. However, <i>Military Support</i> cannot be played twice in one timing window. The active player cannot play <i>Military Support</i> again until the Sol player&rsquo;s next turn.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How often can Sol&rsquo;s Military Support be used?&rdquo;: &ldquo;At most once per Sol turn.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Military Support</i> may be played the turn the Sol player passes.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How often can Sol&rsquo;s Military Support be used?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it is used at the start of Sol&rsquo;s turn.&rdquo; The turn Sol passes still starts.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Military Support</i> may be played when the Sol player has no command tokens in their strategy pool.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Sol&rsquo;s Military Support work if Sol has no strategy token &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It works without a token &mdash; the note only spends Sol&rsquo;s strategy token if able.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Sol player has no ground forces on a planet they control, ground combat will not occur, and so they cannot place an infantry on that planet. This includes if they lose the last of their ground forces during the <b>Bombardment</b> step of the invasion.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is there a ground combat if Bombardment or Space Cannon Defense wipes out one side first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; ground combat only happens if both players still have ground forces on the planet after those steps.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_creuss">The Ghosts of Creuss</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The effect of the <i>Enforced Travel Ban</i> law does not affect the Creuss player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What exactly does Creuss&rsquo;s Quantum Entanglement connect &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;no game effect, Enforced Travel Ban included, can stop that.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Creuss player has units or controls planets in a system with an alpha wormhole, and another player has units or controls planets in a system with a beta wormhole, or vice versa, then the Creuss player and that player are neighbors.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are the Ghosts of Creuss neighbors with a player on a beta wormhole when Creuss sits on an alpha wormhole?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; Quantum Entanglement makes alpha and beta wormholes adjacent for Creuss, and adjacency created that way also makes them neighbors, so the two players can transact.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other players cannot use <sc>Quantum Entanglement</sc> to produce hits from a unit with the <sc>Deep Space Cannon</sc> ability against the Creuss player&rsquo;s units.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What exactly does Creuss&rsquo;s Quantum Entanglement connect &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Other players can&rsquo;t fire Space Cannon or move through that connection.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Enforced Travel Ban</i> law has no effect on <sc>Slipstream</sc>.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Enforced Travel Ban stop Creuss&rsquo;s Slipstream bonus?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; Slipstream only checks for a wormhole in the ship&rsquo;s starting system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may receive the <i>Creuss IFF</i> in a transaction during their turn. If received at the start of that player&rsquo;s turn, that player may play it immediately.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I receive Creuss IFF in a transaction at the start of my turn and play it in that same window?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it is still the start of your turn until you actually take an action.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Creuss IFF</i> cannot place wormhole tokens in the home system of an eliminated player, unless the player resolving it controls a planet in that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Creuss IFF note put a wormhole token in a system with enemy ships if I control a planet there?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the two placement options are alternatives: a system containing a planet you control, or a non-home system with no other players&rsquo; ships. Meeting either is enough&rdquo; An eliminated player&rsquo;s home system is still a home system, so only the planet-control option allows it.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any wormhole may trigger <i>Dimensional Splicer</i>, including the one produced by the ability of the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Flagship:~:text=Hil%20Colish"><i>Hil Colish</i></a>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Creuss flagship&rsquo;s delta wormhole trigger Dimensional Splicer?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it is a wormhole.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Creuss player chooses which ship the hit is assigned to.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When Dimensional Splicer or Yin&rsquo;s Devotion hits my ship, who picks the target &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Creuss or Yin player chooses which of your ships takes the hit.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Shields Holding</i> action card may be used to cancel the hit.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Direct Hit and Shields Holding work on hits from Dimensional Splicer or Yin Devotion?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes to both.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The chosen ship may cancel the hit by using its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability, if present. A different ship with the <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability cannot do so.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When Dimensional Splicer or Yin&rsquo;s Devotion hits my ship &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only that ship can use its own Sustain Damage to cancel it; you can&rsquo;t move the hit elsewhere.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the chosen ship cancels the hit by using its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability, the <i>Direct Hit</i> action card may be used.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Creuss flagship&rsquo;s delta wormhole trigger Dimensional Splicer?&rdquo;: &ldquo;which can set up Direct Hit.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>This ability is mandatory for the Creuss player in every status phase after it has been researched.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is Creuss&rsquo;s Wormhole Generator mandatory &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A player relays a developer email saying it is mandatory each status phase, since it lacks &lsquo;may&rsquo;.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Wormhole Generator</i> cannot place wormhole tokens in the home system of an eliminated player, unless the Creuss player controls a planet in that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Creuss IFF note put a wormhole token in a system with enemy ships if I control a planet there?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the two placement options are alternatives: a system containing a planet you control, or a non-home system with no other players&rsquo; ships. Meeting either is enough&rdquo; Wormhole Generator uses the same two placement options as the Creuss IFF, and an eliminated player&rsquo;s home system is still a home system, so only the planet-control option allows it.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Creuss wormhole tokens may be placed in The Fracture.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do empty Fracture systems get frontier tokens, and can wormholes go there?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Wormholes can be placed there, since nothing forbids it.&rdquo; wormhole tokens may be placed in Fracture systems.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Wormhole Generator &Omega;</i> cannot place wormhole tokens in the home system of an eliminated player, unless the Creuss player controls a planet in that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Creuss IFF note put a wormhole token in a system with enemy ships if I control a planet there?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the two placement options are alternatives: a system containing a planet you control, or a non-home system with no other players&rsquo; ships. Meeting either is enough&rdquo; Wormhole Generator uses the same two placement options as the Creuss IFF, and an eliminated player&rsquo;s home system is still a home system, so only the planet-control option allows it.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Creuss wormhole tokens may be placed in The Fracture.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do empty Fracture systems get frontier tokens, and can wormholes go there?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Wormholes can be placed there, since nothing forbids it.&rdquo; wormhole tokens may be placed in Fracture systems.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <i>Hil Colish</i> moves first, then the Creuss player uses the new position of the <i>Hil Colish</i> when determining if the active system may be reached by their other ships.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Creuss flagship&rsquo;s &lsquo;move before or after your other ships&rsquo; work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If it moves into the active system first, that system becomes adjacent to other delta wormholes such as the Creuss home system, so your other ships can come in through it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <i>Hil Colish</i> moves last, then it may move itself into (or through, if its move value is increased) the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Creuss%20Gate">Creuss Gate</a> or Creuss home system. Before it does so, the Creuss player&rsquo;s other ships may move through the wormhole created by the <i>Hil Colish</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;or it can move after them. It can also use its own wormhole, for example to go home.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The delta wormhole moves with the <i>Hil Colish</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;Its system counts as containing a delta wormhole.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If another player uses the <i>Light/Wave Deflector</i> technology to move through a system that contains the Creuss player&rsquo;s ships, that player may move through the delta wormhole in the <i>Hil Colish</i>&rsquo;s system.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can other players use the Creuss Gate and the Creuss flagship&rsquo;s delta wormhole?&rdquo;: &ldquo;enemies need a way to move through it (such as Light/Wave Deflector).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <i>Hil Colish</i> is returned to the reinforcements by a gravity rift, its delta wormhole ceases to exist; other ships cannot use it to reach the active system.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;If it moves first through a gravity rift and is destroyed, the wormhole is gone before the others move.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Creuss player may use this ability to access the inactive wormhole nexus without a gamma wormhole. Moving ships into the wormhole nexus this way will cause it to flip.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do players reach the Wormhole Nexus, and can the Creuss agent help on the first turn?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the Creuss agent, used after activation and before movement, makes it adjacent to a delta wormhole system, so ships can move in that turn. Once a unit enters &hellip; the Nexus flips.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a ship moves from the system containing the Ion Storm, it will not flip, unless the active player may move ships through the Ion Storm wormhole and chooses to do so.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does the Ion Storm flip &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;With several wormhole routes, the moving player chooses which, if any, they use, which decides whether the Ion Storm flips.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A ship only triggers Sai Seravus if it moves through a wormhole, including a wormhole in the system it started its movement in. A ship that moves through a system containing a wormhole without using that wormhole does not trigger Sai Seravus.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Creuss commander trigger if my ship just passes through a wormhole system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only if the ship actually moved through the wormhole, whether it started in the wormhole&rsquo;s system or entered it along the way.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Systems containing Creuss wormhole tokens count toward the unlock condition of Sai Seravus.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Creuss wormhole tokens count for unlocking the Creuss commander?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; the tokens are wormholes for all purposes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any units in either system are moved with their system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When the Creuss hero swaps two system tiles, do the units move with them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; units are on the tile&rsquo;s planets and space area, so they go with it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Icarus Drive does not have to be in the activated system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;For the Creuss mech&rsquo;s Icarus Drive, which system does &lsquo;this system&rsquo; mean?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The system the mech was in, not necessarily the one another player activated.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The wormhole token is placed in the system the Icarus Drive was in.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;a unit&rsquo;s &lsquo;this system&rsquo; always means its own system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_lizix">The L1Z1X Mindnet</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Any space docks that are placed by this ability during a tactical action may produce units during the <b>Production</b> step of that tactical action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I produce at my home space dock during a tactical action elsewhere &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the production step only uses units in the system you just activated.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the L1Z1X player would have to place a structure, but there are none of that type left in their reinforcements, they may, but are not required to, remove a structure of that type from any system that does not contain one of their command tokens and place that instead.<sup>1,2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my reinforcements are empty, must I take units from the board to place a new one?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; the rules let you remove a unit from the board to place it elsewhere, but you are not required to. This matters for effects such as L1Z1X Assimilate or Swords to Plowshares.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If all my space docks or PDS are on the board, can I place another?&rdquo;: &ldquo;if none are in reinforcements, you may take one from a system without your command token and place it where the effect directs.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <sc>Planetary Shield</sc> ability will prevent the use of the <sc>Harrow</sc> ability on a planet.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the L1Z1X Harrow ability work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Planetary Shield stops it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The L1Z1X player may use their <sc>Harrow</sc> ability at the end of the round in which their last ground force was destroyed. This may cause the combat to end in a draw.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the L1Z1X Harrow ability work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It still resolves after the round in which L1Z1X lose their last ground force; if it then kills the last defender, the combat is a draw.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Every unit in the active system with the <sc>Bombardment</sc> ability may use it against the planet the ground combat is taking place on, regardless of whether that unit bombarded that planet during the <b>Bombardment</b> step of the invasion.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the L1Z1X Harrow ability work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Every ship with Bombardment there can join in, not just those that bombarded that planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An Annihilator may bombard when using <sc>Harrow</sc> if that mech is not currently participating in ground combat, even if it has been committed to a different planet, and has been or will be involved in another ground combat during the current invasion.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can L1Z1X mechs bombard through Harrow?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only when not in that ground combat: a mech fighting on a planet can&rsquo;t bombard it, but one on another planet in the system can.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The L1Z1X player may use the ability of the <span class="removed"><i>X&ndash;89 Bacterial Weapon &Omega;</i></span> or <i>X&ndash;89 Bacterial Weapon &Omega;&Omega;</i> technologies whenever they bombard.<sup>10,15</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are there common clarifications for specific objectives &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Harrow is Bombardment.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the L1Z1X player has no command tokens in their strategy pool, <i>Cybernetic Enhancements</i> cannot be played.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can L1Z1X Cybernetic Enhancements or Xxcha Political Favor be used if their owner has no command token in the strategy pool?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; &hellip; if there is no token to remove, nothing on it resolves.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Inheritance Systems</i> may be used when researching a unit upgrade technology, including the L1Z1X player&rsquo;s own faction technologies such as <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_L1Z1X_Mindnet#Dreadnought:~:text=Super-Dreadnought">Super Dreadnought</a> II.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can L1Z1X&rsquo;s Inheritance Systems be used for?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It ignores all prerequisites for one research, unit upgrades and your own faction technologies like Super Dreadnought included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If <i>Inheritance Systems</i> is researched as the first of two technologies researched back to back, such as with the primary ability of the <i>Technology</i> strategy card or the <i>Ixthian Artifact</i> agenda, it may be used immediately to ignore the prerequisites of the second technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can L1Z1X&rsquo;s Inheritance Systems be used for?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Researched as the first tech of the Technology primary, it can pay for the second.&rdquo; Ixthian Artifact and other back-to-back research follow the same timing; no source names them.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships in systems with one of the L1Z1X player&rsquo;s command tokens may move.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Argent and L1Z1X heroes move ships &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;ignoring command tokens in the systems they leave.&rdquo; (Stated for the Argent hero.)</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships move directly to their destination system. They may only transport units from their origin system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Argent and L1Z1X heroes move ships &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;no path or move value &hellip; The ships can carry units starting with them, but can&rsquo;t pick up along a path.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships moving out of a gravity rift must roll for removal.<sup>1,2,3,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do gravity rifts work &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Each ship that leaves or passes through a rift rolls, and is removed on a 1-3.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships cannot move into a supernova, even with <i>Dark Space Navigation</i>.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an ability moves units outside a tactical action &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Anomaly rules still apply, so no ability moves ships into a supernova unless it says so.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships may travel into an asteroid field only if the L1Z1X player owns the <i>Antimass Deflectors</i> technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Anomaly rules still apply.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An Annihilator may bombard when using the L1Z1X player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_L1Z1X_Mindnet#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Harrow"><sc>Harrow</sc></a> ability if that mech is not currently participating in ground combat, even if it has been committed to a different planet, and has been or will be involved in another ground combat during the current invasion.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can L1Z1X mechs bombard through Harrow?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a mech fighting on a planet can&rsquo;t bombard it, but one on another planet in the system can.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_bastion">Last Bastion</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Galvanize tokens are placed from the Bastion player&rsquo;s reinforcements. If there are none in their reinforcements, a galvanize token cannot be moved from elsewhere on the board.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Last Bastion&rsquo;s spaceport card, Helios upgrade and Galvanize tokens work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Galvanize tokens are limited; with all 7 in use the effect fails until one is freed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The planet&rsquo;s resource value is increased by two with Helios II, not three; the bonuses of Helios I and Helios II do not stack.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Last Bastion&rsquo;s spaceport card, Helios upgrade and Galvanize tokens work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Helios II replaces Helios I, so +2, not +3.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Effects that cancel hits, such as <sc>Sustain Damage</sc>, cannot be used to prevent units from being destroyed.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Sustain Damage save a ship from &hellip; &lsquo;destroy&rsquo; effects?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; Sustain Damage only cancels hits, and these effects destroy units without producing hits.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step of a tactical action, players resolve the <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability of their eligible units starting with the active player, and going around the table in a clockwise order. Each player will make their space cannon roll and have the hits assigned, likely destroying some units, before the next player begins their own space cannon roll. Should the Bastion player have one of their units destroyed by another player&rsquo;s space cannon roll, and choose to use the ability of <i>Entity 4X41A &ldquo;Apollo&rdquo;</i>, then in doing so they destroy a unit with the <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability, that unit will not perform a space cannon roll if the player that owns that unit has not yet made their space cannon roll.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When several players fire Space Cannon Offense, are they resolved together?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; one player at a time &hellip; Each player&rsquo;s roll, hit assignment, Sustain Damage and responses like Direct Hit finish before the next fires, so a unit destroyed earlier &hellip; never gets to shoot.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_mahact">The Mahact Gene&ndash;Sorcerers</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If an agenda requires the Mahact player to remove one of their command tokens from their fleet pool, and they only have other players&rsquo; command tokens in their fleet pool, they must remove one of those tokens, and return it to the reinforcements of the player that owns it.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Armed Forces Standardization affect Mahact holding other players&rsquo; fleet tokens?&rdquo;: &ldquo;other players&rsquo; tokens removed go back to their owners&rsquo; reinforcements.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player that cannot vote due to a game effect or because they have zero influence on readied planets cannot be targeted by <i>Genetic Recombination</i>.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Genetic Recombination target a player who isn&rsquo;t voting, such as one who played a rider?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; it triggers before a player casts votes, and a non-voter never reaches that point.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If multiple Crimson Legionnaires are destroyed at the same time, the Mahact player decides whether to gain a commodity or convert a commodity to a trade good individually for each.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If several Crimson Legionnaires die at once, does each trigger separately?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; each has its own trigger, resolved one after another.&rdquo; each triggers separately, so the choice is made per Legionnaire.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If two Crimson Legionnaires are destroyed at the same time, the Mahact player may choose to gain a commodity, then immediately convert that commodity to a trade good.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If several Crimson Legionnaires die at once, does each trigger separately?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; each has its own trigger, resolved one after another.&rdquo; triggers resolve one after another, so a gained commodity can then be converted by the next.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Jae Mir Kan may be used even if the Mahact player has no command tokens in their strategy pool.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Mahact agent, or other effects used instead of spending a strategy token, work with an empty strategy pool?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; they replace the token, so none is needed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Mahact player cannot use any &ldquo;when/after you activate a system&rdquo; abilities.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I use Scanlink Drone Network or other activation abilities when activating a system with the Mahact commander?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the commander ends your turn as part of resolving it, so no window for other abilities opens&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other players cannot use any &ldquo;when/after another player activates a system that&hellip;&rdquo; abilities.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I use Scanlink Drone Network or other activation abilities when activating a system with the Mahact commander?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the commander ends your turn as part of resolving it, so no window for other abilities opens&rdquo; No window opens for any player.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Mahact player places a second command token in a system using Il Na Viroset, the <i>Counterstroke</i> action card cannot be played.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I play Counterstroke on a system I activate that already has my command token?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Mahact&rsquo;s commander doesn&rsquo;t help either, since activating that way ends your turn at once with no after-activation abilities&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Mahact player moves another player&rsquo;s ships, ground forces on planets are left behind.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Mahact hero&rsquo;s Benediction work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;move all units in its space area.&rdquo; Ground forces on planets are not in the space area.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Mahact player moves the <a href="/F_saar">Saar</a> player&rsquo;s units, any Floating Factories in the origin system will also be moved.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Mahact hero&rsquo;s Benediction work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;move all units in its space area (fighters, ground forces in space and Saar docks included).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Mahact player may move another player&rsquo;s ships into an asteroid field only if that player owns the <i>Antimass Deflectors</i> technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Mahact hero&rsquo;s Benediction work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The moved ships&rsquo; owner must be able to enter that system, so no pushing them into asteroid fields or supernovas they can&rsquo;t enter.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Mahact player may move another player&rsquo;s ships into a supernova only if that player owns the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Embers_of_Muaat#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Magmus%20Reactor"><i>Magmus Reactor</i></a> <a href="/F_muaat">Muaat</a> factional technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Mahact hero&rsquo;s Benediction work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;no pushing them into asteroid fields or supernovas they can&rsquo;t enter.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who can move into or through a supernova?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Magmus Reactor lets ships move into and stop in one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Mahact player cannot move the <a href="/F_creuss">Creuss</a> player&rsquo;s ships from a system with an alpha wormhole into a system with a beta wormhole (and no alpha wormhole), or vice versa.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Mahact hero&rsquo;s Benediction work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Creuss&rsquo;s alpha-beta adjacency can&rsquo;t be used.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_mentak">The Mentak Coalition</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Game effects that reroll, modify, or otherwise affect combat rolls do not affect dice rolls made when resolving the <sc>Ambush</sc> ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Mentak&rsquo;s Ambush work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It isn&rsquo;t a combat roll, so Morale Boost doesn&rsquo;t apply.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a cruiser or destroyer is removed before the Mentak player can resolve their <sc>Ambush</sc> ability, that ship cannot be one of the ships used when they do resolve their <sc>Ambush</sc> ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;roll one die for each of up to 2 cruisers or destroyers.&rdquo; A removed ship cannot roll.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Shields Holding</i> action card may be used to cancel the hit.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Direct Hit and Shields Holding work on hits from Dimensional Splicer or Yin Devotion?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes to both.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships that roll dice for <sc>Ambush</sc> still make combat rolls as normal, starting in the first round of combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Mentak&rsquo;s Ambush work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the ambushing ships still roll normally from the first round.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>While the agenda phase allows a player to resolve transactions with all other players, it does not make that player neighbors with the Mentak player.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How often can the Mentak use Pillage, and does it work in the agenda phase against everyone?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the agenda phase does not make everyone neighbors: transactions with non-neighbors are allowed then, yet Pillage still only works on the Mentak&rsquo;s actual neighbors, as the developer ruled.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Mentak player may use their <sc>Pillage</sc> ability even if the transaction does not involve trade goods.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly can Mentak use Pillage?&rdquo;: &ldquo;After each instance of a neighbor gaining trade goods or resolving a transaction.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Mentak player may use their <sc>Pillage</sc> ability against both players involved in a transaction.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly can Mentak use Pillage?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Both sides of a transaction between two neighbors can each be pillaged, in the order Mentak chooses.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Mentak player resolves a transaction with another player, they may then use their <sc>Pillage</sc> ability against that player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly can Mentak use Pillage?&rdquo;: &ldquo;After each instance of a neighbor &hellip; resolving a transaction.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player would gain multiple trade goods one at a time, the Mentak player can use their <sc>Pillage</sc> ability for each trade good.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How often can the Mentak use Pillage, and does it work in the agenda phase against everyone?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Pillage triggers separately each time a neighbor gains trade goods or resolves a transaction.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot spend trade goods after the conditions of <sc>Pillage</sc> are met and before the Mentak player resolves it.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly can Mentak use Pillage?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Nobody can dump trade goods between the trigger and Pillage.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the player with <i>Promise of Protection</i> in their play area resolves a transaction with another player, the Mentak player may still use their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Mentak_Coalition#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Pillage"><sc>Pillage</sc></a> ability on that other player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: Pillage needs a neighbor with &ldquo;no face-up Promise of Protection.&rdquo; The protection belongs only to the holder.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Promise of Protection</i> has no effect while it is in a player&rsquo;s hand.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If I hold Mentak&rsquo;s Promise of Protection without playing it &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it also doesn&rsquo;t protect you from Pillage until it is face up in your play area.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an effect specifically requires that trade goods are spent (for example, the <i>Negotiate Trade Routes</i> objective), <i>Mirror Computing</i> does not increase the value of those trade goods.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Mentak&rsquo;s Mirror Computing interact with Bribery or spend-trade-goods objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;still 1 when spent as trade goods, so Bribery and objectives that spend trade goods get no bonus.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Mentak player cannot produce a war sun unless they have their war sun technology.<sup>1,2,3,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, on the Vuil&rsquo;raith: &ldquo;by analogy with rulings on Mentak&rsquo;s Salvage Operations.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The produced unit must be paid for.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Saar&rsquo;s Chaos Mapping production work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you still pay for it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Fourth Moon</i> prevents other players&rsquo; ships from using <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> during a combat round in which it is destroyed, as <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> is used before the <i>Fourth Moon</i> is removed.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Mentak ships that Ambush still roll in the first round, and how does The Fourth Moon block Sustain Damage?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Fourth Moon stops other players&rsquo; Sustain Damage in any round it takes part in, including the round it is destroyed, because Sustain Damage is used before hits remove it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Mentak player&rsquo;s opponent chooses which promissory note they give to the Mentak player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With Diplomatic Pressure or the Mentak commander, who picks the promissory note handed over?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The target does.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Unlike capacity, the fleet pool limit applies at all times, including during combat. If placing ships with this ability causes the number of the Mentak player&rsquo;s non-fighter ships in the active system to exceed the number of command tokens in their fleet pool, they must immediately remove the excess ships, returning them to their reinforcements.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is the fleet pool limit checked, and can I bring extra ships into a fight and trim afterwards?&rdquo;: &ldquo;At all times, except for ships that are only moving through other systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If all of the Mentak player&rsquo;s ships are destroyed, they may still place ships with this ability. If they do, combat will continue.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Mentak hero work, and can it be escaped?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the combat can continue even if your other ships died.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The opponent may still retreat during combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Mentak hero work, and can it be escaped?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The opponent can limit it by retreating.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player cannot place a Valefar token on the Corsair card.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are breakthroughs technologies, and do their passive abilities work while exhausted?&rdquo;: &ldquo;They aren&rsquo;t technologies, so a breakthrough like Mentak&rsquo;s Corsair doesn&rsquo;t count as a unit upgrade&rdquo; Valefar Assimilator copies faction technologies only.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_naalu">The Naalu Collective</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Placing the Naalu 0 token is mandatory, unless the Naalu player has given it to another player with their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Naalu_Collective#Faction_Promissory_Note:~:text=Gift%20of%20Prescience"><i>Gift of Prescience</i></a> promissory note.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Must the Naalu place their 0 initiative token?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; Telepathic has no &lsquo;may&rsquo;, so the Naalu always go first in initiative unless they&rsquo;ve given the token away with Gift of Prescience.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Using <sc>Foresight</sc> is not a retreat. As such, it cannot be canceled with the <i>Intercept</i> action card.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does Naalu Foresight happen, and where can the ships go?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Intercept doesn&rsquo;t stop it, as it isn&rsquo;t a combat retreat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ground forces may be transported.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Skilled Retreat differ from a normal retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;ships can pick up ground forces from planets.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ships moving out of a gravity rift with <sc>Foresight</sc> must roll for removal.<sup>1,2,3,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do gravity rifts work &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Each ship that leaves or passes through a rift rolls, and is removed on a 1-3.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After resolving <sc>Foresight</sc>, play continues to the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does Naalu Foresight happen, and where can the ships go?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Right after the other player&rsquo;s ships move in, before Space Cannon Offense.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If the defender leaves with Skilled Retreat or Foresight &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The attacker carries on to bombardment, invasion and production as normal.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>There is no limit on the number of times <sc>Foresight</sc> may be used each round, as long as the Naalu player has command tokens in their strategy pool.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How often can the Naalu use Foresight, and what triggers it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;As often as they have strategy tokens and an eligible system to move to &mdash; there is no per-round limit.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><sc>Foresight</sc> cannot be used if another player&rsquo;s ships only move through the system that contains the Naalu player&rsquo;s ships.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How often can the Naalu use Foresight, and what triggers it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;not a ship merely passing through.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Naalu player may use <sc>Foresight</sc> to move their ships into a system that contains another player&rsquo;s structures but no ships, but cannot commit ground forces to planets in that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does Naalu Foresight happen, and where can the ships go?&rdquo;: &ldquo;They may go to any adjacent system without other players&rsquo; ships, including &hellip; one with only enemy structures, but can&rsquo;t land ground forces there.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The primary ability of the <i>Diplomacy</i> strategy card does not activate a system, so it does not trigger <i>Neuroglaive</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Diplomacy tokens, retreats or action cards like Ghost Ship or Plague count as activating a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Tokens placed by Diplomacy or a retreat and cards aimed at a system don&rsquo;t activate it, so they don&rsquo;t trigger Space Cannon Offense, Support for the Throne returns or other &lsquo;activate&rsquo; effects.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The text of the <i>Matriarch</i> is changed to: &ldquo;During an invasion in this system, you may commit fighters to planets as if they were ground forces. When combat ends, return those units to the space area.&rdquo;<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Naalu fighters fighting with the Matriarch win a ground combat or take control of a planet?&rdquo;: &ldquo;per the errata they return to space when combat ends.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The committed fighters are returned to the space area when combat ends, and thus will not be on the planet for the <b>Establish Control</b> step. If the Naalu player does not commit at least one ground force to a planet and have it survive the combat, they will be unable to gain control of that planet, and the ground combat will end in a draw.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Naalu fighters fighting with the Matriarch win a ground combat or take control of a planet?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You need actual ground forces to take control.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any structures on the planet will not be destroyed if the Naalu player committed no ground forces.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens to space docks and PDS when an enemy takes the planet?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Fighters fighting via the Naalu flagship don&rsquo;t destroy them, since they aren&rsquo;t on the planet afterwards.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Dacxive Animators</i> technology will not allow the Naalu player to gain control of a planet without committing a ground force.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Naalu fighters fighting with the Matriarch win a ground combat or take control of a planet?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If only fighters remain, the combat is a draw, Dacxive Animators doesn&rsquo;t trigger.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Fighters committed to a planet use their own combat value, including that of any fighter unit upgrade.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What combat value do Naalu fighters use in ground combat &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Their own fighter combat value, upgrades included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the chosen player is not the Naalu player, it is not considered their turn for game effects.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the tactical action granted by the Codex Naalu agent use a token &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The tactical action happens during the Naalu player&rsquo;s turn.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When producing units, the Naalu player must produce all the units they intend to produce before they may unlock M&rsquo;aban. M&rsquo;aban cannot be applied to the instance of producing the units that unlocks it.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I unlock the Naalu commander partway through a production and use it for the rest?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; production is one step, so the unlock condition is only met after it finishes; use the commander from your next production.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>For example, before production, the Naalu player has ten fighters on the game board. They pay two resources during production. They cannot produce two fighters, unlock M&rsquo;aban, then produce three more fighters. Instead, they would produce all four fighters, and then unlock M&rsquo;aban.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I unlock the Naalu commander partway through a production and use it for the rest?&rdquo;: &ldquo;production is one step, so the unlock condition is only met after it finishes&rdquo; the example follows directly from this rule.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Naalu player may choose to produce one fighter for one resource. If they do, they may produce one additional fighter.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Yin and Naalu commanders&rsquo; extra infantry and fighters work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;When producing those units you may produce one extra for its cost, outside your Production limit.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Naalu player cannot look at the agenda deck while a player is resolving the primary ability of the Politics strategy card.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;A FAQ answer quoted by a player says it can&rsquo;t &hellip; interrupt the Politics primary.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When the <i>Covert Legislation</i> agenda is revealed during the agenda phase, the Naalu player cannot look at the hidden agenda.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;it can&rsquo;t peek at the card Covert Legislation draws.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each other player chooses which promissory note they give the Naalu player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are my unplayed promissory notes in my hand &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;With the Naalu hero, each player chooses which note to give.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other players do not roll any anti&ndash;fighter barrage dice in the system. Effects that trigger from <sc>Anti&ndash;Fighter Barrage</sc>, such as the <a href="/F_argent">Argent</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Argent_Flight#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Raid%20Formation"><sc>Raid Formation</sc></a> faction ability, do not trigger.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Naalu Omega mech stop opponents rolling Anti-Fighter Barrage at all?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; other players can&rsquo;t use barrage in that system, so no dice are rolled and effects tied to it, like Argent&rsquo;s, don&rsquo;t trigger.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Naalu player may only give promissory notes from their hand; they cannot give promissory notes that are in their play area.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: unplayed notes &ldquo;are your hand until placed face up in a play area.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_naaz_rokha">The Naaz&ndash;Rokha Alliance</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li><sc>Distant Suns</sc> may be used if the planet contains more than one of the Naaz&ndash;Rokha player&rsquo;s mechs.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Naaz-Rokha&rsquo;s Distant Suns work with more than one mech on the planet?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; &lsquo;contains 1 of your mechs&rsquo; means at least one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Naaz&ndash;Rokha player has multiple Eidolon on the planet they explore, they will draw only one additional exploration card.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Naaz-Rokha&rsquo;s Distant Suns work with more than one mech on the planet?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; &lsquo;contains 1 of your mechs&rsquo; means at least one.&rdquo; The ability triggers once, so one additional card.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The planet is not readied until after the planet is explored.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does exploration work?&rdquo;: exploring &ldquo;happens before &lsquo;after you gain control&rsquo; effects like Integrated Economy or Pre-Fab Arcologies.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Naaz&ndash;Rokha player may choose to perform the secondary ability of zero, one or two strategy cards.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which strategy cards can the Winnu and Naaz-Rokha heroes use, and do players pay for secondaries?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Naaz-Rokha hero resolves the secondaries of up to two readied or unchosen cards&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Naaz&ndash;Rokha player had an Eidolon in the space area of the active system, they must mandatorily use its ability to convert it to a Z&ndash;Grav Eidolon.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is flipping Naaz-Rokha mechs to ships mandatory &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Flipping is mandatory, as nothing says &lsquo;may&rsquo;, but only for mechs already in the space area when combat starts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_nekro">The Nekro Virus</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The Nekro player makes their prediction during the same timing window as riders, after any &ldquo;when an agenda is revealed&rdquo; abilities, such as the <i>Veto</i> action card or the <a href="/F_xxcha">Xxcha</a> player&rsquo;s Quash faction ability. They must predict an outcome; abstaining is not an outcome.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does the Nekro make its Galactic Threat prediction, and what can it predict?&rdquo;: &ldquo;In the same window as riders: after &lsquo;when revealed&rsquo; effects such as Veto or Quash, before anyone votes. It predicts an outcome; abstaining isn&rsquo;t one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Nekro player may still play rider action cards.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does the Nekro make its Galactic Threat prediction &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Nekro may also play riders on the same or different outcomes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the speaker breaks a tie without casting any votes, then the speaker&rsquo;s technologies are ineligible to be gained.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Galactic Threat gain a technology if nobody voted for the predicted outcome?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; the Nekro gains a technology from a player who voted for that outcome. If it won through the speaker&rsquo;s tie-break &hellip; with no player voting for it, there is nobody to take from.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If no player casts votes, then no technology can be gained.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Galactic Threat gain a technology if nobody voted for the predicted outcome?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; the Nekro gains a technology from a player who voted for that outcome. If it won through the speaker&rsquo;s tie-break &hellip; with no player voting for it, there is nobody to take from.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any method that destroys a unit during combat will trigger <sc>Technological Singularity</sc>, including an anti&ndash;fighter barrage roll, the <i>Direct Hit</i> action card, the <a href="/F_creuss">Creuss</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Dimensional%20Splicer"><i>Dimensional Splicer</i></a> faction technology, etc.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which kills trigger Nekro&rsquo;s Technological Singularity?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Anti-Fighter Barrage is part of combat and counts.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When the Nekro Virus gain a technology, does the other player lose it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It triggers when any opponent&rsquo;s unit is destroyed in combat, even one the Yin sacrifice with Devotion or one killed by Assault Cannon or Anti-Fighter Barrage.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>This includes any method used by the Nekro player&rsquo;s opponent that destroys their own units, usually as an ability cost.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When the Nekro Virus gain a technology, does the other player lose it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;even one the Yin sacrifice with Devotion.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a unit is removed, but not destroyed, that unit will not trigger the <sc>Technological Singularity</sc> ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Infantry II trigger on infantry removed by the Jol-Nar agent?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; removing isn&rsquo;t destroying.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Space cannon and bombardment rolls happen outside of combat. Any hits produced cannot be used to trigger the <sc>Technological Singularity</sc> ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which kills trigger Nekro&rsquo;s Technological Singularity?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Kills from Space Cannon, Bombardment, or structures destroyed when control is established don&rsquo;t count, since those happen outside combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Nekro player chooses to gain a unit upgrade technology, that upgrade will be in effect from that point in the combat onwards.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When the Nekro Virus gain a technology, does the other player lose it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it works immediately, even for the rest of that combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Destroying an opponent&rsquo;s unit during the <b>Anti&ndash;Fighter Barrage</b> step, and gaining a unit upgrade technology will allow units of that type to use the abilities and attributes of the upgrade for the combat rolls of the same round.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: the gained technology &ldquo;works immediately, even for the rest of that combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Destroying an opponent&rsquo;s unit at the start of a combat, such as with the ability of the <i>Assault Cannon</i> technology, and gaining a unit upgrade technology with an <sc>Anti&ndash;Fighter Barrage</sc> ability will allow units of that type to use that ability during the <b>Anti&ndash;Fighter Barrage</b> step of the first round of combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: the gained technology &ldquo;works immediately, even for the rest of that combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Nekro player destroys a unit, and gains a technology with an ability that is used during the same timing window, they may use that ability immediately.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When the Nekro Virus gain a technology, does the other player lose it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it works immediately, even for the rest of that combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Nekro player cannot use <i>The Prophet&rsquo;s Tears</i> relic to draw an action card when they gain a technology using this ability.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Nekro Virus use the Prophet&rsquo;s Tears relic?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; it triggers when you research a technology, which the Nekro never do.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Nekro player may still resolve the secondary ability of the <i>Technology</i> strategy card, which will instead gain them command tokens. They must still spend a command token from their strategy pool and four resources to do so.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Nekro Virus pay normally to use the Technology secondary?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; like anyone they spend a strategy token and 4 resources, and because they can&rsquo;t research, Propagation gives them 3 command tokens instead.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Nekro player owns <i>The Prophet&rsquo;s Tears</i> relic, they may draw an action card whenever they would research a technology.<sup>4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Unverified</b>: <sup>2</sup> Conflicts: RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Nekro Virus use the Prophet&rsquo;s Tears relic?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; it triggers when you research a technology, which the Nekro never do, so it does nothing for them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <i>Anti&ndash;Intellectual Revolution</i> law is in play, the Nekro player will not destroy a ship when they would research a technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Anti-Intellectual Revolution work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Nekro are unaffected, since Propagation gains tokens instead of researching.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Nekro player may play the <i>Reveal Prototype</i> action card, gaining three command tokens instead of researching, but only if there is a unit upgrade technology the card could research.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Nekro Virus play Reveal Prototype?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes, gaining 3 command tokens instead of the technology, but only if there&rsquo;s a valid unit upgrade the card could research.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When copying another technology, each <i>Valefar Assimilator</i> may later be used to copy a different faction technology.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can the Nekro copy with Valefar Assimilator?&rdquo;: &ldquo;two at a time, one per token; to copy a third you move a token.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Nekro player would copy a faction&ndash;specific unit upgrade, and they are already copying a faction&ndash;specific unit upgrade of the same type, they must use the same Valefar Assimilator token; they lose the previous faction unit upgrade.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens to the Nekro&rsquo;s own unit upgrades &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A second faction upgrade for the same unit replaces the first, using the same token.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Nekro player cannot copy faction&ndash;specific units that have not been upgraded.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can the Nekro copy with Valefar Assimilator?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Units printed on faction sheets, like Muaat&rsquo;s Prototype War Sun I or flagships, aren&rsquo;t technologies and can&rsquo;t be copied.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Nekro player has a generic unit upgrade, and gains a faction&ndash;specific unit upgrade, then the faction&ndash;specific version will be in effect. If the faction&ndash;specific unit upgrade is later lost, they revert to the generic unit upgrade.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;The copied faction upgrade covers your generic one, which you keep; only one is in effect.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Owning two unit upgrades for the same type of unit in this manner only counts as one unit upgrade toward the <i>Develop Weaponry</i> and <i>Revolutionize Warfare</i> objectives.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do technologies the Nekro copy with Valefar Assimilator count for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;But when a copied faction upgrade covers a generic one, like Dreadnought II, only one of the two counts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Nekro player copies the <a href="/F_nomad">Nomad</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Nomad#Flagship:~:text=Memoria"><i>Memoria II</i></a> flagship unit upgrade technology, its attributes and abilities replace those of <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Nekro_Virus#Flagship:~:text=The%20Alastor"><i>The Alastor</i></a>.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can the Nekro copy with Valefar Assimilator?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Units printed on faction sheets, like Muaat&rsquo;s Prototype War Sun I or flagships, aren&rsquo;t technologies and can&rsquo;t be copied (the Nomad&rsquo;s Memoria II card is the exception).&rdquo; Memoria II is the only copyable flagship upgrade.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>For the <i>Adapt New Strategies</i> objective, a <i>Valefar Assimilator</i> technology will count if and only if it is currently copying another technology.<sup>5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do technologies the Nekro copy with Valefar Assimilator count for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; a copied technology counts with its colour and type.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Muaat player&rsquo;s <i>Magmus Reactor</i> is lost, the Nekro player&rsquo;s units may remain in any supernovae they are currently in, but cannot move additional units into or through.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens when the Nekro move a Valefar token off a technology their units depend on?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Ships already in a supernova after losing Magmus Reactor are fine.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_winnu">Winnu</a> player&rsquo;s flagship, the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Winnu#Flagship:~:text=Salai%20Sai%20Corian"><i>Salai Sai Corian</i></a>, will roll one dice for each infantry the Nekro player has participating in a space combat via the effect of <i>The Alastor</i>.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Nekro ground forces fighting via the flagship count for other effects?&rdquo;: &ldquo;they count as non-fighter ships for things like the Winnu flagship&rsquo;s extra dice (per the LRR FAQ).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>This applies only to copying another player&rsquo;s flagship unit upgrade technology, currently only the <a href="/F_nomad">Nomad</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Nomad#Flagship:~:text=Memoria"><i>Memoria II</i></a>, with a <i>Valefar Assimilator</i> X or Y token. Copying a flagship with <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Nekro_Virus#Breakthrough:~:text=Valefar%20Assimilator%20Z"><i>Valefar Assimilator Z</i></a> does not replace the abilities of <i>The Alastor</i>.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can the Nekro copy with Valefar Assimilator?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Units printed on faction sheets, like Muaat&rsquo;s Prototype War Sun I or flagships, aren&rsquo;t technologies and can&rsquo;t be copied (the Nomad&rsquo;s Memoria II card is the exception).&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With the Nekro breakthrough, can the flagship copy several other flagships, and what does it copy?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you may put an Assimilator Z token on their faction sheet instead, and your flagship gains that flagship&rsquo;s text abilities.&rdquo; Gains, in addition to its own.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ground forces participating in a space combat due to the effect of <i>The Alastor</i> are units that can be transported, so they do not count toward the Nekro player&rsquo;s fleet pool limit.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do ground forces fighting with the Nekro Virus flagship work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;They take part in the space combat as if they were ships.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When the Nekro player gains their third technology and unlocks Nekro Acidos, they cannot apply Nekro Acidos to that instance of gaining a technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Nekro commander draw an action card for the very technology that unlocks it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; unlock conditions are checked only after the current effect fully resolves.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Unit upgrades do not have a color. The Nekro player cannot gain a unit upgrade with <i>Polymorphic Algorithm</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What counts for technology-colour objectives like Diversify Research and Master the Sciences?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Unit upgrades have no colour&rdquo; unit upgrades are colorless; Polymorphic Algorithm gain is by color.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Placing a Valefar Assimilator Z token is not gaining a technology. It does not trigger the ability of Nekro Acidos, the Nekro player&rsquo;s commander.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With the Nekro breakthrough, can the flagship copy several other flagships, and what does it copy?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It isn&rsquo;t a technology, so the Nekro commander doesn&rsquo;t trigger.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Nekro player&rsquo;s flagship gains only the text abilities of a copied flagship. Unit abilities, such as <sc>Deploy</sc>, <sc>Space Cannon</sc> or <sc>Sustain Damage</sc>, are not copied.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With the Nekro breakthrough, can the flagship copy several other flagships, and what does it copy?&rdquo;: &ldquo;your flagship gains that flagship&rsquo;s text abilities &hellip; Unit abilities like Deploy, Space Cannon or Sustain Damage aren&rsquo;t text abilities, so copying flagships that rely on them gains nothing.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_nomad">The Nomad</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The Nomad player may only predict agenda outcomes via game effects that allow them to do so, e.g. rider action cards.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When can a player make a prediction for the Nomad&rsquo;s Future Sight?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only when an ability lets you predict, usually a rider.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Intimidate Council</i> objective requires the system to be adjacent to Mecatol Rex. As such, the Nomad player cannot use the <i>Memoria</i>&rsquo;s ability to score this objective. A similar prohibition applies to the <i>Learn the Secrets of the Cosmos</i> and <i>Threaten Enemies</i> objectives.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do ships in Mecatol Rex&rsquo;s system, or in an anomaly, count as adjacent to it for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Intimidate Council needs ships in two different systems next to Mecatol Rex.&rdquo; The objective needs the systems to be adjacent, not the ships; the Memoria&rsquo;s ability makes only the ship adjacent to mech systems.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a player gains trade goods that were on a strategy card, they do not gain them from the supply.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which trade good gains count for the Nomad agent Artuno?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Trade goods gained from the supply, from action cards, agendas, the Trade card or exploration. Converting commodities doesn&rsquo;t count, and as written neither do trade goods taken off strategy cards.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When the Nomad player gains trade goods that were on Artuno the Betrayer, they do not gain them from the supply.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: trade goods taken from a card are not counted.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Converting commodities to trade goods is not gaining trade goods from the supply, and does not trigger Artuno the Betrayer.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which trade good gains count for the Nomad agent Artuno?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Trade goods gained from the supply, from action cards, agendas, the Trade card or exploration. Converting commodities doesn&rsquo;t count, and as written neither do trade goods taken off strategy cards.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Thundarian may be used during a combat that the Nomad player is not participating in.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Nomad agent be used in combats the Nomad aren&rsquo;t in?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it has no participation requirement.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Nomad#Flagship:~:text=Memoria"><i>Memoria</i></a> may transport ground forces from systems containing one of the Nomad player&rsquo;s command tokens while the ability of Ahk&ndash;Syl Siven is active.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With the Nomad hero active, can the Memoria pick up units from systems with Nomad command tokens?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; the hero lets the flagship and the units it transports leave command-token systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_ral_nel">The Ral Nel Consortium</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Each Linkship II rolls one space cannon die, regardless of how many PDS are in its system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are there quick answers for &hellip; Ral Nel Linkships?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Each Linkship II rolls one Space Cannon die however many PDS share its space.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Ral Nel player plays the <i>Skilled Retreat</i> action card, they cannot resolve the ability of the <i>Last Dispatch</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Skilled Retreat differ from a normal retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It isn&rsquo;t a retreat in rules terms but a move.&rdquo; The Last Dispatch triggers &ldquo;When this unit retreats.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Skilled Retreat</i> is not a retreat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Skilled Retreat differ from a normal retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It isn&rsquo;t a retreat in rules terms but a move.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_norr">Sardakk N&rsquo;orr</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li><sc>Unrelenting</sc> does not affect anti&ndash;fighter barrage, bombardment or space cannon rolls.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Morale Boost, faction modifiers and rerolls affect Anti-Fighter Barrage, Space Cannon, Bombardment or Ambush?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; only rolls made against a unit&rsquo;s combat value are combat rolls.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When resolving the ability of an Exotrireme II, the N&rsquo;orr player chooses which ships are destroyed.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Sustain Damage save a ship from Exotrireme II &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;For Exotrireme II, the Sardakk player chooses which ships (up to 2) are destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Effects that cancel hits, such as the <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability, cannot be used to prevent ships from being destroyed.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;No &mdash; Sustain Damage only cancels hits, and these effects destroy units without producing hits.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player retreats, the N&rsquo;orr player will be unable to use the ability of an Exotrireme II to destroy an opponent&rsquo;s ship.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly can Exotrireme II use its ability?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it can&rsquo;t be used if that Exotrireme was destroyed or a retreat was announced.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the N&rsquo;orr player is in a combat with the <a href="/F_yin">Yin</a> player, then the two players will alternate when resolving end of combat round abilities, starting with the attacker.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When both players have end-of-round abilities, like Yin&rsquo;s Devotion and Sardakk&rsquo;s Exotrireme II, who goes first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The active player resolves one first, then the players alternate one ability at a time.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An Exotrireme II that used its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability during a combat round may still be destroyed to resolve its ability at the end of that round.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly can Exotrireme II use its ability?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A dreadnought can use Sustain Damage that round and still be sacrificed afterwards.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>This ability allows the N&rsquo;orr player to commit ground forces to a planet even if they did not move any ships into the active system.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Sardakk commander commit ground forces from adjacent systems without moving any ships in &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; the invasion step happens in every tactical action, so you can activate the system, move nothing, and commit ground forces from adjacent planets.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The N&rsquo;orr player may only commit ground forces as the active player.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Sardakk commander be used when defending?&rdquo;: &ldquo;only the active player commits ground forces&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Parley</i> action card will place the committed ground forces in the space area of the active system. If the N&rsquo;orr player does not have enough capacity in the space area for the extra ground forces, including zero capacity, they will have to remove fighters and ground forces to meet capacity limits.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens to committed ground forces when Parley is played?&rdquo;: &ldquo;They return to the space area rather than being destroyed, within capacity.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Parley</i> action card will place the committed ground forces in the space area of the active system. As the N&rsquo;orr player will not have enough capacity in the space area for their ground forces, they will have to remove all ground forces from that planet, that are now in the space area of that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens to committed ground forces when Parley is played?&rdquo;: &ldquo;They return to the space area rather than being destroyed, within capacity.&rdquo; The N&rsquo;orr player has no capacity in the space area, so all of them are removed.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_ul">The Titans of Ul</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A planet cannot contain more than one sleeper token.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which side of the sleeper token is used &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A planet holds one sleeper.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Titans put more than one sleeper on a planet with Terragenesis &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a planet holds at most one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Ul player may only move a sleeper token with <sc>Terragenesis</sc> once all of their sleeper tokens are on the game board.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which side of the sleeper token is used, when does Terragenesis move one, and can Awaken work with no PDS left?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Terragenesis moves one only once all yours are on the board.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Only sleeper tokens present in the system when it is activated may be replaced with PDS.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When can the Titans use Awaken, and can Ceasefire or Nullification Field stop it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Right after activating a system that already held your sleeper token.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A sleeper token that is placed during the <b>Activation</b> step, most commonly by using the <i>Scanlink Drone Network</i> technology and the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Titans_of_Ul#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Terragenesis"><sc>Terragenesis</sc></a> ability, cannot be replaced via the <sc>Awaken</sc> ability during the same tactical action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When can the Titans use Awaken, and can Ceasefire or Nullification Field stop it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a sleeper placed later in that activation, say by Scanlink exploration, can&rsquo;t be awakened then.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Ul player activates a system containing their sleeper tokens and the <a href="/F_xxcha">Xxcha</a> player&rsquo;s ships, the Ul player, as the active player, may resolve <sc>Awaken</sc> before the Xxcha player resolves the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Xxcha_Kingdom#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Nullification%20Field"><i>Nullification Field</i></a> technology. Any resulting ground combat still occurs.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When can the Titans use Awaken, and can Ceasefire or Nullification Field stop it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Awaken and Nullification Field share the after-activation window, and the active Titans go first, so they can awaken before their turn is ended; the resulting ground combat still happens.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A sleeper token is not removed when another player gains control of the planet it is on. The Ul player may still replace it using <sc>Awaken</sc>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is the Titans&rsquo; Hel Titan a ground force, and does a sleeper stay when another player takes the planet?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A sleeper token is only removed by Awaken or by the planet being destroyed, so it stays on a captured planet and can be awakened later.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Ul player retreats into a system containing their sleeper token, <sc>Awaken</sc> is not triggered, as the system is not activated.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If the Titans retreat into a system with a sleeper, does it awaken?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a retreat places a command token but doesn&rsquo;t activate the system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If another player plays the Ul player&rsquo;s <i>Ceasefire</i> promissory note, the Ul player may still place units into the active system using their <sc>Awaken</sc> ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When can the Titans use Awaken, and can Ceasefire or Nullification Field stop it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Ceasefire only stops movement, so Awaken still works.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Once played, <i>Terraform</i> is never returned to the Ul player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Titans play their own Terraform note, or reuse it after it&rsquo;s attached?&rdquo;: &ldquo;once attached it can&rsquo;t be played again or traded.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a planet has multiple traits, the player exploring the planet chooses which of the corresponding exploration decks to draw from.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which exploration deck do I draw from when a planet has more than one trait?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You choose one of the matching trait decks and draw a single card from it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When multiple units use their <sc>Production</sc> ability, the <sc>Production</sc> value is pooled. As such, if the Ul player has two Hel&ndash;Titans in a system, they may produce two infantry or two fighters for one resource, rather than two resources.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Production limit the number of units or their total cost?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The number of units.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Effects that remove ground forces, and then place them on the game board without transporting them, such as the <i>Decoy Operation</i> action card or the <i>Transit Diodes</i> technology, may be used on a Hel&ndash;Titan.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Transit Diodes move Titans Hel-Titans or ordinary PDS?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Hel-Titans yes: they are ground forces, and Transit Diodes removes and places units rather than transporting them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A Hel&ndash;Titan is a structure, and so cannot be targeted by the <a href="/F_vuilraith">Vuil&rsquo;raith</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Vuil&#x27;Raith_Cabal#:~:text=Vortex"><i>Vortex</i></a> faction technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is a Hel-Titan a valid target for effects on non-structures &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It is a structure as well as a ground force, so the Vuil&rsquo;raith vortex, which needs a non-structure, can&rsquo;t take it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Only PDS present in the system when it is activated may be replaced with the <i>Ouranos</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Titans deploy their flagship for free &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the PDS must be there when you activate.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A PDS that is placed during the activation step, most commonly by using the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Titans_of_Ul#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Awaken"><sc>Awaken</sc></a> ability, cannot be replaced with the <i>Ouranos</i> during the same tactical action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;one placed by Awaken during that activation doesn&rsquo;t qualify.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Ul player may use the <sc>Deploy</sc> ability of a Hecatoncheires when placing a PDS with the <i>Construction</i> strategy card, including for each PDS placed with the primary ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Titans deploy their mech instead of placing a PDS with Construction?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; Construction places PDS, so the Titans&rsquo; deploy works there, even twice with the primary.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A planet controlled by another player may contain at most one sleeper token.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;a planet holds at most one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_jol_nar">The Universities of Jol&ndash;Nar</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The <sc>Fragile</sc> ability does not affect anti&ndash;fighter barrage, bombardment or space cannon rolls.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Morale Boost, faction modifiers and rerolls affect Anti-Fighter Barrage, Space Cannon, Bombardment or Ambush?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; only rolls made against a unit&rsquo;s combat value are combat rolls.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When using Brilliant, the Jol&ndash;Nar player spends only the command token from their strategy pool, not the four resources. They then resolve the primary ability of the <i>Technology</i> strategy card, and may research an additional technology by spending six resources.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When Jol-Nar uses Brilliant to resolve the Technology primary as a secondary, do they need another token for the second tech?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; they pay one strategy token, then resolve the full primary: research one technology and optionally spend 6 resources for a second.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When the Jol&ndash;Nar player uses <sc>Brilliant</sc>, they may research a second technology by spending six resources, without spending an additional command token.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;No &mdash; they pay one strategy token, then resolve the full primary.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Research Agreement</i> may be triggered when the Jol&ndash;Nar player researches a technology outside of the <i>Technology</i> strategy card. For example, it may be triggered by the <i>Divert Funding</i> action card, the <i>Focused Research</i> action card, the <i>Reveal Prototype</i> action card, the <i>Technology Rider</i> action card, the <i>Ixthian Artifact</i> agenda, the <i>Wormhole Research</i> agenda, or the <i>Enigmatic Device</i> exploration card.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Jol-Nar&rsquo;s Research Agreement work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;When Jol-Nar researches a non-faction technology, by any means, while you hold the note, both of you end up with it &hellip; It doesn&rsquo;t trigger when Jol-Nar merely gains a tech.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Research Agreement</i> cannot be triggered by a game effect that instructs the Jol&ndash;Nar player to directly gain a technology, without researching. For example, it cannot be triggered by the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Embers_of_Muaat#Faction_Promissory_Note:~:text=Fires%20of%20the%20Gashlai"><i>Fires of the Gashlai</i></a> <a href="/F_muaat">Muaat</a> faction promissory note, the <i>Plagiarize</i> action card, the <i>Research Grant Reallocation</i> agenda, or the <i>Maw of Worlds</i> relic.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Jol-Nar&rsquo;s Research Agreement work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;When Jol-Nar researches a non-faction technology, by any means, while you hold the note, both of you end up with it &hellip; It doesn&rsquo;t trigger when Jol-Nar merely gains a tech.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Spatial Conduit Cylinder</i> is exhausted after the system is activated, before movement; the active system must already contain one of the Jol&ndash;Nar player&rsquo;s units.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Jol-Nar&rsquo;s Spatial Conduit Cylinder work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It is exhausted right after activation, before movement, so the system must already contain your units.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Jol&ndash;Nar player&rsquo;s PDS II units in systems made adjacent by <i>Spatial Conduit Cylinder</i> may use their <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability against the active system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;systems with your units count as adjacent to it for that action, so all your PDS II there can fire into it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each result of 9 or 10 produces three hits in total: its normal hit and two additional hits.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How many hits can the Jol-Nar flagship produce?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Each die showing 9 or 10 produces its normal hit plus 2 more.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Jol&ndash;Nar player has the Infantry II unit upgrade, any of their infantry removed with Doctor Sucaban will not roll for resurrection.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Infantry II trigger on infantry removed by the Jol-Nar agent?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; removing isn&rsquo;t destroying.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ta Zern and Agnlan Oln work identically. Thunder&rsquo;s Edge replaces Ta Zern with Agnlan Oln because the <a href="/F_deepwrought">Deepwrought</a> player&rsquo;s hero is also named Ta Zern.<sup>2,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do I need to swap Ta Zern for Agnlan Oln when combining Thunder&rsquo;s Edge with Prophecy of Kings?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only for theme and clarity &mdash; the two cards work identically. Ta Zern now belongs to the Deepwrought, and the swap avoids two components sharing a name.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_vuilraith">The Vuil&rsquo;raith Cabal</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The Vuil&rsquo;raith player cannot capture the <a href="/F_ul">Ul</a> player&rsquo;s Hel Titan PDS.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is a Hel-Titan a valid target for effects on non-structures, and can it produce?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It is a structure as well as a ground force, so the Vuil&rsquo;raith vortex, which needs a non-structure, can&rsquo;t take it.&rdquo; Devour also captures only non-structure units.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Units destroyed by <sc>Space Cannon</sc> or <sc>Bombardment</sc> rolls are not destroyed during combat, and are not captured by <sc>Devour</sc>. Units destroyed by <sc>Anti&ndash;Fighter Barrage</sc> are destroyed during combat, and are captured.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Vuil&rsquo;raith Devour ability capture units destroyed by Space Cannon or Bombardment?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; those happen outside space and ground combat, so Devour doesn&rsquo;t see them; Anti-Fighter Barrage is part of combat and counts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Vuil&rsquo;raith player produces multiple units, their <sc>Amalgamation</sc> ability may be applied to each.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What are captured units good for, and how do Amalgamation and Riftmeld use them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Each captured unit or token gives one unit.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><sc>Amalgamation</sc> does not change the cost of the produced units, only the resources spent.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What are captured units good for, and how do Amalgamation and Riftmeld use them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;its printed cost still counts toward limits like Integrated Economy&rsquo;s.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Vuil&rsquo;raith player uses the ability of the <i>Integrated Economy</i> technology, <sc>Amalgamation</sc> will have no effect on the limit to the units they may build.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What are captured units good for, and how do Amalgamation and Riftmeld use them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;its printed cost still counts toward limits like Integrated Economy&rsquo;s.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Vuil&rsquo;raith player produces one fighter or infantry without spending resources for each captured fighter or infantry they spend.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What are captured units good for, and how do Amalgamation and Riftmeld use them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Each captured unit or token gives one unit, even fighters or infantry.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A captured faction&ndash;specific unit counts as a unit of its base type; for example, a captured <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Embers_of_Muaat#War_Sun:~:text=Prototype%20War%20Sun">Prototype War Sun</a> counts as a war sun.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What are captured units good for, and how do Amalgamation and Riftmeld use them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Any captured unit of the base type works, a Prototype War Sun counting as a war sun.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Vuil&rsquo;raith player cannot produce a war sun using <sc>Amalgamation</sc> unless they own their war sun unit upgrade technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Vuil&rsquo;raith produce a captured war sun without the War Sun technology?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No, by analogy with rulings on Mentak&rsquo;s Salvage Operations and Nekro war suns.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The returned captured unit may belong to any player, regardless of whether that player owns the corresponding unit upgrade.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With Riftmeld, must the captured unit belong to a player who has the upgrade?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; any captured unit of the matching type lets you ignore that upgrade&rsquo;s prerequisites.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each gravity rift will still provide its usual +1 to each ship&rsquo;s move value.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do gravity rifts work &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A ship whose path moves out of or through a rift gets +1 move for that path, one per rift.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Crucible</i> will provide an additional +1 to each ship&rsquo;s move value, which will not increase if that ship moves through two or more gravity rifts.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do the Crucible note&rsquo;s +1 and a gravity rift&rsquo;s +1 stack, and can I use several end-of-turn abilities?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes to both: movement bonuses stack&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Vortex</i> cannot target the <a href="/F_ul">Ul</a> player&rsquo;s Hel Titan, as it is a structure.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is a Hel-Titan a valid target for effects on non-structures, and can it produce?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It is a structure as well as a ground force, so the Vuil&rsquo;raith vortex, which needs a non-structure, can&rsquo;t take it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system containing a Dimensional Tear is an anomaly.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Vuil&rsquo;raith ships roll for Cormund&rsquo;s gravity rift if their Dimensional Tear dock is there, and do two tears stack?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it is simply a gravity rift, and counts as an anomaly for objectives.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>This system qualifies for the <i>Make History</i> and similar such objectives.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do the Vuil&rsquo;raith roll for ordinary gravity rifts?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Those dock systems are anomalies, which counts for objectives like Making History.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Multiple Dimensional Tears in a single system have no additional effects.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Vuil&rsquo;raith ships roll for Cormund&rsquo;s gravity rift if their Dimensional Tear dock is there, and do two tears stack?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Two tears in one system don&rsquo;t stack the bonus either.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a Dimensional Tear is in a system that is normally a gravity rift (e.g. the Cormund system), the Vuil&rsquo;raith player will not have to roll for removal when moving out of or through that system. There are no additional effects.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Vuil&rsquo;raith ships roll for Cormund&rsquo;s gravity rift if their Dimensional Tear dock is there, and do two tears stack?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No roll: a system with more than one rift counts as one gravity rift, and Dimensional Tear says your ships don&rsquo;t roll for that system&rsquo;s rift.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When <i>The Terror Between</i> is destroyed, it still captures units that were destroyed at the same time.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does the Vuil&rsquo;raith flagship capture?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the Cabal flagship is destroyed too but still captures everything else destroyed there.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If ships are destroyed outside of combat, those ships will still be captured by <i>The Terror Between</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does the Vuil&rsquo;raith flagship capture?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Every other non-structure unit destroyed in its system, the Cabal&rsquo;s own included, even outside combat: Bombardment, Space Cannon, action cards, agendas.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Vuil&rsquo;raith player chooses to capture a fighter or infantry, they will capture one, not two.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do you capture fighters and infantry?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A captured fighter or infantry goes back to its owner&rsquo;s reinforcements &hellip; and the captor places a matching token on their faction sheet instead.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each non&ndash;fighter ship affected will roll only once, regardless of how many Dimensional Tears it is adjacent to.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Vuil&rsquo;raith hero make a ship roll once per adjacent dimensional tear?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; each non-fighter ship in or next to a dimensional tear rolls once, however many tears it touches.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_winnu">The Winnu</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Placing the structure will occur after the <b>Production</b> step. If the Winnu player places a Space Dock, they cannot produce out of it during the same action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If Winnu place a mech with Hope&rsquo;s End &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a dock placed by Winnu&rsquo;s Reclamation comes after the tactical action, too late to produce in it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If either strategy card had trade goods on it at the start of the strategy phase, those trade goods remain with the player that originally chose the strategy card.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Hacan&rsquo;s Quantum Datahub Node swap strategy cards?&rdquo;: &ldquo;trade goods were already collected when the cards were picked.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Changing a planet&rsquo;s resource value will affect the <sc>Production</sc> value of a Space Dock on that planet.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How many units can a space dock produce?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A Space Dock I has Production equal to its planet&rsquo;s resource value plus 2.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>While <i>Lazax Gate Folding</i> is exhausted, the wormhole passive effect still applies if its conditions are met.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Winnu use Lazax Gate Folding&rsquo;s action without Mecatol Rex, and does the wormhole part work while exhausted?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The passive part &hellip; doesn&rsquo;t need the card ready, so it works while exhausted.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The action ability of <i>Lazax Gate Folding</i> cannot be used if the Winnu player does not control Mecatol Rex.<sup>2,3,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;The action needs you to control Mecatol Rex, so it can&rsquo;t be used as a stall.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Winnu player&rsquo;s opponent has only fighters participating in the combat, the <i>Salai Sai Corian</i> will roll no dice.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can each Winnu flagship die score a hit?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it rolls one die per enemy non-fighter ship.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During a combat against the <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player in a system containing each player&rsquo;s flagships, the <i>Salai Sai Corian</i> will count the Nekro player&rsquo;s infantry participating in the combat when determining how many dice to roll.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Nekro ground forces fighting via the flagship count for other effects?&rdquo;: &ldquo;they count as non-fighter ships for things like the Winnu flagship&rsquo;s extra dice.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player chosen to perform the secondary ability must still spend a command token from their strategy pool to do so, except for the <i>Leadership</i> strategy card.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a strategy card&rsquo;s primary ability cost a command token, and does the Leadership secondary?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Secondaries cost a strategy pool token, except Leadership&rsquo;s.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Winnu#Faction_Promissory_Note:~:text=Acquiescence"><i>Acquiescence &Omega;</i></a> cannot be triggered by this ability, as using <i>Imperial Seal</i> is a component action, not a strategic action.<sup>2,10</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Coup d&rsquo;Etat stop a primary ability resolved through the Winnu hero or Overrule?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; those are component actions that resolve a primary, not strategic actions, and Coup d&rsquo;Etat only cancels strategic actions.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Winnu player places a Reclaimer with the <i>Imperial Arms Vault</i> ability of <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Hope&#x27;s_End">Hope&rsquo;s End</a>, it cannot use its ability to place a structure that turn, as that happens at the end of the turn, after the tactical action has been resolved.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If Winnu place a mech with Hope&rsquo;s End, can the Winnu mech ability then place a space dock?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; Hope&rsquo;s End resolves at the end of your turn, after the window where the mech&rsquo;s ability triggers.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Placing the structure will occur after the <b>Production</b> step. If the Winnu player places a Space Dock, they cannot produce out of it during the same action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;a dock placed by Winnu&rsquo;s Reclamation comes after the tactical action, too late to produce in it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_xxcha">The Xxcha Kingdom</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A planet is adjacent to both the system it is in, and every system adjacent to that.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is a system adjacent to itself, and are planets in the same system adjacent?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Planets are treated as adjacent to other planets in their system and in neighboring systems&rdquo; planet adjacency covers its own and neighboring systems.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Xxcha player cannot gain control of a space station with <sc>Peace Accords</sc>, as a space station is not a planet.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When do I gain and lose control of a space station?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A space station isn&rsquo;t a planet&rdquo; From the space-station control entry; Peace Accords not named.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Xxcha player may use <sc>Peace Accords</sc> to gain control of a planet controlled by another player, if that planet contains no units.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Xxcha&rsquo;s Peace Accords take a planet another player controls if no units are on it?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; the condition refers to units on the planet itself, so an enemy-controlled planet with no units on it qualifies.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><sc>Quash</sc>, <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Xxcha_Kingdom#Faction_Promissory_Note:~:text=Political%20Favor"><i>Political Favor</i></a>, the <i>Political Secret</i> promissory note, and the <i>Veto</i> action card are all played in the same timing window, before the rider timing window.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do &lsquo;when an agenda is revealed&rsquo; effects like Veto and Quash come before Riders?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; &lsquo;when&rsquo; effects (Quash, Veto, Political Favor, Political Secret) resolve first, then &lsquo;after&rsquo; effects (Riders &hellip;).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><sc>Quash</sc> may be used on the replacement agenda.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Xxcha Quash more than once, including the replacement agenda?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; Quash can be used on any newly revealed agenda as long as Xxcha pays a strategy token each time.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><sc>Quash</sc> may be used on a law elected by <i>Miscount Disclosed</i> when that law is treated as revealed for the revote.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the revote from Miscount Disclosed work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Then vote on that law as if it had just been revealed, so Veto, Quash and a fresh round of riders can be played on it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Xxcha_Kingdom#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Quash"><sc>Quash</sc></a>, <i>Political Favor</i>, the <i>Political Secret</i> promissory note, and the <i>Veto</i> action card are all played in the same timing window, before the rider timing window.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;&rsquo;when&rsquo; effects (Quash, Veto, Political Favor, Political Secret) resolve first.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Xxcha player has no command tokens in their strategy pool, <i>Political Favor</i> cannot be played.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can L1Z1X Cybernetic Enhancements or Xxcha Political Favor be used if their owner has no command token in the strategy pool?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Instinct Training</i> may cancel a <i>Sabotage</i> action card.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Xxcha&rsquo;s Instinct Training cancel any action card, even outside their own combats?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it cancels any other player&rsquo;s action card, Sabotage included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player has a second copy of an action card, they may play it if the first was canceled by <i>Instinct Training</i>.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my action card is cancelled by Sabotage, can I play another copy &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a cancelled card is treated as never having been played.&rdquo; Same principle.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an action card is canceled by <i>Instinct Training</i>, no costs are paid.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;a cancelled card is treated as never having been played.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any targeted planets, system, units, players, cards, outcomes, and/or other such objects.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly is Sabotage played&rdquo;: &ldquo;After the player announces the card&rsquo;s choices (target planet, ship or player, rider prediction, retreat destination and so on).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the action card that was canceled by <i>Instinct Training</i> was to be used to perform a component action, the active player must perform a different action, or pass.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my component action card is cancelled by Sabotage &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a cancelled card counts as not played, so you still take an action (or pass, if allowed).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the component action was to be the second action the active player was to perform on their turn, through an effect such as the <i>Fleet Logistics</i> technology, the active player may end their turn without performing a second action and without passing.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;If it was the optional extra action from Fleet Logistics, you can just end your turn.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Once the Xxcha player resolves the ability of <i>Nullification Field</i>, the active player cannot use any &ldquo;after you activate a system&rdquo; abilities.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When must Xxcha use Nullification Field, and what can still happen after it ends the turn?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Once the turn ends, nothing more happens in it: no further after-activation abilities.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other players, including the Xxcha player, cannot use any &ldquo;after another player activates a system that&hellip;&rdquo; abilities.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When must Xxcha use Nullification Field, and what can still happen after it ends the turn?&rdquo;: &ldquo;no further after-activation abilities.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> substep of a tactical action, the active player resolves the <sc>Space Cannon</sc> abilities of their units first. If the Xxcha player is not the active player, and the <i>Loncara Ssodu</i> is destroyed by another player&rsquo;s space cannon roll, it will not resolve its own <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When several players fire Space Cannon Offense, are they resolved together?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a unit destroyed earlier, such as an Xxcha flagship, never gets to shoot.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A planet is adjacent to both the system it is in, and every system adjacent to that.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is a system adjacent to itself, and are planets in the same system adjacent?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Planets are treated as adjacent to other planets in their system and in neighboring systems&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A planet must be exhausted to be targeted by Ggrocuto Rinn.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Xxcha agent target a ready planet just to remove an infantry?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; you can&rsquo;t ready a planet that is already ready, so the target must be exhausted for any of it to work.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ggrocuto Rinn may ready a planet controlled by another player. If that planet is in a system adjacent to a planet the Xxcha player controls, the infantry removed may belong to another player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Xxcha agent ready another player&rsquo;s planet and remove their infantry?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it can ready any planet, including another player&rsquo;s, and if that planet is adjacent to a planet you control, the infantry you remove can be another player&rsquo;s too.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A planet with zero influence may be used to cast one additional vote.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does the Xxcha commander allow, and does it beat Political Secret or a rider?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Each planet you exhaust to vote gives 1 extra vote, so even a 0-influence planet gives 1.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Xxcha player may play rider action cards and vote on the same agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does the Xxcha commander allow, and does it beat Political Secret or a rider?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Xxcha can vote despite a rider or Political Secret.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Xxcha player&rsquo;s <i>Political Secret</i> promissory note will not prevent them from voting.<sup>2,14</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does the Xxcha commander allow, and does it beat Political Secret or a rider?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Xxcha can vote despite a rider or Political Secret.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player has the Xxcha player&rsquo;s <i>Alliance</i> promissory note, the Nekro player may vote.<sup>2,14</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does the Xxcha commander allow, and does it beat Political Secret or a rider?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Through the Xxcha alliance even the Nekro can vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Xxcha player draws, reveals and resolves the <i>Public Execution</i> agenda, the elected player may still vote in the agenda phase following the current action phase.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After the Xxcha hero resolves agendas in the action phase &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A player elected for Public Execution can still vote in the next agenda phase.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After the Xxcha player chooses and reveals an agenda, they may play any rider action cards on the revealed agenda.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Xxcha hero resolve agendas, and can Xxcha play riders on them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the agendas are revealed, so Xxcha can play riders such as Imperial Rider on them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other players may still perform one transaction total with the Xxcha player during this turn, if they are neighbors.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After the Xxcha hero resolves agendas in the action phase &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Transactions during the hero&rsquo;s action still need neighbors, since it isn&rsquo;t the agenda phase.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a third law is put into play, the <i>Dictate Policy</i> objective cannot be scored until the agenda phase.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After the Xxcha hero resolves agendas in the action phase, do exhausted planets stay exhausted, and does Public Execution carry over?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Agenda-phase secret objectives such as Drive the Debate can&rsquo;t be scored from it, since it isn&rsquo;t the agenda phase.&rdquo; Dictate Policy is an agenda-phase secret objective.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Drive the Debate</i> objective cannot be scored while resolving <i>Political Data Nexus</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After the Xxcha hero resolves agendas in the action phase &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Agenda-phase secret objectives such as Drive the Debate can&rsquo;t be scored from it, since it isn&rsquo;t the agenda phase.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other players cannot play action cards, including riders, while the Xxcha player resolves <i>Political Data Nexus</i>.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Xxcha hero resolve agendas, and can Xxcha play riders on them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No other player may use abilities during it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The combined value may be spent as either influence or resources, not both.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a planet&rsquo;s resources and influence both be spent, or spent twice?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; exhausting a planet spends either its resources or its influence, never both.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Political Data Nexus &Omega;</i> does not change the value used by abilities that reference a planet&rsquo;s resource value; the printed resource value of the planet is used.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Xxcha hero&rsquo;s ability to combine a planet&rsquo;s resources and influence work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It doesn&rsquo;t change printed values, so it doesn&rsquo;t raise a space dock&rsquo;s Production.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A space dock will not add the influence value of the planet it is on to its <sc>Production</sc> value.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Xxcha hero&rsquo;s ability to combine &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it doesn&rsquo;t raise a space dock&rsquo;s Production.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> substep of a tactical action, the active player resolves the <sc>Space Cannon</sc> abilities of their units first. If the Xxcha player is not the active player, and their ships are removed by another player&rsquo;s space cannon roll, they must immediately remove units to meet their capacity limit. They will not resolve the <sc>Space Cannon</sc> abilities of any of their removed mechs.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When several players fire Space Cannon Offense &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;one player at a time &hellip; Each player&rsquo;s roll, hit assignment &hellip; finish before the next fires.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>An Indomitus being transported in the space area of a system may still use its <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can mechs carried in the space area use Space Cannon?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; nothing limits Space Cannon to units on planets, so carried mechs, such as Xxcha mechs, can fire in the active system under the normal targeting rules.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A source of both influence and resources, such as a planet, may be spent for one or the other, but not both.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a planet&rsquo;s resources and influence both be spent, or spent twice?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; exhausting a planet spends either its resources or its influence, never both.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>For example, Archon Tau will provide one resource or one influence, not two.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a planet&rsquo;s resources and influence both be spent, or spent twice?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; exhausting a planet spends either its resources or its influence, never both.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>For example, if the Xxcha player wishes to score the <i>Amass Wealth</i> objective, each planet they exhaust will contribute to either the resource requirement or the influence requirement, but cannot partially contribute to both.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a planet&rsquo;s resources and influence both be spent, or spent twice?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; exhausting a planet spends either its resources or its influence, never both.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_yin">The Yin Brotherhood</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li><sc>Indoctrination</sc> is limited to once per ground combat.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Yin&rsquo;s Devotion work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Indoctrination works once per ground combat, at its start.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an invasion involves ground combats on multiple planets, <sc>Indoctrination</sc> may be used at the start of each of them.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;once per ground combat.&rdquo; Each planet&rsquo;s ground combat is a separate combat.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the last of a player&rsquo;s ground forces in a ground combat are removed by <sc>Indoctrination</sc>, that player loses the combat immediately.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If Yin&rsquo;s Indoctrination replaces the only defending infantry, have the Yin won the ground combat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;if it removes the last defender the Yin win.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The text of <sc>Devotion</sc> is changed to: &ldquo;After each space battle round, you may destroy 1 of your cruisers or destroyers in the active system to produce 1 hit and assign it to 1 of your opponent&rsquo;s ships in that system.&rdquo;<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Yin&rsquo;s Devotion work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the errata adds &lsquo;in the active system&rsquo;.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When resolving <sc>Devotion</sc>, the Yin player chooses which ship is assigned the hit.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When Dimensional Splicer or Yin&rsquo;s Devotion hits my ship, who picks the target &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Creuss or Yin player chooses which of your ships takes the hit.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The targeted ship may cancel the hit with its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability, if present. Other ships with the <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability cannot cancel the hit.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Only that ship can use its own Sustain Damage to cancel it; you can&rsquo;t move the hit elsewhere.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Shields Holding</i> action card may be used to cancel the hit.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Direct Hit and Shields Holding work on hits from Dimensional Splicer or Yin Devotion?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes to both.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the targeted ship cancels the hit with its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability, the Yin player may play the <i>Direct Hit</i> action card to destroy that ship.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Direct Hit and Shields Holding work on hits from Dimensional Splicer or Yin Devotion?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes to both. Direct Hit was errata&rsquo;d to cover hits produced by your units or abilities&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Yin player is in a combat with the <a href="/F_norr">N&rsquo;orr</a> player, then the two players will alternate when resolving end of combat round abilities, starting with the attacker.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When both players have end-of-round abilities, like Yin&rsquo;s Devotion and Sardakk&rsquo;s Exotrireme II, who goes first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The active player resolves one first, then the players alternate one ability at a time.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Flagship abilities are not faction abilities.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With the Nekro breakthrough, can the flagship copy several other flagships &hellip;?&rdquo;: the flagship&rsquo;s abilities are &ldquo;text abilities,&rdquo; not faction abilities.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The infantry may be placed on a planet that does not contain a unit with <sc>Production</sc>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How many infantry does Yin Spinner give, and does it trigger off Integrated Economy?&rdquo;: &ldquo;on any planet you control in that system&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Yin Spinner</i> places one infantry each time the Yin player&rsquo;s units use <sc>Production</sc>, regardless of how many units are produced.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How many infantry does Yin Spinner give, and does it trigger off Integrated Economy?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The original gives one infantry per use of Production, however many units produced.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Yin Spinner</i> is not triggered by the <i>Integrated Economy</i> technology, as no unit uses its <sc>Production</sc> ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;it doesn&rsquo;t trigger from Integrated Economy, which uses no Production ability.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other effects that produce units without a unit using its <sc>Production</sc> ability will trigger <i>Yin Spinner &Omega;</i>.<sup>2,7,10</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;The Omega version gives 2 infantry, triggers whenever you produce.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Yin player may choose to produce one infantry for one resource. If they do, they may produce one additional infantry.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Yin and Naalu commanders&rsquo; extra infantry and fighters work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you may produce one extra for its cost, outside your Production limit.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>As such, each time the Yin player spends one resource on infantry, they may produce one, two or three infantry.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Yin and Naalu commanders&rsquo; extra infantry and fighters work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you may produce one extra for its cost, outside your Production limit, so 1 resource buys 3 infantry (Yin) or 3 fighters (Naalu), and it works even when producing just one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A total of three infantry may be placed. These may all be placed on one planet, or placed one each on three planets, or split two/one between two planets.<sup>2,12</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Yin hero commit 3 infantry per planet or 3 in total?&rdquo;: &ldquo;3 in total, spread across one or more non-home planets.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_yssaril">The Yssaril Tribes</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li><sc>Stall Tactics</sc> is a faction ability, not an action card, and so cannot be canceled by the <i>Sabotage</i> action card.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What is Yssaril&rsquo;s Stall Tactics for, and can it be Sabotaged?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It is a faction ability, not an action card, so Sabotage cannot cancel it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><sc>Scheming</sc> applies whenever the Yssaril player draws action cards.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Yssaril&rsquo;s Scheming trigger on cards taken from other players &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Scheming only triggers when you draw action cards from the deck, not when you take or receive them from a player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Effects that give the Yssaril player action cards without drawing them, such as <i>The Codex</i> relic or the <i>Reverse Engineer</i> action card, will not trigger <sc>Scheming</sc>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Yssaril&rsquo;s Scheming trigger on cards taken from other players &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Scheming only triggers when you draw action cards from the deck, not when you take or receive them from a player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of the <i>Executive Sanctions</i> law does not affect the Yssaril player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Public Execution stop the elected player voting in the next agenda phase, and does Yssaril&rsquo;s Crafty save their hand?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Crafty does beat effects that cap hand size, such as Executive Sanctions.&rdquo; Yssaril is unaffected by Executive Sanctions hand cap.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><sc>Crafty</sc> does not prevent the Yssaril player from being made to discard action cards by other effects.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Public Execution &hellip; and does Yssaril&rsquo;s Crafty save their hand?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The discard comes from being elected, not from the hand limit, so Yssaril must discard too.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Yssaril&rsquo;s Scheming trigger on cards taken from other players &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Scheming only triggers when you draw action cards from the deck, not when you take or receive them from a player.&rdquo; &ldquo;Ignoring the hand limit doesn&rsquo;t stop effects that make you discard.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When resolving <i>Mageon Implants</i>, the Yssaril player is not drawing a card. As such, <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Yssaril_Tribes#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Scheming"><sc>Scheming</sc></a> will not trigger.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Yssaril&rsquo;s Scheming trigger on cards taken from other players &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Scheming only triggers when you draw action cards from the deck, not when you take or receive them from a player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Yssaril player may claim orally what action cards they saw.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After looking at another player&rsquo;s action cards (for example with Mageon Implants), can I tell other players what I saw?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; nothing in the rules stops you from sharing or trading that information.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <a href="/F_argent">Argent</a> player owns the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Argent_Flight#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Aerie%20Hololattice"><i>Aerie Hololattice</i></a> technology, the <i>Y&rsquo;sia Y&rsquo;ssrila</i> cannot move through systems that contain the Argent player&rsquo;s structures.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Argent&rsquo;s Aerie Hololattice stop ships with Light/Wave Deflector moving through?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it says other players can&rsquo;t move through systems with Argent structures, and &lsquo;cannot&rsquo; is absolute; Light/Wave Deflector only covers systems with enemy ships.&rdquo; The same applies to the Y&rsquo;sia Y&rsquo;ssrila, which also only ignores systems with other players&rsquo; ships.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Taking action cards from a player using <i>Guild of Spies</i> will not trigger the Yssaril player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Yssaril_Tribes#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Scheming"><sc>Scheming</sc></a> ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Yssaril&rsquo;s Scheming trigger on cards taken from other players &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Scheming only triggers when you draw action cards from the deck, not when you take or receive them from a player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/C_action_cards">Action Cards</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li><i>Ancient Burial Sites</i> is played before the first agenda is revealed.<sup>*</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When must Rider action cards be played&rdquo;: &ldquo;Riders go after the agenda is revealed and before the first vote is cast.&rdquo; The card&rsquo;s own timing (&ldquo;At the start of the agenda phase&rdquo;) puts it earlier still.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The chosen player may still play rider cards or use other abilities.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a player hit by Assassinate Representative still play riders?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it only stops that player voting; unlike Political Secret, it doesn&rsquo;t block action cards.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The text of <i>Bribery</i> is changed to: &ldquo;After the speaker votes on an agenda: Spend any number of trade goods. For each trade good spent, cast 1 additional vote for the outcome on which you voted.&rdquo;<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who can play Bribery, and when?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Per its errata, Bribery is played after the speaker votes and each trade good spent adds a vote to the outcome you voted for.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Bribery</i> may be played after the speaker has abstained, or would vote if they are unable to.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who can play Bribery, and when?&rdquo;: &ldquo;nor can a speaker who abstained, but others who voted still can.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player must have cast votes for an outcome to play <i>Bribery</i>, and the additional votes must be cast for the same outcome.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who can play Bribery, and when?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only a player who actually voted. &hellip; each trade good spent adds a vote to the outcome you voted for.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <a href="/F_argent">Argent</a> player is speaker, only they can play <i>Bribery</i>, and must do so immediately after they cast votes.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who can play Bribery, and when?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If an Argent speaker votes first with Zeal, Bribery comes right after that vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may choose themself as the player who gains their breakthrough.<sup>*</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are there quick answers for &hellip; Brilliance&rdquo;: &ldquo;Brilliance&rsquo;s chosen player gains their own breakthrough.&rdquo; Does not address choosing oneself.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The chosen player gains their own faction&rsquo;s breakthrough.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are there quick answers for &hellip; Brilliance&rdquo;: &ldquo;Brilliance&rsquo;s chosen player gains their own breakthrough.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any predictions made on the outcome of the agenda resolve based on the newly elected player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Confusing and Confounding Legal Text interact with riders and Drive the Debate?&rdquo;: &ldquo;They act before the outcome resolves, so riders and agenda-phase secrets like Drive the Debate use the final elected player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any predictions made on the outcome of the agenda resolve based on the newly elected player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Confusing and Confounding Legal Text interact with riders and Drive the Debate?&rdquo;: &ldquo;They act before the outcome resolves, so riders and agenda-phase secrets like Drive the Debate use the final elected player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After playing a <i>Construction Rider</i>, a player may play other rider cards.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can one player play several riders on the same agenda, on different outcomes?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; FFG confirmed by email that you can play several riders in the same vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any other riders may be played to predict the same or a different outcome.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Each rider is played on its own, so each can predict a different outcome, letting you cover both For and Against.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of a successful <i>Construction Rider</i> will be resolved after the effect of the agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an agenda outcome and a rider both award points, which resolves first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The agenda&rsquo;s outcome resolves first, then the riders that predicted it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player wishes to place a space dock, but there are none left in their reinforcements, they may remove a space dock from any system that does not contain one of their command tokens and place that instead.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If all my space docks or PDS are on the board, can I place another?&rdquo;: &ldquo;if none are in reinforcements, you may take one from a system without your command token and place it where the effect directs.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player successfully resolves a <i>Construction Rider</i> while they own the <i>Minister of Industry</i> law, they use the <sc>Production</sc> ability of their units in the system where they place their space dock. They may exhaust planets for resources, but those planets cannot be used to cast votes for the remainder of the agenda phase.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What triggers Minister of Industry?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a dock placed by a Construction Rider in the agenda phase does, payable only with planets you haven&rsquo;t exhausted.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Coup D&rsquo;etat</i> cannot be played when a player resolves the primary ability of a strategy card through a component action, such as with the <a href="/F_winnu">Winnu</a> player&rsquo;s hero, <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Winnu#Leaders:~:text=Mathis%20Mathinus">Mathis Mathinus</a>, as that is not a strategic action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Coup d&rsquo;Etat stop a primary ability resolved through the Winnu hero or Overrule?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; those are component actions that resolve a primary, not strategic actions, and Coup d&rsquo;Etat only cancels strategic actions.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any combat roll modifiers will not apply to the dice roll for <i>Courageous to the End</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Morale Boost, faction modifiers and rerolls affect &hellip;&rdquo;: &ldquo;only rolls made against a unit&rsquo;s combat value are combat rolls. &hellip; Sardakk&rsquo;s Courageous to the End are abilities.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Effects that cancel hits, such as <sc>Sustain Damage</sc>, cannot be used to prevent ships from being destroyed.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Sustain Damage save a ship from &hellip; &lsquo;destroy&rsquo; effects?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; Sustain Damage only cancels hits, and these effects destroy units without producing hits.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After playing a <i>Diplomacy Rider</i>, a player may play other rider cards.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; FFG confirmed by email that you can play several riders in the same vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any other riders may be played to predict the same or a different outcome.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Each rider is played on its own, so each can predict a different outcome.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of a successful <i>Diplomacy Rider</i> will be resolved after the effect of the agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an agenda outcome and a rider both award points, which resolves first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The agenda&rsquo;s outcome resolves first, then the riders that predicted it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The target player chooses which promissory note they give.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With Diplomatic Pressure or the Mentak commander, who picks the promissory note handed over?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The target does; the other player can&rsquo;t look at or name a specific note.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot play a second <i>Diplomatic Pressure</i> targeting the same player during the same agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I play two copies of the same action card &hellip; in one timing window?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you cannot play more than one action card with the same name in a single timing window to affect the same units or effect.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may play a second <i>Diplomatic Pressure</i> targeting a different player during the same agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I play two copies of the same action card &hellip; in one timing window?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you cannot play more than one action card with the same name in a single timing window to affect the same units or effect.&rdquo; A different target is a different effect.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may play a second <i>Diplomatic Pressure</i> targeting the same player during a later agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I play two copies of the same action card &hellip; in one timing window?&rdquo;: the limit applies only within &ldquo;a single timing window.&rdquo; A later agenda is a new window.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If multiple ships use their <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability, a <i>Direct Hit</i> may be played for each.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can several Direct Hits or Shields Holdings be played in the same combat round?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Several Direct Hits, yes: each Sustain Damage use is its own trigger, so you can answer each with a Direct Hit on that ship.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A <i>Direct Hit</i> may be used whenever hits produced by a player&rsquo;s units or abilities are canceled by a ship using its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;each Sustain Damage use is its own trigger.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a player uses the <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability of one of their ships, they do not have to decide if another of their ships in the same combat will use its <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability until after their opponent has used or declined to use a <i>Direct Hit</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can several Direct Hits or Shields Holdings be played in the same combat round?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Sustain Damage is used unit by unit, so you can wait to see a Direct Hit before sustaining with more.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A mech cannot be destroyed by <i>Direct Hit</i>, as it is not a ship.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Direct Hit destroy a mech that uses Sustain Damage?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; Direct Hit targets ships, and mechs are ground forces.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Disable</i> has no effect on the <i>Magen Defense Grid &Omega;</i> technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Codex (Omega) Magen Defense Grid still work if the PDS on the planet has been hit by Disable?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; Disable only strips the PDS&rsquo;s Planetary Shield and Space Cannon; it is still a structure, so Magen Defense Grid still produces its hit.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Disable</i> does not affect the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step, and is not played until the start of the <b>Invasion</b> step.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Ceasefire or Disable stop Space Cannon?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Disable is played at invasion and only shuts off PDS on the planets being invaded; Space Cannon Offense has already been fired by then.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player must have cast votes for an outcome to play <i>Distinguished Councilor</i>.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;cards that add to votes you cast, like Distinguished Councilor, need at least one real vote first.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player plays <i>Divert Funding</i>, they return a technology, but gain 3 command tokens instead of researching a technology, due to their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Nekro_Virus#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Propagation"><sc>Propagation</sc></a> faction ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Nekro Virus pay normally to use the Technology secondary?&rdquo;: &ldquo;because they can&rsquo;t research, Propagation gives them 3 command tokens instead.&rdquo; The same applies to the research in Divert Funding.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Emergency Repairs</i> cannot prevent a ship from being destroyed by the <i>Direct Hit</i> action card, as <i>Emergency Repairs</i> is played at the end of the combat round.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Emergency Repairs save a ship from Direct Hit?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; Direct Hit is played as Sustain Damage is used, while hits are being assigned; Emergency Repairs comes at the end of the round, too late.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The selected space dock will use the <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability during the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step of the current tactical action.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When can Experimental Battlestation be used, and what can it shoot?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It fires as part of Space Cannon Offense.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Hits may only be assigned to the active player&rsquo;s ships.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;During Space Cannon Offense, who can shoot at whom?&rdquo;: other players fire &ldquo;only at the active player&rsquo;s ships.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Experimental Battlestation</i> cannot be played if no ships are moved into the active system, even if the active player already has ships in that system.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;not when nothing moves in.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a ship is moved out of the active system and then back in, <i>Experimental Battlestation</i> may be played.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When can Experimental Battlestation be used, and what can it shoot?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only when another player&rsquo;s ships actually move during the movement step of a tactical action.&rdquo; A ship moving back in has moved in.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If all moving ships are lost to a gravity rift, and none arrive in the active system, <i>Experimental Battlestation</i> cannot be played.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Only when another player&rsquo;s ships actually move &hellip; not when nothing moves in.&rdquo; Ships lost in a rift never arrive.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Experimental Battlestation</i> cannot be played if ships are moved outside of the <b>Move Ships</b> step, such as during a retreat, or while resolving the <a href="/F_mahact">Mahact</a> player&rsquo;s <i>Benediction</i> hero ability.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When can Experimental Battlestation be used, and what can it shoot?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only when another player&rsquo;s ships actually move during the movement step of a tactical action: not on retreats.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A second <i>Flank Speed</i> cannot be played to give +2 to the move value of each ship.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I play two copies of the same action card, like Morale Boost or Flank Speed, in one timing window?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; you cannot play more than one action card with the same name in a single timing window to affect the same units or effect.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Canceling <i>Flank Speed</i> will not cancel the system activation.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my component action card is cancelled by Sabotage, have I used my action for the turn?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Anything that happened before you played it stands: if Flank Speed or Upgrade is Sabotaged after you activated a system, the system stays activated.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other players must decide whether to play <i>Sabotage</i>, or otherwise cancel the action card, before the player playing <i>Focused Research</i> announces which technology they are researching.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly is Sabotage played&rdquo;: &ldquo;Focused Research is the exception: its technology is chosen during resolution, after the Sabotage window.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_ul">Ul</a> player cannot use <i>Ghost Squad</i> to move a Hel&ndash;Titan.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Titans move Hel Titan PDS with Ghost Squad?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Probably not &mdash; Hel Titans are structures as well as ground forces, and structures can&rsquo;t move or be transported.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After playing an <i>Imperial Rider</i>, a player may play other rider cards.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; FFG confirmed by email that you can play several riders in the same vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any other riders may be played to predict the same or a different outcome.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Each rider is played on its own, so each can predict a different outcome.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of a successful <i>Imperial Rider</i> will be resolved after the effect of the agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an agenda outcome and a rider both award points, which resolves first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The agenda&rsquo;s outcome resolves first, then the riders that predicted it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player wishes to place a structure, but there are none of that type left in their reinforcements, they may remove a structure of that type from any system that does not contain one of their command tokens and place that instead. That player may make the choice for each structure.<sup>1,2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If all my space docks or PDS are on the board, can I place another?&rdquo;: &ldquo;if none are in reinforcements, you may take one from a system without your command token and place it where the effect directs&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <i>Covert Legislation</i> agenda is revealed, it resolves before <i>Insider Information</i>; the player looks at the agenda after the one drawn by the speaker.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Insider Information interact with Covert Legislation?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Covert Legislation is a &lsquo;when&rsquo; effect that takes the next agenda first, so Insider Information, an &lsquo;after&rsquo; effect, sees the card after that.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Intercept</i> cannot be played to prevent <i>Skilled Retreat</i>.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Skilled Retreat differ from a normal retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It isn&rsquo;t a retreat in rules terms but a move, so Intercept can&rsquo;t cancel it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After playing a <i>Leadership Rider</i>, a player may play other rider cards.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; FFG confirmed by email that you can play several riders in the same vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any other riders may be played to predict the same or a different outcome.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Each rider is played on its own, so each can predict a different outcome.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of a successful <i>Leadership Rider</i> will be resolved after the effect of the agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an agenda outcome and a rider both award points, which resolves first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The agenda&rsquo;s outcome resolves first, then the riders that predicted it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <i>Enforced Travel Ban</i> law is in play, <i>Lost Star Chart</i> will allow a player to move through wormholes during this action.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Unverified</b>: <sup>2</sup> Conflicts: RulesPal, &ldquo;What does Enforced Travel Ban do&rdquo;: &ldquo;It also cancels the adjacency from Lost Star Chart for movement.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Lost Star Chart work, including with Enforced Travel Ban?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It creates its own adjacency, which a relayed FFG answer says still works under Enforced Travel Ban.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the active system contains a beta wormhole, a player that has a unit with the <sc>Deep Space Cannon</sc> ability in an alpha wormhole may produce hits against ships in the active system during the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step of the tactical action, and vice versa.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Space Cannon fire at ships outside the active system&rdquo;: &ldquo;PDS II&rsquo;s Deep Space Cannon and the Xxcha flagship let a unit in an adjacent system fire into the active system.&rdquo; Lost Star Chart makes alpha and beta systems adjacent.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot use <i>Lucky Shot</i> against their own units.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;abilities that destroy units can only be used against other players&rsquo; units and planets.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Effects that cancel hits, such as <sc>Sustain Damage</sc>, cannot be used to prevent ships from being destroyed.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Sustain Damage save a ship from &hellip; &lsquo;destroy&rsquo; effects?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; Sustain Damage only cancels hits, and these effects destroy units without producing hits.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Maneuvering Jets</i> may cancel hits produced during either the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> or the <b>Space Cannon Defense</b> step.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Maneuvering Jets protect infantry from Space Cannon Defense?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it cancels Space Cannon hits on your ground forces as well as your ships.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Maneuvering Jets</i> cannot be used to cancel hits during combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Maneuvering Jets protect infantry from Space Cannon Defense?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it cancels Space Cannon hits on your ground forces as well as your ships.&rdquo; Space Cannon hits only.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot play a second <i>Maneuvering Jets</i> to cancel a second hit produced by the same Space Cannon roll.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I play two copies of the same action card &hellip; in one timing window?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you cannot play more than one action card with the same name in a single timing window to affect the same units or effect.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step, each player makes their Space Cannon roll independent of each other player, in clockwise order. As such, a player may use one <i>Maneuvering Jets</i> after each other player makes a space cannon roll.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: the limit on same-name cards applies to &ldquo;a single timing window &hellip; the same units or effect.&rdquo; Each player&rsquo;s roll is a separate effect.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player&rsquo;s turn is ended by a game effect, they cannot use <i>Master Plan</i> to perform an additional action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When must Xxcha use Nullification Field&rdquo;: &ldquo;Once the turn ends, nothing more happens in it: no further after-activation abilities, no transactions and no extra action from Master Plan.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player&rsquo;s second action would be to use a component action to play an action card, and that card is canceled, they are not required to perform an alternate second action on that turn.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my component action card is cancelled by Sabotage, have I used my action for the turn?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If it was the optional extra action from Fleet Logistics, you can just end your turn.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A second <i>Morale Boost</i> cannot be played during a combat round to give +2 to the combat rolls of a player&rsquo;s units.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I play two copies of the same action card, like Morale Boost or Flank Speed, in one timing window?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; you cannot play more than one action card with the same name in a single timing window to affect the same units or effect.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Morale Boost</i> has no effect on anti&ndash;fighter barrage rolls.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Morale Boost, faction modifiers and rerolls affect Anti-Fighter Barrage &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; only rolls made against a unit&rsquo;s combat value are combat rolls.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Wormholes are not anomalies.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, anomalies: &ldquo;Only tiles with a red border: asteroid fields, nebulae, gravity rifts and supernovas. Empty systems and wormhole systems are not anomalies unless bordered.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may move ships into a supernova. After moving into, those ships may remain there and move out during a later action without harm.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Mirage sit in a supernova, and can I activate a supernova, build there and leave?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A supernova can be activated; it only stops ships moving into or through it, so placing structures, producing, committing ground forces with the Sardakk commander, or entering with Nav Suite all work, and ships can always move out.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The returned ground forces cannot be committed to another planet during the same tactical action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can ground forces that retreated with my ships &hellip; land on a planet in the new system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;landing only happens in the invasion step of a tactical action in the active system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The active player must announce which technology they are gaining before other players decide whether to play a <i>Sabotage</i>, or otherwise cancel the action card.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly is Sabotage played: before or after the card&rsquo;s target is chosen?&rdquo;: &ldquo;After the player announces the card&rsquo;s choices (target planet, ship or player, rider prediction, retreat destination and so on), but before anything changes the game state&rdquo; general rule that card choices are announced before Sabotage; only Focused Research is an exception.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A planet with zero infantry on it may be targeted by <i>Plague</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I play an action card whose effect would amount to nothing, like Plague on a planet with no infantry?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; doing the card&rsquo;s text with a result of zero still counts as resolving it &hellip; Players relaying an official reply confirmed Plague for zero.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After playing a <i>Politics Rider</i>, a player may play other rider cards.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; FFG confirmed by email that you can play several riders in the same vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any other riders may be played to predict the same or a different outcome.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Each rider is played on its own, so each can predict a different outcome.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of a successful <i>Politics Rider</i> will be resolved after the effect of the agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an agenda outcome and a rider both award points, which resolves first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The agenda&rsquo;s outcome resolves first, then the riders that predicted it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The player who must choose a different strategy card does not gain any trade goods that are on the strategy card they originally chose. Trade goods on a strategy card are gained by the player who ends up with that strategy card.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Whoever ends up choosing that card.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Public Disgrace</i> may only be played during the regular choosing of a strategy card during the strategy phase. It cannot be played when other effects cause a player to take a strategy card, such as the <i>Imperial Arbiter</i> agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Public Disgrace be played against a strategy card swap from Quantum Datahub Node or Imperial Arbiter?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; Public Disgrace triggers when a player chooses a strategy card, and those swaps at the end of the strategy phase aren&rsquo;t choices.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The chosen ship cannot be moved out of a system that contains the player&rsquo;s command token. It may transport fighters and ground forces.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How far can the ship moved by Rescue come from, and can it carry units?&rdquo;: &ldquo;not from a system with your command token &hellip; It can transport fighters and ground forces.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Reverse Engineer</i> may be played if the action card is discarded after being played or canceled, if it is discarded due to hand size limits, or if it is discarded for another game effect.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Reverse Engineer take an action card another player just played or had Sabotaged &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It takes a component action card another player discards for any reason, so one they just played or one cancelled by Sabotage.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot announce a retreat if there is not at least one eligible system to retreat to.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who can retreat from a space combat &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can only announce one if you have a legal system to retreat to (so Rout needs one too).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot announce a retreat from the space combat initiated by the <a href="/F_mahact">Mahact</a> player&rsquo;s <i>Benediction</i> hero ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Mahact hero&rsquo;s Benediction work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Neither side may retreat or use abilities that move ships, Skilled Retreat included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player has a second copy of an action card, they may play it if the first was canceled by <i>Sabotage</i>.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my action card is cancelled by Sabotage, can I play another copy &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; a cancelled card is treated as never having been played, so the same-name restriction doesn&rsquo;t stop a second copy.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an action card is canceled by <i>Sabotage</i>, no costs are paid.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;a cancelled card is treated as never having been played.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player playing an action card announces the following before <i>Sabotage</i> may be played:<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly is Sabotage played&rdquo;: &ldquo;After the player announces the card&rsquo;s choices (target planet, ship or player, rider prediction, retreat destination and so on).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any targeted planets, system, units, players, cards, outcomes, and/or other such objects.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly is Sabotage played&rdquo;: &ldquo;After the player announces the card&rsquo;s choices (target planet, ship or player, rider prediction, retreat destination and so on).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any dice are rolled and any cards are drawn only after all other players have declined to play <i>Sabotage</i>.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;but before anything changes the game state, such as rolling dice or spending trade goods.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an action card instructs a player to research a technology, that player need not announce what technology they are researching until all other players have declined to play <i>Sabotage</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When exactly is Sabotage played&rdquo;: &ldquo;Focused Research is the exception: its technology is chosen during resolution, after the Sabotage window.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the action card that was canceled by <i>Sabotage</i> was to be used to perform a component action, the active player must perform a different action, or pass.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my component action card is cancelled by Sabotage, have I used my action for the turn?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a cancelled card counts as not played, so you still take an action (or pass, if allowed).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the component action was to be the second action the active player was to perform on their turn, through an effect such as the <i>Fleet Logistics</i> technology, the active player may end their turn without performing a second action and without passing.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;If it was the optional extra action from Fleet Logistics, you can just end your turn.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Scramble Frequency</i> rerolls all of the dice rolled for that ability, including dice rolled against different planets.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Scramble Frequency reroll all the dice of a unit ability?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; all of that ability&rsquo;s dice are rolled together, so the card rerolls all of them, across different values or bombarded planets.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Shields Holding</i> may be used to cancel hits produced by any ability during space combat.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which hits can Shields Holding cancel?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Hits during space combat, including Anti-Fighter Barrage in the first round and ability hits like Dimensional Splicer, Ambush or Devotion.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Shields Holding</i> may be used to cancel hits produced by an anti&ndash;fighter barrage roll.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which hits can Shields Holding cancel?&rdquo;: &ldquo;including Anti-Fighter Barrage in the first round.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During a space combat, each player may play a <i>Shields Holding</i> during the same timing window, as each card cancels a different set of hits.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Each combatant may play one in the same round.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot play a second <i>Shields Holding</i> to cancel a second pair of hits produced by the same dice roll or other source.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which hits can Shields Holding cancel?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Each combatant may play one in the same round.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Shields Holding</i> cannot be used to cancel hits produced by <sc>Space Cannon</sc> abilities.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which hits can Shields Holding cancel?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Not Space Cannon Offense or Defense, which happen outside space combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Shields Holding</i> cannot be used to prevent an effect that directly destroys a unit.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which hits can Shields Holding cancel?&rdquo;: &ldquo;not &lsquo;destroy&rsquo; effects such as Courageous to the End.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Shields Holding</i> cannot be used during ground combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which hits can Shields Holding cancel?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Hits during space combat.&rdquo; Ground combat is not space combat.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The command token is placed from a single chosen player&rsquo;s reinforcements. <i>Signal Jamming</i> cannot be played choosing a player who has no command tokens in their reinforcements.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Signal Jamming take command tokens from more than one player?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; it names a single other player, whose reinforcements supply the one token you place; that player must have one there for the card to be playable.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Using <i>Skilled Retreat</i> is not a retreat. As such, it cannot be canceled with the <i>Intercept</i> action card.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Skilled Retreat differ from a normal retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It isn&rsquo;t a retreat in rules terms but a move, so Intercept can&rsquo;t cancel it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a player uses <i>Skilled Retreat</i>, the combat is a draw. Neither player can resolve abilities that trigger after winning or losing a combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If the defender leaves with Skilled Retreat or Foresight, does the attacker still invade, and has anyone won a combat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;But no combat was won: after a normal retreat the player left in the system wins, whereas Foresight happens before combat and Skilled Retreat leaves no winner, so &lsquo;win a combat&rsquo; objectives and effects don&rsquo;t trigger.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may use <i>Skilled Retreat</i> to move into a system that does not contain their ships, as long as it does not contain another player&rsquo;s ships.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Skilled Retreat differ from a normal retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It can go to any adjacent system without other players&rsquo; ships, including the one the attacker came from.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ground forces may be transported.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Skilled Retreat differ from a normal retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;ships can pick up ground forces from planets.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After moving, capacity and fleet pool limits apply in the destination system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Skilled Retreat differ from a normal retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Capacity and fleet limits apply again at the destination, and you choose which excess units to lose.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may move their ships to a system that already contains one of their command tokens. If they do, no command token is placed.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I retreat or Skilled Retreat into &hellip; systems that contain my command token?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A normal retreat or Skilled Retreat may go into a system that already has your command token; you just don&rsquo;t place a second one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>All of the player&rsquo;s ships in the active system are moved, including fighters, even if there is not enough capacity to transport them.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Skilled Retreat differ from a normal retreat?&rdquo;: &ldquo;fighters move with the fleet without needing capacity. Capacity and fleet limits apply again at the destination.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The active player must still spend a command token to perform the secondary ability, other than for <i>Leadership</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a strategy card&rsquo;s primary ability cost a command token, and does the Leadership secondary?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Secondaries cost a strategy pool token, except Leadership&rsquo;s.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player must have at least one unit with the <sc>Bombardment</sc> ability to play and resolve <i>Tactical Bombardment</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Tactical Bombardment be played in a system with no enemy planets?&rdquo;: &ldquo;it only requires that you have a unit with Bombardment in that system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may select a system that contains zero readied planets controlled by other players.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Tactical Bombardment be played in a system with no enemy planets?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it only requires that you have a unit with Bombardment in that system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After playing a <i>Technology Rider</i>, a player may play other rider cards.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; FFG confirmed by email that you can play several riders in the same vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any other riders may be played to predict the same or a different outcome.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Each rider is played on its own, so each can predict a different outcome.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of a successful <i>Technology Rider</i> will be resolved after the effect of the agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an agenda outcome and a rider both award points, which resolves first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The agenda&rsquo;s outcome resolves first, then the riders that predicted it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may exhaust a planet with a technology specialty to ignore a prerequisite on a technology.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do technology specialties work when researching?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Exhaust a ready planet with a specialty to ignore one prerequisite of that color.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After playing a <i>Trade Rider</i>, a player may play other rider cards.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; FFG confirmed by email that you can play several riders in the same vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any other riders may be played to predict the same or a different outcome.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Each rider is played on its own, so each can predict a different outcome.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of a successful <i>Trade Rider</i> will be resolved after the effect of the agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an agenda outcome and a rider both award points, which resolves first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The agenda&rsquo;s outcome resolves first, then the riders that predicted it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The text of <i>Unstable Planet</i> is changed to: &ldquo;Action: Choose 1 hazardous planet. Exhaust that planet and destroy up to 3 infantry on it.&rdquo;<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;it was errata&rsquo;d to destroy up to 3 infantry.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may select a planet with fewer than three infantry on it (including zero).<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Unstable Planet destroy fewer than 3 infantry if there are only 1 or 2?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it was errata&rsquo;d to destroy up to 3 infantry, so it removes whatever is there, up to 3.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may choose an exhausted hazardous planet.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;It can also target an exhausted hazardous planet, since its effects are joined by &lsquo;and&rsquo;.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The cruiser must be in the system when it is activated for <i>Upgrade</i> to be played. <i>Upgrade</i> cannot be played if the cruiser is moved into the active system later in the turn.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When can the Upgrade action card be played?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Immediately after you activate a system containing your ships &mdash; not later in that tactical action, such as after producing a new cruiser.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Uprising</i> cannot target an exhausted planet.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I play a component action that I can&rsquo;t fully resolve?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t &hellip; use Uprising on an exhausted planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Uprising</i> cannot be played if all planets owned by each other player are exhausted.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I play a component action that I can&rsquo;t fully resolve?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t &hellip; use Uprising on an exhausted planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The text of <i>Veto</i> is changed to: &ldquo;When an agenda is revealed: Discard that agenda and reveal 1 agenda from the top of the deck. Players vote on this agenda instead.&rdquo;<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Veto and Political Favor were errata&rsquo;d to &lsquo;when&rsquo; timing to match Quash.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Veto</i>, the <a href="/F_xxcha">Xxcha</a> <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Xxcha_Kingdom#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Quash"><sc>Quash</sc></a> faction ability, the Xxcha <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Xxcha_Kingdom#Faction_Promissory_Note:~:text=Political%20Favor"><i>Political Favor</i></a> promissory note, and the <i>Political Secret</i> promissory note are all played in the same timing window, before the rider timing window.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do &lsquo;when an agenda is revealed&rsquo; effects like Veto and Quash come before Riders?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; &lsquo;when&rsquo; effects (Quash, Veto, Political Favor, Political Secret) resolve first, then &lsquo;after&rsquo; effects (Riders &hellip;).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After playing a <i>Warfare Rider</i>, a player may play other rider cards.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; FFG confirmed by email that you can play several riders in the same vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Any other riders may be played to predict the same or a different outcome.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Each rider is played on its own, so each can predict a different outcome.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of a successful <i>Warfare Rider</i> will be resolved after the effect of the agenda.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When an agenda outcome and a rider both award points, which resolves first?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The agenda&rsquo;s outcome resolves first, then the riders that predicted it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A ship with the <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability may cancel a hit produced by an anti&ndash;fighter barrage roll if <i>Waylay</i> causes that ship to be eligible to be hit.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Argent Flight&rsquo;s Raid Formation work &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;With Waylay, those hits still hit ships and the opponent assigns them normally, using Sustain Damage on undamaged ships if they like.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/C_agendas">Agendas</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When <i>Anti&ndash;Intellectual Revolution</i> is a law in play, a player that directly gains a technology will be unaffected.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Anti-Intellectual Revolution work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;For: after you research a technology you must destroy one of your non-fighter ships.&rdquo; The trigger is researching.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player is never affected by the <i>Anti&ndash;Intellectual Revolution</i> law.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Anti-Intellectual Revolution work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Nekro are unaffected, since Propagation gains tokens instead of researching.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Nekro player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Nekro_Virus#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Valefar%20Assimilator"><i>Valefar Assimilator</i></a> technologies count for the &ldquo;Against&rdquo; effect, whether their tokens are on another player&rsquo;s faction technology or not.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do factions start with their faction technologies &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Nekro Virus is the exception, since its two Valefar Assimilator technologies are listed as starting technologies.&rdquo; The Nekro own both Valefar Assimilator cards from the start, whether or not a token is on another technology, so each counts as a technology they own.</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do technologies the Nekro copy with Valefar Assimilator count for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a copied technology counts with its colour and type.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player with no non&ndash;fighter ships on the game board may still research technology when <i>Anti&ndash;Intellectual Revolution</i> is a law in play.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Anti-Intellectual Revolution work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It is an after-research effect, not a cost, so a player with no such ships can still research.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the elected player is the <a href="/F_mahact">Mahact</a> player, they choose which command tokens to remove from their fleet pool. Any other players&rsquo; command tokens removed are returned to those players&rsquo; reinforcements.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Armed Forces Standardization affect Mahact holding other players&rsquo; fleet tokens?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Mahact chooses which tokens to remove, and other players&rsquo; tokens removed go back to their owners&rsquo; reinforcements.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The &ldquo;For&rdquo; outcome of Arms Reduction only affects dreadnoughts and cruisers. Afterward, if a player&rsquo;s fighters or ground forces in a space area exceed their capacity, the excess units are removed; they are not destroyed.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Arms Reduction touch ships other than dreadnoughts and cruisers &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; For only cuts dreadnoughts beyond 2 and cruisers beyond 4. Afterwards check capacity; fighters and ground forces that no longer fit are removed, which is not the same as being destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Mechs lose all of their printed abilities other than <sc>Sustain Damage</sc>, including <sc>Bombardment</sc>, <sc>Space Cannon</sc> and <sc>Production</sc>. Their cost and combat values are unchanged, including any additional dice they roll, such as for the <a href="/F_naaz_rokha">Naaz&ndash;Rokha</a> player&rsquo;s mechs.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Under Articles of War, do mechs lose Bombardment, Space Cannon and Production too?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; mechs lose all their printed abilities except Sustain Damage. Their cost and combat stats stay, so they still fight, and a mech that rolls 2 dice still does.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Players choose strategy cards in the normal order, but each player gives the card they choose to another player who does not yet have their full number of strategy cards. A player keeps the card they chose only if no other player can receive it.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does strategy card selection work under Checks and Balances?&rdquo;: &ldquo;on your pick you give the card you choose to a player who doesn&rsquo;t yet have their full number of cards. Only if nobody else can take it, as for the last picker, do you keep it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the &ldquo;For&rdquo; outcome is resolved, a player who has been given a strategy card still chooses a strategy card on their turn and gives it to an eligible player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Under Checks and Balances, does a player who was given a card still pick one on their turn?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; you always pick and give away a card.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the &ldquo;Against&rdquo; outcome is resolved, it applies to every player, regardless of how they voted; each player readies only three of their exhausted planets, and any planets that were not exhausted remain readied.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Checks and Balances&rsquo; Against outcome work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It replaces the readying at the end of the agenda phase for everyone, however they voted: each player readies only 3 exhausted planets, and planets that weren&rsquo;t exhausted stay ready.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The elected objective becomes a public objective and stops being a secret objective.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;While leaked it is a public objective and no longer counts toward its owner&rsquo;s secret objectives.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The player that originally owned the secret objective elected by <i>Classified Document Leaks</i> cannot score it again after it becomes a public objective.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does a secret objective made public by Classified Document Leaks work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Nobody can score it twice, its original owner included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The elected secret objective no longer counts toward the original player&rsquo;s limit of three secret objectives.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;While leaked it is a public objective and no longer counts toward its owner&rsquo;s secret objectives, so they may hold another secret.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If <i>Classified Document Leaks</i> is discarded, the elected secret objective is not returned to the original player, and it is no longer scorable as a public objective. It remains in play, not discarded, and does not count toward the original player&rsquo;s secret objective total.<sup>1</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Unverified</b>: <sup>2</sup> Conflicts: RulesPal says &ldquo;If the law is later discarded, the card returns to its owner as a secret.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Committee Formation</i> is resolved after any riders have been played, if the owner so chooses. Riders will resolve if the predicted outcome matches the player elected by the effect of <i>Committee Formation</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is Committee Formation used, and does it combine with riders?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Just before voting, after the &lsquo;when&rsquo; and &lsquo;after&rsquo; windows, so riders are already down. &hellip; Riders that predicted the chosen player pay out.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The owner of <i>Committee Formation</i> may play riders before they resolve <i>Committee Formation</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;playing a rider and then electing that player works.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player may own and resolve Committee Formation, even though they cannot vote.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is Committee Formation used &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Nekro can hold and use it despite not voting.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>All trade goods are gained together.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Swords to Plowshares work on a planet with one infantry &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;All the trade goods from Swords to Plowshares or Compensated Disarmament arrive in a single step.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If <i>The Crown of Emphidia</i> law is discarded, no player loses a victory point.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;No &mdash; a point from these laws is lost only through the condition printed on the card.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the player that gains <i>The Crown of Emphidia</i> controls no planets in their home system, they cannot lose it.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do I lose The Crown of Emphidia if I lose a planet I control in another player&rsquo;s home system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;only planets in your own home system count for it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The owner of The Crown of Emphidia only loses it if they lose control of a planet in their own home system, not a planet they control in another player&rsquo;s home system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;No &mdash; only planets in your own home system count for it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Only combat rolls may be rerolled with The Crown of Thalnos; anti&ndash;fighter barrage and bombardment rolls, including those from the <a href="/F_lizix">L1Z1X</a> player&rsquo;s Harrow faction ability, cannot. A player may reroll dice for units that would otherwise be destroyed, as hits are not assigned until after rolling; each unit whose rerolled die does not produce a hit is destroyed, even if its other dice produced hits.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which rolls can The Crown of Thalnos reroll?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only combat rolls, not Anti-Fighter Barrage, Bombardment (L1Z1X Harrow included) &hellip; You may reroll units that look doomed, because hits aren&rsquo;t assigned until rolling ends; any unit whose reroll fails to hit is destroyed, even if its other dice hit.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the planet elected by Demilitarized Zone is given to another player by the Colonial Redistribution agenda, no units are placed on it.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a home planet &hellip; be elected for Demilitarized Zone or Colonial Redistribution?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A Demilitarized Zone planet given away by Colonial Redistribution gets no units, since none can be placed there.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of the <i>Enforced Travel Ban</i> law does not affect the <a href="/F_creuss">Creuss</a> player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What exactly does Creuss&rsquo;s Quantum Entanglement connect &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;no game effect, Enforced Travel Ban included, can stop that.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Enforced Travel Ban</i> only prevents movement using the normal adjacency provided by wormholes. Other effects that create adjacency, such as the <i>Lost Star Chart</i> action card, may be used to allow movement between systems containing wormholes.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Unverified</b>: <sup>2</sup> Conflicts: RulesPal, &ldquo;What does Enforced Travel Ban do&rdquo;: &ldquo;It also cancels the adjacency from Lost Star Chart for movement.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Lost Star Chart work, including with Enforced Travel Ban?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It creates its own adjacency, which a relayed FFG answer says still works under Enforced Travel Ban.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When the <i>Enforced Travel Ban</i> law is in play, units cannot move through wormholes, even by effects that move ships outside of the <b>Movement</b> step of a tactical action, such as while retreating.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where can my ships retreat to?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Under Enforced Travel Ban, alpha and beta wormholes give no adjacency for a retreat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When the <i>Enforced Travel Ban</i> law is in play, the <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability may still be used through wormholes.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;the systems stay adjacent for everything else, so Space Cannon still fires through them.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Players may still be neighbors via systems that are adjacent via wormholes.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does Enforced Travel Ban do&rdquo;: &ldquo;the systems stay adjacent for everything else.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The &ldquo;Against&rdquo; effect of <i>Enforced Travel Ban</i> will affect PDS in or adjacent to systems containing an alpha, beta, delta or gamma wormhole.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does Enforced Travel Ban do&rdquo;: &ldquo;Against: every PDS in or next to a system with any wormhole, gamma and delta included, is destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of the <i>Executive Sanctions</i> law does not affect the <a href="/F_yssaril">Yssaril</a> player.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Public Execution stop the elected player voting in the next agenda phase, and does Yssaril&rsquo;s Crafty save their hand?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Crafty does beat effects that cap hand size, such as Executive Sanctions.&rdquo; Yssaril is unaffected by Executive Sanctions hand cap.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When Fleet Regulations becomes a law, any command tokens in excess of four in a player&rsquo;s fleet pool are returned to their reinforcements; they cannot be moved to other pools. Any ships in excess of the new fleet pool limit are removed immediately.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When Fleet Regulations passes, what happens to fleet pool tokens above 4 and ships over the limit?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Excess fleet tokens return to reinforcements; you can&rsquo;t move them to other pools. Any ships over the new limit must be removed immediately.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of the <i>Fleet Regulations</i> law does not prevent the <a href="/F_letnev">Letnev</a> player from using their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Barony_of_Letnev#:~:text=Armada"><sc>Armada</sc></a> faction ability. They may have up to six non&ndash;fighter ships in a system if they have four command tokens in their fleet pool.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When Fleet Regulations passes &hellip;&rdquo;: &ldquo;Abilities that raise fleet limits, like Letnev&rsquo;s, still add on top.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If <i>Leadership</i> is elected, players may still spend influence to gain additional command tokens when resolving its secondary ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Galactic Crisis Pact work when Leadership is elected?&rdquo;: &ldquo;there&rsquo;s no token cost to waive, but spending influence for tokens still applies.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <i>Holy Planet of Ixth</i> law is discarded, no player loses a victory point.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;No &mdash; a point from these laws is lost only through the condition printed on the card.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may use the first technology they research to meet the prerequisites of the second technology they research.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a technology I just researched &hellip; count as a prerequisite?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the first technology from the Technology primary counts for the second.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player with three or fewer units in a system adjacent to the Mecatol Rex system destroys all of their units in that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which units does The Ixthian Artifact destroy?&rdquo;: &ldquo;3 units of each player in every adjacent system (all of them if a player has fewer).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_creuss">Creuss</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Quantum%20Entanglement"><sc>Quantum Entanglement</sc></a> faction ability does not make any additional systems adjacent to the Mecatol Rex system for the purposes of <i>Ixthian Artifact</i>.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which units does The Ixthian Artifact destroy?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Adjacency is the normal kind for everyone: a matching wormhole on Mecatol counts, but Creuss&rsquo;s Quantum Entanglement doesn&rsquo;t extend it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Creuss player&rsquo;s flagship, the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Flagship:~:text=Hil%20Colish"><i>Hil Colish</i></a>, is in the Mecatol Rex system, the Creuss home system and the Creuss Gate system are adjacent to it, as each contains a delta wormhole. Units in those systems will be affected.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which units does The Ixthian Artifact destroy?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a matching wormhole on Mecatol counts&rdquo; The Hil Colish puts a delta wormhole in its system.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If <i>Judicial Abolishment</i> is revealed when only one law is in play, players may only vote for that law or abstain. If no votes are cast, the speaker must decide for that law to be elected.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If Judicial Abolishment comes up with only one law in play, can that law be saved?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; that law is the only eligible outcome. Abstaining isn&rsquo;t an outcome, and if nobody votes the speaker must break the 0-0 tie by choosing it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Minister of Commerce</i> triggers when its owner has their commodities replenished by any game effect.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Minister of Commerce, Industry, Peace and War interact &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Minister of Commerce still pays 1 trade good per neighbor when your commodities were already full, since you still replenished.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The owner of Minister of Commerce gains trade goods from Minister of Commerce when they replenish commodities, even if they already have their maximum number of commodities.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Minister of Commerce still pays 1 trade good per neighbor when your commodities were already full, since you still replenished.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>All trade goods are gained together.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Swords to Plowshares work on a planet with one infantry &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;All the trade goods from Swords to Plowshares or Compensated Disarmament arrive in a single step.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>All units in that system may use their <sc>Production</sc> ability, not just the placed space dock.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Minister of Industry lets you produce in that system right after placing the dock.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The produced units must be paid for.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can Integrated Economy produce?&rdquo;: &ldquo;up to the new planet&rsquo;s resource value in cost, paid normally.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Moving the <a href="/F_saar">Saar</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Clan_of_Saar#Space_Dock:~:text=Floating%20Factory">Floating Factory</a> into a system is not placing a space dock, and does not trigger Minister of Industry.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What triggers Minister of Industry?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A Saar Floating Factory moving into a system doesn&rsquo;t trigger it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Once a player resolves the ability of <i>Minister of Peace</i>, the active player cannot use any &ldquo;after you activate a system&rdquo; abilities.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When is Minister of Peace used &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Once it ends the active player&rsquo;s turn, no other abilities in that window can be resolved, per the developer&rsquo;s later FAQ ruling.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other players, including the <i>Minister of Peace</i> player, cannot use any &ldquo;after another player activates a system that&hellip;&rdquo; abilities.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;no other abilities in that window can be resolved.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The player that owns <i>Minister of Peace</i> does not have to have any of their units in the active system to resolve <i>Minister of Peace</i>, so long as any non&ndash;active player owns at least one unit in the active system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Minister of Commerce, Industry, Peace and War interact &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Minister of Peace&rsquo;s owner needn&rsquo;t have units in the activated system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Performing any action is the trigger for the remainder of the ability of <i>Minister of War</i>.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Minister of War be used after any action?&rdquo;: &ldquo;its owner can discard it after any action &hellip; it doesn&rsquo;t need to follow an action that removed a token.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Resolving Minister of War is not an action. As such, a player with the <i>Fleet Logistics</i> technology may perform three actions in one turn.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Minister of Commerce, Industry, Peace and War interact &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Minister of War isn&rsquo;t an action itself, so with Fleet Logistics you can take three actions in a turn.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The elected law remains in play while it is being voted on.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When Miscount Disclosed forces a revote on a law, does that law stay in effect during the revote?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; the law remains in play until the revote resolves.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If <i>Representative Government</i> is elected by <i>Miscount Disclosed</i>, each player will cast only one vote during the revote.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;the law remains in play until the revote resolves, so Representative Government, for example, still limits everyone to 1 vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Once the law is elected, any riders on <i>Miscount Disclosed</i> will resolve. Following that, the elected law is then treated as being revealed.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the revote from Miscount Disclosed work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;First vote on Miscount Disclosed, with riders predicting only which law is chosen. Then vote on that law as if it had just been revealed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When the elected law is treated as revealed, the <i>Veto</i> action card or the <a href="/F_xxcha">Xxcha</a> <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Xxcha_Kingdom#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Quash"><sc>Quash</sc></a> faction ability may be played. If so, the law will be discarded and replaced.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;vote on that law as if it had just been revealed, so Veto, Quash and a fresh round of riders can be played on it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>After the elected law is treated as revealed, riders may be played to predict the outcome of the elected law.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;so Veto, Quash and a fresh round of riders can be played on it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Planets are not readied between the vote on Miscount Disclosed and the vote on the elected law. The vote on the elected law does not replace the second agenda of the agenda phase, so three agendas may be voted on during that agenda phase.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Planets are not readied in between, and the revote doesn&rsquo;t replace the phase&rsquo;s second agenda, so a phase can have three votes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If <i>Holy Planet of Ixth</i>, <i>Shard of the Throne</i> or <i>The Crown of Emphidia</i> is elected by <i>Miscount Disclosed</i>, the previous owner keeps their victory point, and the newly elected owner gains a victory point, even if they were also the previous owner.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If Shard of the Throne, The Crown of Emphidia or Holy Planet of Ixth is discarded or re-elected &hellip;&rdquo;: &ldquo;If the law is discarded some other way, such as Repeal Law, or re-elected via Miscount Disclosed, the old owner keeps the point; a newly elected owner gains one, and the same player re-elected scores again.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player controls planets in another player&rsquo;s home system, those planets are not exhausted by the &ldquo;For&rdquo; outcome of <i>New Constitution</i>.<sup>2,3,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;A New Constitution only exhausts the planets each player controls in their own home system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Players may still be neighbors via systems that are adjacent via wormholes.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does Enforced Travel Ban do&rdquo;: &ldquo;the systems stay adjacent for everything else.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a player is elected for <i>Political Censure</i>, they may play the <i>Confusing Legal Text</i> action card before it resolves.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do Confusing and Confounding Legal Text interact &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;only the player originally elected can play them. They act before the outcome resolves.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If <i>Political Censure</i> is elected by <i>Miscount Disclosed</i>, the previous owner loses one victory point when they lose the card, and the newly elected player gains one victory point. If the same player is elected again, they lose and then regain the victory point.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If Miscount Disclosed revotes Political Censure, who gains or loses points?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the previous owner loses 1 and the new owner gains 1; the same player re-elected loses and regains it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>While <i>Political Censure</i> is being voted on again, it remains in play, so its current owner still cannot play action cards until the vote has resolved.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When Miscount Disclosed forces a revote on a law, does that law stay in effect during the revote?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; the law remains in play until the revote resolves, so Representative Government, for example, still limits everyone to 1 vote.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Political Censure</i> only prevents the elected player from playing action cards; it does not affect strategy cards. The elected player cannot discard <i>Political Censure</i> themselves.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does Political Censure stop, and how is it removed?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only playing action cards; strategy cards are unaffected. The elected player can&rsquo;t discard it themselves.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other effects that produce units without a unit using its <sc>Production</sc> ability will not cause <i>Prophecy of Ixth</i> to be discarded.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does producing with Sling Relay trigger the Prophecy of Ixth drawback?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; it only applies when you use a unit&rsquo;s Production ability, which Sling Relay doesn&rsquo;t.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_yssaril">Yssaril</a> player&rsquo;s Crafty faction ability does not prevent them from discarding their action cards if they are elected by Public Execution, as the discard is not caused by the hand limit.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Public Execution &hellip;, and does Yssaril&rsquo;s Crafty save their hand?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The discard comes from being elected, not from the hand limit, so Yssaril must discard too.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If <i>Public Execution</i> is resolved during the action phase, such as with the <a href="/F_keleres">Keleres</a> <i>Executive Order</i> technology, the elected player may still vote in the following agenda phase.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After the Xxcha hero resolves agendas in the action phase, do exhausted planets stay exhausted, and does Public Execution carry over?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A player elected for Public Execution can still vote in the next agenda phase.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Public Execution stop the elected player voting in the next agenda phase &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The voting ban only covers that agenda phase.&rdquo; Neither names Executive Order; it also resolves an agenda outside the agenda phase.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player has copied a faction&ndash;specific war sun unit upgrade technology with <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Nekro_Virus#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Valefar%20Assimilator"><i>Valefar Assimilator</i></a>, they own a war sun technology for the purposes of <i>Publicize Weapon Schematics</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can the Nekro copy with Valefar Assimilator?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Faction technology cards another player has actually researched, unit upgrades included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The agenda card, not the planet, is exhausted to ignore the prerequisite.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Research Team agendas work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You exhaust the Research Team card, not the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The elected planet does not gain a technology specialty, and does not count as a planet with a technology specialty for objectives.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How do the Research Team agendas work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;They don&rsquo;t give the planet a technology specialty, so they don&rsquo;t count for objectives about specialty planets.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The agenda card, not the planet, is exhausted to ignore the prerequisite.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;You exhaust the Research Team card, not the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The elected planet does not gain a technology specialty, and does not count as a planet with a technology specialty for objectives.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;They don&rsquo;t give the planet a technology specialty, so they don&rsquo;t count for objectives about specialty planets.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The agenda card, not the planet, is exhausted to ignore the prerequisite.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;You exhaust the Research Team card, not the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The elected planet does not gain a technology specialty, and does not count as a planet with a technology specialty for objectives.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;They don&rsquo;t give the planet a technology specialty, so they don&rsquo;t count for objectives about specialty planets.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The agenda card, not the planet, is exhausted to ignore the prerequisite.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;You exhaust the Research Team card, not the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The elected planet does not gain a technology specialty, and does not count as a planet with a technology specialty for objectives.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;They don&rsquo;t give the planet a technology specialty, so they don&rsquo;t count for objectives about specialty planets.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If multiple players have the most or fewest victory points, they gain victory points in speaker order.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If players are tied for the most or fewest points when Seed of an Empire resolves, who scores?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Every tied player gains the point. Effects aimed at the player with the most or fewest points hit everyone tied; the speaker doesn&rsquo;t break this kind of tie.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When several players are tied for the most or fewest victory points &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;All of them &mdash; every tied player counts.&rdquo; Order is not addressed.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <i>Shard of the Throne</i> law is discarded, no player loses a victory point.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;No &mdash; a point from these laws is lost only through the condition printed on the card.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Shard of the Throne does not change hands if a combat ends in a draw, or if no combat occurs, such as when ground forces land on a planet without opposing ground forces or the Bombardment ability destroys the last defending ground forces.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Shard of the Throne change hands if both sides&rsquo; ground forces are wiped out?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; if neither player has ground forces left, nobody won the combat, so the Shard stays put. It also never moves through combats that don&rsquo;t happen, such as landing unopposed or bombarding the last defenders.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a planet contains only one infantry, that infantry is destroyed and its owner gains one trade good. All trade goods from Swords to Plowshares are gained together, so the <a href="/F_mentak">Mentak</a> player may use their Pillage faction ability on each affected neighbor only once.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Swords to Plowshares work on a planet with one infantry &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Half of one rounded up is one, so the lone infantry is destroyed and you gain 1 trade good. All the trade goods from Swords to Plowshares or Compensated Disarmament arrive in a single step, so Mentak can pillage each affected neighbor only once.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The &ldquo;Against&rdquo; effect of <i>Wormhole Reconstruction</i> will cause each player to place command tokens in each system that contains an alpha, beta, gamma, delta or epsilon wormhole and one or more of that player&rsquo;s ships.<sup>2,5,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do gamma and delta wormholes count for Wormhole Research and Wormhole Reconstruction?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Wormhole Reconstruction&rsquo;s Against outcome hits every wormhole system, delta included.&rdquo; Every wormhole system includes gamma and epsilon.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player with ships in a delta wormhole may research a technology from the &ldquo;For&rdquo; effect. Those ships will not be destroyed.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;ships in any wormhole system, gamma and delta included, qualify &hellip; only ships in alpha or beta systems are then destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a system contains both a delta and gamma wormhole and an alpha or beta wormhole, such as the active wormhole nexus, then ships in that system will be destroyed.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;only ships in alpha or beta systems are then destroyed.&rdquo; A system with an alpha or beta wormhole qualifies whatever else it has.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/C_events">Events</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Players cannot look through the mercenaries deck. Only the faceup top card is known.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can players look through the Mercenaries for Hire deck?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; only the face-up top card is known.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Units that are captured without being destroyed, such as by the <a href="/F_vuilraith">Vuil&rsquo;raith</a> player&rsquo;s hero, do not add commodities.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does the Total War variant&rsquo;s commodity tally work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Units captured without being destroyed, as by the Vuil&rsquo;raith hero, add nothing.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Neutral units have no cost; destroying them does not count toward <i>Total War</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do neutral fighters need capacity, and do neutral units count for Total War?&rdquo;: &ldquo;They have no cost, so destroying them adds nothing for Total War.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/C_exploration_cards">Exploration Cards</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The <a href="/F_xxcha">Xxcha</a> player may use their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Xxcha_Kingdom#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Peace%20Accords"><sc>Peace Accords</sc></a> faction ability to gain control of a planet with <i>Demilitarized Zone</i> attached.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a planet with the Demilitarized Zone attachment be invaded?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Effects that take control without placing units, such as Peace Accords, still can.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Ion Storm token is in a system containing a wormhole of the same type, a player chooses which wormhole they use when moving through a wormhole of that type. The Ion Storm token only flips if it was the chosen wormhole.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does the Ion Storm flip &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;With several wormhole routes, the moving player chooses which, if any, they use, which decides whether the Ion Storm flips.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The player that gains the Mirage planet card gains control of Mirage and places a control token on it. As it was not controlled by another player, they explore it as a cultural planet.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When I find Mirage by exploring, do I control it and explore it too?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; take the planet card, place a control token, and explore it as a cultural planet, since you gained control of a planet nobody controlled.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/C_legendary_planets">Legendary Planets</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The player chooses which discarded action card to take.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are there quick answers for Watchtower, Reflect, Garbozia&rsquo;s legendary ability &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Garbozia&rsquo;s legendary ability lets you choose which discarded action card to take.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The player who gains Thunder&rsquo;s Edge may use its ability immediately, as it is gained readied at the end of their turn.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I use Thunder&rsquo;s Edge&rsquo;s ability as soon as I gain it, and does it give a breakthrough?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Gained at the end of your turn, it arrives ready while you&rsquo;re still in that window, so you can use it at once.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the gained planet was not controlled by another player, it is explored.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When I find Mirage by exploring &hellip;&rdquo;: explored &ldquo;since you gained control of a planet nobody controlled.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/C_objectives">Objectives</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A <i>Valefar Assimilator</i> technology will count if it is currently copying another player&rsquo;s faction technology.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do technologies the Nekro copy with Valefar Assimilator count for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; a copied technology counts with its colour and type, so the Nekro can score faction-technology objectives that way.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The influence, resources and trade goods must be spent during the status phase. Any that were spent during the action phase will have no effect.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;For objectives that require spending resources, influence, tokens or trade goods, does earlier spending count?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; you pay the full amount in one go when you score the objective, as its cost; nothing spent earlier in the round counts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Trade goods may be spent as influence or resources when scoring <i>Amass Wealth</i>.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Trade goods can stand in for resources or influence there, though a &lsquo;spend trade goods&rsquo; objective needs actual trade goods.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The number of units in the system is irrelevant, if there is at least one.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Defy Space and Time need more than one unit in the Wormhole Nexus?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; one unit is enough; the plural is just the card template.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system containing a <a href="/F_vuilraith">Vuil&rsquo;raith</a> <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Vuil&#x27;Raith_Cabal#:~:text=Dimensional%20Tear">Dimensional Tear</a> is an anomaly.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do the Vuil&rsquo;raith roll for ordinary gravity rifts?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Those dock systems are anomalies, which counts for objectives like Making History.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The lost planet may be in any player&rsquo;s home system. It does not have to be a planet in the home system of the player scoring <i>Become a Martyr</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Become a Martyr be scored by losing a planet in another player&rsquo;s home system that I had taken?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it requires losing a planet in a home system, not your own.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_creuss">Creuss</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Quantum%20Entanglement">Quantum Entanglement</a> faction ability only makes alpha and beta wormhole systems adjacent; a player still needs ships in both a system containing an alpha wormhole and a system containing a beta wormhole.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are there common clarifications for specific objectives &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Become the Gatekeeper needs ships in both an alpha and a beta system, as Creuss&rsquo;s Quantum Entanglement only makes them adjacent.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system containing a <a href="/F_vuilraith">Vuil&rsquo;raith</a> <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Vuil&#x27;Raith_Cabal#:~:text=Dimensional%20Tear">Dimensional Tear</a> is an anomaly.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do the Vuil&rsquo;raith roll for ordinary gravity rifts?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Those dock systems are anomalies, which counts for objectives like Making History.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The trade goods must be spent during the status phase. Any trade goods that were spent during the action phase will have no effect.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;For objectives that require spending resources, influence, tokens or trade goods, does earlier spending count?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; you pay the full amount in one go when you score the objective, as its cost; nothing spent earlier in the round counts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The number of units in the system is irrelevant, if there is at least one.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Defy Space and Time need more than one unit in the Wormhole Nexus?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; one unit is enough; the plural is just the card template.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the edge of a system tile is touching a hyperlane tile, that edge does not cause that system tile to be on the edge of the game board.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
