        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Space Cannon, Bombardment or landing on an empty planet count as winning a combat for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; &hellip; bombarding away all defenders &hellip; involves no combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_lizix">L1Z1X</a> player may score <i>Make an Example of Their World</i> by using their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_L1Z1X_Mindnet#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Harrow"><sc>Harrow</sc></a> faction ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are there common clarifications for specific objectives &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;destroying the last ground forces with L1Z1X&rsquo;s Harrow counts for Make an Example, since Harrow is Bombardment.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Scoring <i>Make an Example of Their World &Omega;</i> will not prevent that player from scoring a secret objective in any ground combats during this tactical action, as the <b>Bombardment</b> step occurs outside of combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How and when are action-phase secret objectives scored?&rdquo;: &ldquo;only one per combat or step: one in a space combat and another in the ground combat that follows is fine.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Space Cannon, Bombardment or landing on an empty planet count as winning a combat for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; &hellip; bombarding away all defenders &hellip; involves no combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The number of units in the system is irrelevant, if there is at least one.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Defy Space and Time need more than one unit in the Wormhole Nexus?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; one unit is enough; the plural is just the card template.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system containing a <a href="/F_vuilraith">Vuil&rsquo;raith</a> <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Vuil&#x27;Raith_Cabal#:~:text=Dimensional%20Tear">Dimensional Tear</a> is an anomaly.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do the Vuil&rsquo;raith roll for ordinary gravity rifts?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Those dock systems are anomalies, which counts for objectives like Making History.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The influence must be spent during the status phase. Any influence that was spent during the action phase will have no effect.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;For objectives that require spending resources, influence, tokens or trade goods, does earlier spending count?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; you pay the full amount in one go when you score the objective, as its cost; nothing spent earlier in the round counts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Trade goods may be spent as influence when scoring <i>Manipulate Galactic Law</i>.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Trade goods can stand in for resources or influence there, though a &lsquo;spend trade goods&rsquo; objective needs actual trade goods.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The trade goods must be spent during the status phase. Any trade goods that were spent during the action phase will have no effect.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;For objectives that require spending resources, influence, tokens or trade goods, does earlier spending count?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; you pay the full amount in one go when you score the objective, as its cost; nothing spent earlier in the round counts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_saar">Saar</a> player&rsquo;s Floating Factories are never on a planet.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where do Saar Floating Factories go &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;In the space area, never on a planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The number of units in the system is irrelevant, if there is at least one.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Defy Space and Time need more than one unit in the Wormhole Nexus?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; one unit is enough; the plural is just the card template.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The number of units in the system is irrelevant, if there is at least one.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Defy Space and Time need more than one unit in the Wormhole Nexus?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; one unit is enough; the plural is just the card template.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the edge of a system tile is touching a hyperlane tile, that edge does not cause that system tile to be on the edge of the game board.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With hyperlanes, which systems are on the edge of the board?&rdquo;: &ldquo;A system is on the edge only if one of its sides touches neither another system nor a hyperlane tile.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When using the five&ndash; and seven&ndash;player hyperlane setups, the system tile in the center of the ring of hyperlanes is not on the edge of the game board.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;tiles beside hyperlanes, and a system enclosed by them, aren&rsquo;t edge systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The number of units in the system is irrelevant, if there is at least one.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Defy Space and Time need more than one unit in the Wormhole Nexus?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; one unit is enough; the plural is just the card template.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player has units or controls a planet in a system containing an alpha wormhole, and the <a href="/F_creuss">Creuss</a> player has units or controls a planet in a system containing a beta wormhole, or vice versa, those two players are neighbors.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Who counts as my neighbor?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Players with a unit or controlled planet in the same system or adjacent systems, wormholes included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player owns multiple unit upgrade technologies of the same type, only one of them counts for <i>Revolutionize Warfare</i>.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;when a copied faction upgrade covers a generic one, like Dreadnought II, only one of them counts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player will qualify for <i>Rule Distant Lands</i> if they can select two home systems other than their own, and they control a planet in or adjacent to each of those systems.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are there common clarifications for specific objectives &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Rule Distant Lands needs planets near two different players&rsquo; home systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player that currently outright has the most victory points cannot score <i>Spark a Rebellion</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I score an objective for beating &lsquo;the player with the most victory points&rsquo; &hellip; if I&rsquo;m the leader?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If you lead alone, nobody else qualifies.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If multiple players are tied for the most victory points, <i>Spark a Rebellion</i> may be scored by winning a combat against any of them.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;when players are tied for most points, each of them counts as having the most.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Spark a Rebellion</i> may be scored by winning a combat against a player with zero victory points if all players have zero victory points.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;when players are tied for most points, each of them counts as having the most.&rdquo; A 0&ndash;0 tie is a tie for most.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player who wins a combat against the owner of the Shard of the Throne law, who has the most victory points, may both score <i>Spark a Rebellion</i> and gain Shard of the Throne.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If I beat the Shard of the Throne holder, who leads on points, can I score Spark a Rebellion and take the Shard?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes, both.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player coexisting on a planet may score <i>Stake Your Claim</i> using that planet, even if it is the only planet in its system, as the planet is also controlled by another player.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does coexistence work?&rdquo; (general rules; does not mention Stake Your Claim).</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The planets cannot be in any player&rsquo;s home system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do other players&rsquo; home planets count for &lsquo;non-home system&rsquo; objectives like Subdue the Galaxy?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; &lsquo;non-home&rsquo; excludes every home system, including other players&rsquo; that you control.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The influence must be spent during the status phase. Any influence that was spent during the action phase will have no effect.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;For objectives that require spending resources, influence, tokens or trade goods, does earlier spending count?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; you pay the full amount in one go when you score the objective, as its cost; nothing spent earlier in the round counts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Trade goods may be spent as influence when scoring <i>Sway the Council</i>.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Trade goods can stand in for resources or influence there, though a &lsquo;spend trade goods&rsquo; objective needs actual trade goods.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the last ship is destroyed using a <i>Direct Hit</i> action card, <i>Turn Their Fleets to Dust</i> cannot be scored.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Direct Hit&rsquo;s own text destroys the ship, not the Space Cannon.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player still has fighters in a system after assigning all hits produced during the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step, but is forced to remove all of them to meet capacity limits, <i>Turn Their Fleets to Dust</i> cannot be scored.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;fighters lost to the capacity rule weren&rsquo;t destroyed by Space Cannon.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Scoring <i>Turn Their Fleets to Dust</i> will not prevent that player from scoring a secret objective in the <b>Space Combat</b> step during this tactical action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How and when are action-phase secret objectives scored?&rdquo;: &ldquo;only one per combat or step: one in a space combat and another in the ground combat that follows is fine.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Scoring <i>Turn Their Fleets to Dust &Omega;</i> will not prevent that player from scoring a secret objective in the <b>Space Combat</b> step during this tactical action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How and when are action-phase secret objectives scored?&rdquo;: &ldquo;only one per combat or step: one in a space combat and another in the ground combat that follows is fine.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player&rsquo;s flagship is destroyed during combat, but a later effect places it into the same combat, that player cannot score <i>Unveil Flagship</i>.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Unveil Flagship needs your flagship to survive the combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/C_promissory_notes">Promissory Notes</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If the active player receives an <i>Alliance</i>, they cannot then attempt to trade it with another player. It must be placed face up in their play area immediately.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I trade on a promissory note I received from another player &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Notes face up in your play area, like Support for the Throne, can&rsquo;t be traded or handed back voluntarily.&rdquo; Alliance card text: &ldquo;When you receive this card, if you are not the (color) player, you must place it face up in your play area.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an effect other than activating a system during a tactical action places a command token in a system with the other player&rsquo;s units (for example, the primary ability of the <i>Diplomacy</i> strategy card), this will not cause <i>Alliance</i> to be returned.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, on Support for the Throne: &ldquo;Putting a command token in a system by other means is not activating it.&rdquo; Alliance card text: &ldquo;If you activate a system that contains 1 or more of the (color) player&rsquo;s units, return this card to the (color) player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Activating a system containing only a player&rsquo;s structures will still cause their <i>Alliance</i> to be returned.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, on Support for the Throne: &ldquo;Units include their ships, ground forces, space docks and PDS.&rdquo; Alliance card text: &ldquo;If you activate a system that contains 1 or more of the (color) player&rsquo;s units, return this card to the (color) player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The active player may still use the <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability of any units during the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> step of the tactical action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Ceasefire or Disable stop Space Cannon?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Ceasefire only stops ships moving in, so Space Cannon, Bombardment, invasion and production all still happen.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the active player already has units with capacity in the active system, they may transport ground forces on planets in that system into the space area of that system. Those units may then be committed to invade a planet during this tactical action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can ground forces already on one planet invade another planet in the same system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only if a ship with capacity picked them up into the space area during the movement step.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Ceasefire</i> does not prevent a player from producing units in the active system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do Ceasefire or Disable stop Space Cannon?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Space Cannon, Bombardment, invasion and production all still happen.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Ceasefire only prevents movement.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The effect of <i>Political Secret</i> does not stop a player from using their passive faction ability, only the abilities that player may choose to use.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What exactly does Political Secret stop the affected player from doing?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Mandatory and passive abilities still apply.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A <i>Support For The Throne</i> must be played by the receiving player as soon as it is received. It cannot be kept secret.<sup>2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;promissory notes are never mandatory unless the note says to play it immediately or place it face up.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the active player receives a <i>Support For The Throne</i>, they cannot then attempt to trade it with another player. It must be played immediately.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I trade on a promissory note I received from another player &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Notes face up in your play area, like Support for the Throne, can&rsquo;t be traded or handed back voluntarily.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Support For The Throne</i> is returned when the system is activated, even if the active player will perform no hostile acts toward the other player this turn.<sup>2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When must I return another player&rsquo;s Support for the Throne?&rdquo;: &ldquo;As soon as you activate a system containing any of that player&rsquo;s units, whether or not you move in or attack.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Activating a system containing only a player&rsquo;s structures will still cause their <i>Support For The Throne</i> to be returned.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Units include their ships, ground forces, space docks and PDS.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Activating a system that contains only another player&rsquo;s control tokens does not cause their <i>Support For The Throne</i> to be returned, as control tokens are not units.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;not control tokens, so activating a system where they only have control tokens is fine.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an effect other than activating a system during a tactical action places a command token in a system with the other player&rsquo;s units (for example, the primary ability of the <i>Diplomacy</i> strategy card), this will not cause <i>Support For The Throne</i> to be returned.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Putting a command token in a system by other means is not activating it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player who has given away their <i>Support For The Throne</i> may activate systems containing the holder&rsquo;s units without penalty.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Support for the Throne be given away even if it hands someone the winning point, and can the giver attack the holder?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only the holder risks losing it, by activating a system with the giver&rsquo;s units, any system, even by mistake. The giver can attack freely.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If two players trade their <i>Support For The Throne</i> to one another simultaneously, the active player will be the first to gain a victory point.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If two players swap Support for the Throne and both would hit 10, who wins?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The swapped notes resolve one after the other in normal timing order, so the player who gains the point first wins.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During the agenda phase, the first player in speaker order will be the first to gain a victory point.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If two players could both reach 10 points in the same status phase, who wins?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The speaker-order tiebreak only covers truly simultaneous gains.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may give their <i>Support For The Throne</i> to another player even if doing so gives that player the victory point they need to win the game.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Support for the Throne be given away even if it hands someone the winning point, and can the giver attack the holder?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes to both. Trading it is a normal transaction, and a ban is a house rule.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/C_relics">Relics</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player may only reroll combat rolls with <i>The Crown of Thalnos</i>. They cannot reroll anti&ndash;fighter barrage rolls and similar.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Which rolls can The Crown of Thalnos reroll?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only combat rolls, not Anti-Fighter Barrage, Bombardment.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Units destroyed by <i>The Crown of Thalnos</i> are destroyed at the end of the <b>Roll Dice</b> step, before the <b>Assign Hits</b> step.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;hits aren&rsquo;t assigned until rolling ends; any unit whose reroll fails to hit is destroyed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Dominus Orb</i> does not increase the distance units may move; they must still be able to reach the active system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can units leave a system with my command token &hellip; and does Dominus Orb ignore range?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Dominus Orb doesn&rsquo;t extend range; the units still need to reach the active system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The &ldquo;0&rdquo; side of the d10 represents a result of 10.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the 0 on the ten-sided die count as 0 or 10?&rdquo;: &ldquo;10 &mdash; the Living Rules Reference FAQ confirms the 0 face is a 10.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A command token spent from reinforcements using <i>Scepter of Emelpar</i> instead of from the strategy pool counts toward objectives that require spending command tokens, such as <i>Galvanize the People</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can the Scepter of Emelpar help pay spend-token objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; a token spent from reinforcements through the Scepter in place of a strategy token counts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The &ldquo;0&rdquo; side of the d10 represents a result of 10.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the 0 on the ten-sided die count as 0 or 10?&rdquo;: &ldquo;10 &mdash; the Living Rules Reference FAQ confirms the 0 face is a 10.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system that contains a planet destroyed by <i>Stellar Converter</i>, and no other planets, is considered to contain no planets.<sup>2,4,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a system whose planet was destroyed by Stellar Converter count as having no planets for objectives?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Most likely yes &mdash; the destroyed planet can&rsquo;t be controlled or invaded, so players treat the system as planetless.&rdquo; RulesPal hedges (no official ruling) but supports it.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/C_technology">Technology</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The &minus;1 applies to space cannon rolls during both the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> and <b>Space Cannon Defense</b> steps.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does Antimass Deflectors do?&rdquo;: &ldquo;other players&rsquo; Space Cannon rolls get -1 against all your units, ground forces in Space Cannon Defense included, wherever they are&rdquo; -1 applies to all Space Cannon rolls against the player, Defense explicitly included.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player gains then loses the <i>Antimass Deflectors</i> technology, their units may remain in and move out of any asteroid fields they are currently in, but cannot move additional units into or through.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If I lose Antimass Deflectors while my ships sit in an asteroid field, or place units in an anomaly, what happens?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Nothing &mdash; anomaly rules restrict moving into or through them, not being in them or moving out. Ships already there stay and may leave normally&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Exploring the frontier token happens at the end of the tactical action. Notably, this is after the <b>Space Cannon Offense</b> and/or <b>Space Combat</b> steps.<sup>2,8</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When does Dark Energy Tap explore frontier tokens, and is it mandatory?&rdquo;: &ldquo;After the tactical action ends, and only if your ships are still there, so it comes after Space Cannon Offense and production.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Exploring the frontier token is mandatory if the player has a ship in the active system at the end of the tactical action.<sup>2,8</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;It has no &lsquo;may&rsquo;, so it is mandatory.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player moves or places a ship into a system outside of a tactical action, they cannot explore a frontier token in that system.<sup>2,8</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;After the tactical action ends, and only if your ships are still there.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The +1 from <i>Gravity Drive</i> may be combined with the +1 from the <i>Flank Speed</i> action card, applying +2 to the move value of that ship.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do movement effects like Gravity Drive and gravity rifts apply to Saar Floating Factories?&rdquo;: &ldquo;they get Gravity Drive, Flank Speed and rift bonuses.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The produced unit must be paid for.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Saar&rsquo;s Chaos Mapping production work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you still pay for it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Sling Relay</i> cannot be used without producing a unit.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I play a component action that I can&rsquo;t fully resolve?&rdquo;: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t &hellip; use Sling Relay with nowhere to produce.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may use the ability of <i>Fleet Logistics</i> the same turn they gain it.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I use a technology right after researching it, such as Fleet Logistics?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes. Research Fleet Logistics with the Technology primary on your turn, and once that strategic action, secondaries included, is done you may take a second action that turn.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player&rsquo;s turn is ended by a game effect, they cannot use <i>Fleet Logistics</i> to perform an additional action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When must Xxcha use Nullification Field&rdquo;: &ldquo;Once the turn ends, nothing more happens in it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player&rsquo;s second action would be to use a component action to play an action card, and that card is canceled, they are not required to perform an alternate second action on that turn.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my component action card is cancelled by Sabotage, have I used my action for the turn?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If it was the optional extra action from Fleet Logistics, you can just end your turn.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player&rsquo;s first action is a strategic action, the other players resolve the secondary ability of that strategy card before the player performs their second action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What does Fleet Logistics allow?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If the first is Warfare, other players resolve its secondary before your second action.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A ship that uses <i>Light/Wave Deflector</i> to move through a system containing another player&rsquo;s ships may pick up and transport units from that system.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With Light/Wave Deflector, can I pick up my ground forces from a system that has enemy ships?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; moving through such a system is allowed, and ships can pick up units in any system they pass through.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Light/Wave Deflector</i> does not allow a player to produce ships in a system that contains another player&rsquo;s ships.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Light/Wave Deflector let my ships ignore blockades?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It doesn&rsquo;t let you produce ships in a blockaded system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Exhausting planets to gain trade goods can be done at any time during action phase, except when another ability is being resolved. For example, if a player has one of their planets with a technology specialty targeted with the <i>Uprising</i> action card, they cannot use <i>Psychoarchaeology</i> in response to gain a trade good.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;When can Psychoarchaeology be used?&rdquo;: &ldquo;At any point in the action phase, not only on your turn, but never in the middle of resolving another player&rsquo;s ability&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If multiple planets are exhausted, the trade goods are gained one at a time.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Psychoarchaeology give 1 trade good per planet or 1 total?&rdquo;: &ldquo;each use exhausts one planet with a specialty for 1 trade good, and you can repeat it for each such planet&rdquo; each use gives one trade good, so multiple planets give them one at a time.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>X&ndash;89 Bacterial Weapon &Omega;</i> cannot be used unless the player&rsquo;s <sc>Bombardment</sc> destroys at least one infantry; if all hits are assigned to mechs, it has no effect.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does X-89 Bacterial Weapon Omega work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It needs your Bombardment to destroy at least one infantry; if hits go to mechs and no infantry dies, nothing happens.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A war sun must target the same planet with all three of its <sc>bombardment</sc> dice.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can one ship bombard several planets, or split its dice between them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a unit bombards a single planet with all its dice.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a unit may roll additional <sc>bombardment</sc> dice, such as by the <i>Plasma Scoring</i> technology, that additional die must target the same planet.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;a unit bombards a single planet with all its dice.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may roll for <sc>bombardment</sc> against a planet that contains no ground forces.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;You may pick any planet in the system, since bombardment comes before ground forces are committed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Only one additional dice is rolled for each bombardment or space cannon roll, regardless of how many units are using that ability.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How many extra dice does Plasma Scoring add &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;One extra die per use of the ability, not per unit or per planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During an invasion in a system with multiple planets, only one unit will add a dice to its bombardment roll.<sup>1,2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;Bombardment is a single step, so one extra die however many planets you bombard.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A space dock using the <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability due to the <i>Experimental Battlestation</i> action card may roll an additional die from <i>Plasma Scoring</i>.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Is Plasma Scoring&rsquo;s extra die optional, and does it work with Experimental Battlestation?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It is a &lsquo;may&rsquo;, so you can decline it. It adds a die whenever your units use Space Cannon or Bombardment, including a space dock firing through Experimental Battlestation.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a player exhausts <i>AI Development Algorithm</i> to ignore a prerequisite of a unit upgrade technology that they are researching, they may still use <i>AI Development Algorithm</i> itself for meeting a warfare technology (red) prerequisite on that unit upgrade technology in the same manner as any other technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a technology I just researched, or one that is exhausted, count as a prerequisite?&rdquo;: &ldquo;you can exhaust AI Development Algorithm and still count it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>For example, <i>AI Development Algorithm</i> alone may be used to research the Destroyer II unit upgrade.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;you can exhaust AI Development Algorithm and still count it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Magen Defense Grid</i> cannot be used if all of a player&rsquo;s units in combat that had the <sc>Planetary Shield</sc> ability have lost it.<sup>2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a war sun shut off Magen Defense Grid?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only the original version, which needs a unit with Planetary Shield&rdquo; original Magen needs a unit with Planetary Shield, so it fails if all have lost it.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The hit is produced at the start of ground combat, before any dice are rolled. A ground force destroyed by this hit does not roll.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does the Codex (Omega) Magen Defense Grid still work &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;That hit lands at the start of ground combat, before any dice are rolled.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A mech cannot be placed in a space area with <i>Self&ndash;Assembly Routines</i>, and so cannot be placed in a system that does not contain a planet.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Self-Assembly Routines place a mech when Muaat produce in a supernova?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; there is no planet in a supernova to place it on.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player uses <i>Duranium Armor</i> only once during each round of combat, at the end of the <b>Assign Hits</b> substep. Hits assigned outside of this substep will not allow <i>Duranium Armor</i> to repair an additional unit.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Duranium Armor work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Once per combat round, after hits are assigned.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Duranium Armor</i> is used even if a player&rsquo;s opponent produced no hits during a round of combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;even if you had no hits to assign.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player starts a combat with a damaged unit, they use <i>Duranium Armor</i> in the first round of combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;A unit damaged by Space Cannon before combat can be repaired at the end of round one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may use <i>Duranium Armor</i> to repair a unit damaged by <sc>Space Cannon</sc> or <sc>Bombardment</sc> hits that occurred immediately before the combat.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;A unit damaged by Space Cannon before combat can be repaired at the end of round one.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Only units participating in the combat may be chosen to be repaired.<sup>2,4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal: &ldquo;it repairs one of your damaged units in that combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player with <i>Assault Cannon</i> must mandatorily resolve its ability if they meet the requirement.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If both players have start-of-combat abilities like Assault Cannon &hellip;&rdquo;: &ldquo;Assault Cannon is mandatory when it applies.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player loses ships before they can resolve the <i>Assault Cannon</i> ability, they will be unable to resolve it if they no longer have three or more non&ndash;fighter ships.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;each trigger re-checked when its turn comes. So the attacker&rsquo;s Assault Cannon can push the defender below 3 non-fighter ships and deny the defender&rsquo;s own Assault Cannon.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>This will most commonly happen when both players in a combat have <i>Assault Cannon</i>, and the defender has exactly three non&ndash;fighter ships.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;the attacker&rsquo;s Assault Cannon can push the defender below 3 non-fighter ships and deny the defender&rsquo;s own Assault Cannon.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Effects that cancel hits, such as <sc>Sustain Damage</sc>, cannot be used to prevent ships from being destroyed.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can Sustain Damage save a ship from Exotrireme II, Assault Cannon or other &lsquo;destroy&rsquo; effects?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; Sustain Damage only cancels hits, and these effects destroy units without producing hits.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If units are produced without any unit using its <sc>Production</sc> ability, <i>Sarween Tools</i> will not provide a reduction to the cost.<sup>1,2,3</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can Integrated Economy produce?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It isn&rsquo;t a Production ability, so Sarween Tools &hellip; don&rsquo;t apply.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The units must be on the planet before the <b>Movement</b> step of the tactical action.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can I move into a system, fight, invade and then produce there in the same tactical action?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the steps run activation, movement, space combat, invasion, then production.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Graviton Laser System</i> has no effect during the <b>Space Cannon Defense</b> step, as hits can only be assigned to ground forces.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Graviton Laser System do anything during Space Cannon Defense?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; &hellip; the only targets during Space Cannon Defense are the invading ground forces, so there are never non-fighter ships to force hits onto.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Hits produced while <i>Graviton Laser System</i> is in effect may still be canceled by the <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Graviton Laser System affect Space Cannon hits?&rdquo;: &ldquo;They are still ordinary hits, so Sustain Damage can cancel them before assignment.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Graviton Laser System</i> applies to every die rolled for the <sc>Space Cannon</sc> roll, including dice rolled by units using another unit&rsquo;s <sc>Space Cannon</sc>, such as the <a href="/F_ral_nel">Ral Nel</a> player&rsquo;s Linkships.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Graviton Laser System apply to every Space Cannon die, including Ral Nel Linkships &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it applies to the whole Space Cannon roll, borrowed dice included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The produced units must be paid for.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can Integrated Economy produce?&rdquo;: &ldquo;up to the new planet&rsquo;s resource value in cost, paid normally.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>There is no limit on the number of units produced, so long as the combined cost does not exceed the planet&rsquo;s resource value.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can Integrated Economy produce?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Any units, ships or ground forces, up to the new planet&rsquo;s resource value in cost.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player explores a newly controlled planet before resolving <i>Integrated Economy</i>, so any attachment gained from that exploration affects the planet&rsquo;s resource value.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does exploring a new planet happen before Integrated Economy?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; exploring triggers when you take control, Integrated Economy after, so you explore first. Attachments can stack, so a planet may hold several.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may produce ships with <i>Integrated Economy</i>, unless another player has ships in the space area of that planet&rsquo;s system.<sup>2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can Integrated Economy produce?&rdquo;: &ldquo;ships appear in that system, unless enemy ships are there.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>No unit is using its <sc>Production</sc> ability. The <i>Sarween Tools</i> technology and similar effects will not apply.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How does Sarween Tools work?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It needs a unit&rsquo;s Production ability, so it doesn&rsquo;t apply to effects that just let you produce.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What can Integrated Economy produce?&rdquo;: &ldquo;It isn&rsquo;t a Production ability, so Sarween Tools &hellip; don&rsquo;t apply.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/C_units">Units</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The <a href="/F_muaat">Muaat</a> player may do so without owning their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Embers_of_Muaat#War_Sun:~:text=Prototype%20War%20Sun">Prototype War Sun</a> II faction unit upgrade technology.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do faction units like the Muaat Prototype War Sun count as their base unit type?&rdquo;: &ldquo;the Prototype War Sun is a war sun.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a war sun&rsquo;s ability results in no units on a planet having the <sc>Planetary Shield</sc> ability, then the ability of the <i>Magen Defense Grid</i> technology cannot be used during ground combat on that planet.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a war sun shut off Magen Defense Grid?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Only the original version, which needs a unit with Planetary Shield: a war sun makes other players&rsquo; units lose Planetary Shield.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A war sun has no effect on the <span class="removed"><i>Magen Defense Grid &Omega;</i></span> or <i>Magen Defense Grid &Omega;&Omega;</i> technologies.<sup>2,15</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a war sun shut off Magen Defense Grid?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The Codex Omega version only needs a structure and isn&rsquo;t affected by war suns.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player&rsquo;s flagship is destroyed, it is returned to their reinforcements and may be produced again.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can a destroyed flagship be rebuilt?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; it goes back to reinforcements and can be produced again.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Fighters and ground forces do not count toward capacity during combat. When combat ends however, units will need to be removed to meet capacity requirements.<sup>1,2,3,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;If my carrier is destroyed in combat &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;capacity isn&rsquo;t checked during combat, so they stay while it lasts.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A ship cannot move through a system that contains fighters that are controlled by another player during the <b>Movement</b> step of a tactical action.<sup>1,2,3,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What blocks my ships from moving through a system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Any other player&rsquo;s ships in it, fighters included.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Some effects allow ships to move through such systems, such as the <i>Light/Wave Deflector</i> technology, the <i>In The Silence Of Space</i> action card and the <a href="/F_firmament">Firmament</a> player&rsquo;s agent, Myru Vos.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;With Light/Wave Deflector, can I pick up my ground forces from a system that has enemy ships?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes &mdash; moving through such a system is allowed, and ships can pick up units in any system they pass through.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Fighter II units are still fighters for all other game effects.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Are Fighter II units still fighters for other effects?&rdquo;: &ldquo;they remain fighters for every other effect.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player controls no planets in their home system, at the start of their turn, any Infantry II that have been placed on that player&rsquo;s Infantry II unit upgrade technology card are returned to that player&rsquo;s reinforcements.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;What happens to infantry on the Infantry II card if I don&rsquo;t control my home system?&rdquo;: &ldquo;If you control none there, they are lost (returned to reinforcements), per a relayed developer email.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>It does not apply if the infantry is removed from the board without being destroyed.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;After ships stop moving, are fighters and ground forces still tied to the ship that carried them?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Units removed for lack of capacity are removed, not destroyed, so Infantry II doesn&rsquo;t trigger.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A space dock does not have a capacity value. It allows up to three fighters in its system to not count against capacity, but it cannot hold ground forces in the space area.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a space dock have capacity?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; a normal space dock lets up to 3 fighters in its system ignore capacity &hellip; It holds no ground forces.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player has two space docks in a system, up to six fighters in that system do not count against capacity.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does a space dock have capacity?&rdquo;: &ldquo;two docks in one system cover 6 fighters.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A space dock&rsquo;s <sc>Production</sc> value is not affected by whether its planet is exhausted.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;How many units can a space dock produce?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The planet being exhausted doesn&rsquo;t matter.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
